OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026", "title": "Endpoint pointwise convergence for the Schrodinger equation in higher dimensions", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "080", "family_title": "The exact Sobolev endpoint for Schrödinger convergence", "abstract_html": "We resolve the Sobolev endpoint of Carleson's pointwise convergence problem in every dimension <i>n</i> ≥ 3. For initial data in \\(H^{n/(2(n+1))}(\\mathbb R^n)\\), the free Schrödinger evolution converges almost everywhere to the initial data as \\(t\\downarrow0\\). The evolution is defined by removing Gaussian regularization on one full-measure spatial set, uniformly over the interval \\(0\\lt t\\lt 1\\).", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Endpoint convergence", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(n\\geq 3\\) be a fixed integer and put \\( s_n=\\frac{n}{2(n+1)}. \\) Use the Fourier transform \\( \\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-ix\\cdot\\xi}f(x)\\,dx \\) and the inhomogeneous Sobolev norm …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(n\\geq 3\\) be a fixed integer and put\n\\[\ns_n=\\frac{n}{2(n+1)}.\n\\]\nUse the Fourier transform\n\\[\n\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-ix\\cdot\\xi}f(x)\\,dx\n\\]\nand the inhomogeneous Sobolev norm\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_{H^s}^2=(2\\pi)^{-n}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}(1+|\\xi|^2)^s|\\widehat f(\\xi)|^2\\,d\\xi.\n\\]\nFor \\(a&gt;0\\), define\n\\[\nU_af(x,t)=(2\\pi)^{-n}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\ne^{ix\\cdot\\xi-it|\\xi|^2-a|\\xi|^2}\\widehat f(\\xi)\\,d\\xi.\n\\]\nThis integral is absolutely convergent for \\(f\\in L^2\\). At a Lebesgue point of \\(f\\), write\n\\[\nf^*(x)=\\lim_{r\\downarrow0}\\frac1{|B(x,r)|}\n\\int_{B(x,r)}f(y)\\,dy.\n\\]</p><p>For every complex-valued \\(f\\in H^{s_n}(\\mathbb R^n)\\), there are a full-measure set \\(E_f\\) of Lebesgue points of \\(f\\) and a function\n\\[\nv:E_f\\times[0,1)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb C\n\\]\nsuch that \\(v(x,\\cdot)\\) is continuous, \\(v(x,0)=f^*(x)\\), and\n\\[\n\\lim_{a\\downarrow0}\\sup_{0&lt;t&lt;1}|U_af(x,t)-v(x,t)|=0\n\\qquad(x\\in E_f).\n\\]\nConsequently, on this one set,\n\\[\nu_f(x,t)=\\lim_{a\\downarrow0}U_af(x,t)\n\\]\nexists for every real \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\), and\n\\[\n\\lim_{t\\downarrow0}u_f(x,t)=f^*(x).\n\\]\nThe Gaussian limit is taken first. No Fourier-support, radiality, logarithmic strengthening, or additional regularity assumption is imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(s=s_n\\). Choose \\(f_j\\) with \\(\\widehat f_j\\in C_c^\\infty\\) and\n\\[\n\\|f_j-f\\|_{H^s}\\leq2^{-j}.\n\\]\nSuccessive differences have summable Sobolev norms. Proposition 16 gives, on every unit ball,\n\\[\n\\int\\sum_j\\sup_{0\\leq t&lt;1}\n|S(t)(f_{j+1}-f_j)(x)|\\,dx&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nA countable covering of \\(\\mathbb R^n\\) produces one measurable full-measure set \\(E_0\\) on which \\(S(t)f_j(x)\\) converges uniformly for \\(0\\leq t&lt;1\\). Denote the limit by \\(v(x,t)\\), and set \\(v=0\\) off \\(E_0\\). Each \\(v(x,\\cdot)\\) is continuous on \\([0,1)\\), and \\(v\\) is jointly measurable. Suprema and the defining convergence set can be read from countably many dense times.</p><p>For any fixed real \\(t\\in[0,1)\\), unitarity gives\n\\[\nS(t)f_j\\to S(t)f\\quad\\text{in }L^2,\n\\]\nwhile the construction gives almost-everywhere convergence to \\(v(\\cdot,t)\\). Thus \\(v(\\cdot,t)\\) represents the \\(L^2\\) evolution. Since \\(K_a(x-\\cdot)\\in L^2\\), pairing with that class and using Plancherel yields the everywhere-defined identity\n\\[\nU_af(x,t)=(K_a*v(\\cdot,t))(x).\n\\]\nThe fixed time was arbitrary, so this holds for every \\((x,a,t)\\); no intersection of time-dependent exceptional sets is taken. Convolution is absolutely convergent by Cauchy–Schwarz.</p><p>Define the measurable error envelopes\n\\[\nM_j(x)=\\sup_{0\\leq t&lt;1}|v(x,t)-S(t)f_j(x)|.\n\\]\nThey tend to zero on \\(E_0\\). Uniform convergence there, Fatou, and Proposition 16 imply\n\\[\n\\sup_z\\int_{B(z,1)}M_j(x)\\,dx\n\\leq C\\|f-f_j\\|_{H^s}.\n\\]\nValues off \\(E_0\\) do not affect this estimate. Intersect \\(E_0\\) with the Lebesgue-point sets of the countably many \\(M_j\\). Proposition 17 then gives\n\\[\nK_a*M_j(x)\\to M_j(x)\n\\]\nfor every \\(j\\) at every point of that common set.</p><p>For every \\(y,t\\),\n\\[\n|v(y,t)-S(t)f_j(y)|\\leq M_j(y).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\sup_{0&lt;t&lt;1}|U_af(x,t)-v(x,t)|\n\\leq (K_a*M_j)(x)+M_j(x)+R_j(a),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nR_j(a)=(2\\pi)^{-n}\\int\n|1-e^{-a|\\xi|^2}||\\widehat f_j(\\xi)|\\,d\\xi\\to0.\n\\]\nThis convergence is uniform in \\(x,t\\), since \\(\\widehat f_j\\in L^1\\).</p><p>First let \\(a\\downarrow0\\) at a point of the common set. The limsup is at most \\(2M_j(x)\\). Then let \\(j\\to\\infty\\). This proves uniform Gaussian removal over every real \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\).</p><p>Finally intersect with the Lebesgue points of \\(f\\) and the full-measure set where the fixed-time identity at zero gives \\(v(x,0)=f(x)\\). On the resulting \\(E_f\\),\n\\[\nv(x,0)=f^*(x),\n\\]\nand continuity gives convergence as real \\(t\\downarrow0\\). Together with the Gaussian limit taken first, these are exactly the asserted conclusions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Weighted endpoint multilinear Kakeya", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(d\\geq2\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(d\\geq2\\). For \\(j=1,\\ldots,d\\), let \\(\\mathbb T_j\\) be a family of infinite radius-\\(h\\) cylinders in \\(\\mathbb R^d\\), with unit directions \\(e(T)\\). Assume\n\\[\n|\\det(e(T_1),\\ldots,e(T_d))|\\geq\\vartheta&gt;0\n\\qquad(T_j\\in\\mathbb T_j).\n\\]\nFor nonnegative weights \\(c_j(T)\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\n\\prod_{j=1}^d\n\\left(\\sum_{T\\in\\mathbb T_j}c_j(T)1_T\\right)^{1/(d-1)}\n\\leq\nC_d\\vartheta^{-1/(d-1)}h^d\n\\prod_{j=1}^d\n\\left(\\sum_{T\\in\\mathbb T_j}c_j(T)\\right)^{1/(d-1)}.\n\\]\nFor lattice cubes of side \\(a\\leq h\\), the same right-hand side bounds \\(a^d\\) times the corresponding sum of cube suprema, after a fixed dimensional enlargement of the cylinders. Separate suprema for the factors are allowed.</p>", "proof": "<p>The unweighted unit-radius assertion with arbitrary transverse directions is Guth [16, Theorem 2]; the near-coordinate-axis formulation originates in Bennett–Carbery–Tao [2]. Integral weights follow by repeating cylinders, rational weights by division by a common denominator, and arbitrary nonnegative weights by monotone approximation. Dilation supplies \\(h^d\\).</p><p>Every side-\\(a\\) cube meeting a cylinder lies in its enlargement of radius \\(h+C_da\\lesssim h\\). Integration over the cube proves the lattice assertion, including separate factor suprema. For bounded paraboloid patches, normalizing \\((2v,1)\\) to unit length changes determinants only by fixed factors.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Robust hyperplane projection input", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a set \\(A\\), let \\(\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A)\\) denote its covering number by radius-\\(\\Delta\\) balls.", "statement": "<p>For a set \\(A\\), let \\(\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A)\\) denote its covering number by radius-\\(\\Delta\\) balls.</p><p>Fix \\(d\\geq2\\), \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;d\\), and \\(\\rho&gt;0\\). There exist \\(\\eta_0,\\gamma,\\Delta_0&gt;0\\) such that the following holds whenever\n\\[\n0&lt;\\eta\\leq\\eta_0,\\qquad 0&lt;\\Delta&lt;\\Delta_0.\n\\]\nSuppose \\(A\\) lies in a fixed unit ball and\n\\[\n\\Delta^{-\\alpha+\\eta}\\leq\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A)\n\\leq\\Delta^{-\\alpha-\\eta},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A\\cap B(x,r))\n\\leq\\Delta^{-\\eta}r^\\rho\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A)\n\\qquad(\\Delta\\leq r\\leq1).\n\\]\nLet \\(\\nu\\) be a probability law on unit kernel directions satisfying\n\\[\n\\nu\\{v:|v\\cdot w|&lt;r\\}\\leq\\Delta^{-\\eta}r^\\rho\n\\qquad(|w|=1,\\ \\Delta\\leq r\\leq1).\n\\]\nThen some \\(v\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\nu\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_\\Delta(\\pi_{v^\\perp}A')\n\\geq\\Delta^{-(d-1)\\alpha/d-\\gamma}\n\\]\nfor every \\(A'\\subset A\\) with\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A')\\geq\\Delta^\\eta\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A).\n\\]\nFixed multiplicative constants and bounded affine normalizations are permitted after reducing the tolerances and gain.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply He [21, Theorem 1] with projection dimension \\(m=d-1\\). The competing subspaces have dimension \\(d-m=1\\). If one is spanned by a unit vector \\(w\\), its Schubert angle to \\(v^\\perp\\) is \\(|v\\cdot w|\\); hence the displayed directional hypothesis is exactly the required Schubert-cycle nonconcentration.</p><p>Choose the theorem tolerance larger than \\(\\eta\\), and, if necessary, reduce \\(\\rho\\). The stated lower size bound implies the external theorem's lower bound, while the stated subset threshold is stronger than its threshold. The conclusion is simultaneous for all sufficiently large subsets and excludes only a small set of directions. Shrinking the projection gain and tolerances absorbs the fixed comparison constants.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Arbitrary auxiliary weights; packet setting from original Convention 2.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\(k\\geq1\\), and set \\( q_k=\\frac{2(k+2)}k,\\qquad p_k=\\frac{2(k+1)}k. \\) Frequency labels lie in a fixed bounded subset of \\(\\mathbb R^k\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(k\\geq1\\), and set\n\\[\nq_k=\\frac{2(k+2)}k,\\qquad p_k=\\frac{2(k+1)}k.\n\\]\nFrequency labels lie in a fixed bounded subset of \\(\\mathbb R^k\\). A scale-\\(N\\) packet, \\(N\\geq1\\), has a frequency label \\(v\\) in a mesh-\\(N^{-1}\\) grid and a unit native cell \\(T\\), in coordinates\n\\[\nX_{N,v}(x,t)=\\left(\\frac{x-2vt}{N},\\frac{t}{N^2}\\right).\n\\]\nWrite \\(z_T\\) for its physical center and \\(d_T(z)\\) for native distance to its cell. Fix sufficiently large \\(P'&gt;P\\), and put\n\\[\nw_T(z)=(1+d_T(z))^{-P}.\n\\]\nA band-limited packet of amplitude \\(s_T\\) is\n\\[\nf_T(x,t)=e^{i(x\\cdot v-t|v|^2)}F_T(X_{N,v}(x,t)),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\widehat F_T\\) lies in a fixed box and\n\\[\n|F_T(X)|\\leq s_T(1+\\operatorname{dist}(X,T))^{-P'}.\n\\]\nThe alternative smooth class has compact native support in a fixed enlargement and sufficiently many uniformly bounded native derivatives.</p><p>On each time slab of length \\(N^2\\), assume bounded multiplicity per frequency slot and spatial intercept cell of side \\(N\\), after transporting centers to the slab midpoint. In particular,\n\\[\n\\#\\{T:d_T(z)\\leq a\\}\\leq CN^k(1+a)^{C_k}\\qquad(a\\geq1).\n\\]\nThe packet energy parameter is\n\\[\nE=N^{k+2}\\sum_Ts_T^2.\n\\]\nIt is a decomposition parameter, not a spacetime orthogonality assertion. Estimates are first for finite families and finite cube sets, then pass through absolutely convergent decompositions or arbitrarily accurate truncations.</p><p>Let \\(2&lt;p\\leq q_k\\). For auxiliary weights \\(b_T\\geq0\\), require \\(f_T=0\\) when \\(b_T=0\\), and define\n\\[\nB_b(z)^2=\\sum_Tb_T^2w_T(z).\n\\]\nFor every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\int B_b(z)^{2-p}\\left|\\sum_Tf_T(z)\\right|^p\\,dz\n\\leq\nC_\\varepsilon N^\\varepsilon N^{k+2}\n\\sum_Ts_T^pb_T^{2-p}.\n\\]\nThe integrand is zero where both the field and \\(B_b\\) vanish. Unit-cube suprema are allowed.</p><p>If the labels lie within \\(C/N\\) of a \\(\\kappa\\)-dimensional affine plane, \\(1\\leq\\kappa\\leq k\\), the range extends to \\(2&lt;p\\leq q_\\kappa\\). For \\(\\kappa=0\\), every fixed finite \\(p&gt;2\\) is permitted. Original decay and regularity orders are fixed independently of \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(R=N^2\\) and \\(\\Lambda=R^\\vartheta\\), choosing \\(\\vartheta\\) small after the fixed packet orders. Apply Lemma 3.2 to relative-tail atoms, not directly to the original polynomial-tail packets.</p><p>A reciprocal sampling lattice, with spacing \\(\\gamma\\) in native space and \\(\\gamma/\\Lambda\\) in native time, gives the exact expansion\n\\[\nF_T(X)=\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z^k,\\ m\\in\\mathbb Z}\nc_{T,j,m}K_x(X_x-u_{T,x}-\\gamma j)\nK_t(\\Lambda(X_t-u_{T,t})-\\gamma m),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n|c_{T,j,m}|\n\\leq Cs_T(1+|j|+|m|/\\Lambda)^{-P'}.\n\\]\nThe kernels are fixed Schwartz kernels from smooth Fourier cutoffs. An atom has tangential width \\(O(R^{-1/2})\\), normal thickness \\(O(\\Lambda/R)\\), and norm comparable to\n\\[\n|c_{T,j,m}|N^{(k+2)/p}\\Lambda^{-1/p}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\((j,m)\\), write \\(g_T\\) for the corresponding atom, and let \\(B_{b,j,m}\\) be the square background using weights centered at the shifted atom centers. Partition spacetime into \\(R\\)-time-core test boxes. Retain atom time centers within \\(2R\\) of a core, and use one common slab of radius \\(5R\\); omitted centers have an arbitrary \\(\\Lambda^{-A}\\) gain. Retain only trajectories meeting a padded spatial box; other atoms have arbitrarily small spatial tails. Phase-space counting and three-factor Hölder charge these tails before using Lemma 3.2.</p><p>Each retained atom has relative \\(R^{-B}\\) tail outside a support dilate, say \\(2T\\), and activity is counted on \\(3T\\). Snapping labels to slot centers changes trajectories by \\(O(N)\\), absorbed by this collar. For\n\\[\nJ=1+|j|+|m|/\\Lambda,\n\\]\ncolor the unclipped strips into at most \\(C\\Lambda^{C_d}J^{C_d}\\) bounded-overlap families.</p><p>Set\n\\[\na_T=|c_{T,j,m}|b_T^{2/p-1}.\n\\]\nDiscard only \\(a_T&lt;N^{-C_0}\\max a_T\\). If \\(h_T=|g_T|/|c_{T,j,m}|\\lesssim w_{T,j,m}\\), then three-factor Hölder gives\n\\[\nB_{b,j,m}^{2/p-1}\\sum_{\\mathrm{discarded}}|g_T|\n\\lesssim\n\\left(\\sum_{\\mathrm{discarded}}a_T^ph_T\\right)^{1/p}\n\\left(\\sum_Th_T\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nPolynomial local counting and \\(\\int h_T\\lesssim N^{k+2}/\\Lambda\\) make this negligible for large \\(C_0\\).</p><p>The retained \\(a_T\\) occupy \\(O(\\log N)\\) classes. Sort cubes by \\(B_{b,j,m}\\simeq\\beta_0\\) and atoms by \\(b_T\\simeq\\alpha_0\\). On a counted activity cube,\n\\[\nM_{j,m}\\lesssim\\Lambda^P\\beta_0^2/\\alpha_0^2,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\beta_0^{2-p}M_{j,m}^{p/2-1}\n\\lesssim\\Lambda^{P(p/2-1)}\\alpha_0^{2-p}.\n\\]\nWithin a simultaneous \\(a_T,b_T\\) class the atom norms are comparable, permitting Lemma 3.2. Moderate relative ratios have only \\(O(\\log N)\\) ranges. Small ratios gain from\n\\[\nB_{b,j,m}^{2/p-1}|c_{T,j,m}|\n=\n|c_{T,j,m}|b_T^{2/p-1}\n(b_T/B_{b,j,m})^{1-2/p}.\n\\]\nOutside activity strips, retain the anisotropic envelope\n\\[\n(1+|X_x-u_{T,x}-\\gamma j|)^{-A}\n(1+\\Lambda|X_t-u_{T,t}-\\gamma m/\\Lambda|)^{-A};\n\\]\nits temporal compression preserves the factor \\(\\Lambda^{-1}\\). These estimates give\n\\[\n\\int B_{b,j,m}^{2-p}\\left|\\sum_Tg_T\\right|^p\n\\leq\nC_\\varepsilon N^{\\varepsilon/2}\\Lambda^C J^C\n\\frac{N^{k+2}}{\\Lambda}\n\\sum_T|c_{T,j,m}|^pb_T^{2-p}.\n\\]</p><p>Native-distance comparison gives \\(B_{b,j,m}\\leq CJ^{P/2}B_b\\). Apply Minkowski in the original weighted \\(L^p\\) norm. The \\(m\\)-sum costs \\(\\Lambda^{1-1/p}\\), and the \\(j\\)-sum converges for a fixed sufficiently large original \\(P'\\). Choosing \\(\\vartheta\\) sufficiently small pays all \\(\\Lambda\\), color, and logarithmic losses. Lemma 3.1 supplies unit suprema.</p><p>For the affine \\(\\kappa\\)-plane assertion, rotate into tangential and normal coordinates, remove the constant normal carrier and linear shear, and slice in normal space. Normal frequencies are \\(O(N^{-1})\\). Group sliced packets by\n\\[\nj_T(y)=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{y-y_T}{N}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nNative distances satisfy\n\\[\n1+d_T\\asymp1+d_{T,\\kappa}+|j_T(y)|.\n\\]\nEach fixed group has bounded projected multiplicity. Apply the \\(\\kappa\\)-dimensional estimate with amplitudes decaying in \\(j_T(y)\\); integration in \\(y\\) supplies exactly \\(N^{k-\\kappa}\\), and Minkowski sums the normal groups using the fixed decay reserve.</p><p>If \\(\\kappa=0\\), only boundedly many frequency slots occur. With\n\\[\n\\phi_T=(1+d_T)^{-P'},\\qquad \\sum_T\\phi_T\\leq C,\n\\]\nthree-factor Hölder gives\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_Ts_T\\phi_T\\right)^p\n\\leq\n\\left(\\sum_Ts_T^pb_T^{2-p}\\phi_T\\right)\n\\left(\\sum_Tb_T^2\\phi_T\\right)^{(p-2)/2}\n\\left(\\sum_T\\phi_T\\right)^{p/2}.\n\\]\nMultiply by \\(B_b^{2-p}\\), use \\(\\phi_T\\leq w_T\\), and integrate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Reproduction, tails, and unit suprema", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the band-limited packet setting of Proposition 3:", "statement": "<p>In the band-limited packet setting of Proposition 3:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. A polynomially decaying packet is an absolutely convergent sum of packets with the same fixed enlarged native Fourier support and arbitrarily rapid Schwartz decay about translated native centers. The coefficient at translation \\(j\\in\\mathbb Z^{k+1}\\) is at most\n\\[\nC_As_T(1+|j|)^{-P'},\n\\]\nwhere \\(A\\) is the requested output decay order.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every fixed native derivative order obeys the corresponding envelope bound. For a function \\(F\\) whose Fourier support lies in a fixed bounded set, every \\(r&gt;0\\), and every sufficiently large fixed \\(L\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_{|u-z|\\leq C}|F(u)|^r\n\\leq C_{r,L}\\int |F(y)|^r(1+|y-z|)^{-L}\\,dy.\n\\]</p><p>Consequently, integral packet estimates pass to unit-cube suprema and to separate factor suprema in products. Output Schwartz orders may be chosen after a loss parameter; original regularity and decay orders remain fixed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take a smooth native partition \\(\\sum_j\\psi_j=1\\), supported within fixed distance of \\(j\\). Let \\(K\\) be the Schwartz inverse transform of a smooth compactly supported multiplier equal to one on the original Fourier box. Then\n\\[\nF_T=\\sum_jK*(\\psi_jF_T),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|\\partial^\\beta K*(\\psi_jF_T)(X)|\n\\leq C_{A,\\beta}s_T(1+|j|)^{-P'}(1+|X-j|)^{-A}.\n\\]\nThis gives absolute convergence and all prescribed output derivatives. Reproduction without the partition gives derivative bounds for the original packet.</p><p>For the submean estimate, including \\(r&lt;1\\), use the weighted supremum\n\\[\nM=\\sup_y|F(y)|(1+|y-z|)^{-a}\n\\]\nand a point attaining at least half of it. Reproduction and its derivative version imply\n\\[\n|\\nabla F(y)|\\leq CM(1+|y-z|)^a.\n\\]\nA fixed small ball about that point therefore has a comparable lower bound for \\(|F|\\). Integrating gives the weighted maximal estimate and hence the stated inequality; truncate the weighted supremum if needed.</p><p>Translating a native center by \\(j\\) changes envelope comparisons and phase-space multiplicity by fixed polynomial factors in \\(1+|j|\\). These are summable once the original \\(P'\\) exceeds the fixed comparison powers and \\(k+1\\). The requested output order affects constants, not that original exponent. Apply the submean inequality separately to product factors and use Tonelli.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Buffered refined decoupling", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Put \\(d=k+1\\), \\(R\\geq2\\), and \\(2\\leq p\\leq q_k\\).", "statement": "<p>Put \\(d=k+1\\), \\(R\\geq2\\), and \\(2\\leq p\\leq q_k\\). For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), there is \\(\\vartheta_0&gt;0\\) such that the following holds for\n\\[\n0&lt;\\vartheta\\leq\\vartheta_0,\\qquad \\Lambda=R^\\vartheta.\n\\]</p><p>Work in a fixed \\(O(R)\\) test box and one common padded time slab \\(S\\) of length \\(O(R)\\). Every tube core meets a fixed \\(O(R)\\) enlargement of the test box. Labels belong to a translated bounded-overlap mesh-\\(R^{-1/2}\\) lattice in a bounded patch, from a class closed under parabolic rescaling; finitely many cap-grid colors are allowed.</p><p>A labeled tube is\n\\[\nT=\\{(x,t)\\in S:|x-2vt-u|_\\infty\\leq\\Lambda R^{1/2}\\}.\n\\]\nDilation \\(cT\\) changes only transverse width, not the common slab. Same-label unclipped strips have bounded overlap.</p><p>Fix \\(1\\leq a\\leq10\\) and \\(a+\\frac12\\leq b\\leq20\\). Suppose nonzero \\(F_T\\) have comparable \\(L^p\\) norms,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{supp}\\widehat F_T\n\\subset\n\\{(\\xi,\\tau):|\\xi-v|\\lesssim R^{-1/2},\\\n|\\tau+|\\xi|^2|\\lesssim\\Lambda/R\\},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|F_T\\|_{L^p(\\mathbb R^d\\setminus aT)}\n\\leq R^{-B}\\|F_T\\|_p,\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large fixed \\(B\\). If \\(U\\) is a union of interior \\(R^{1/2}\\)-cubes, each meeting at most \\(M\\geq1\\) counted tubes \\(bT\\), then\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_TF_T\\right\\|_{L^p(U)}\n\\leq C_{\\varepsilon,\\vartheta,d,p}\nR^\\varepsilon M^{1/2-1/p}\n\\left(\\sum_T\\|F_T\\|_p^p\\right)^{1/p}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use weighted local Bourgain–Demeter decoupling:\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_JH_J\\right\\|_{L^p(Q_s)}\n\\leq D_{\\delta,L,d,p}s^\\delta\n\\left(\\sum_J\\|H_J\\|_{L^p(w_{Q_s,L})}^2\\right)^{1/2},\n\\]\nfor \\(s^{-1/2}\\)-caps and \\(O(s^{-1})\\) normal thickness, where\n\\[\nw_{Q_s,L}(z)=\n\\left(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{dist}(z,Q_s)}s\\right)^{-L}.\n\\]\nSumming ordinary local decoupling over translated cubes proves this weighted form.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal A_d(R,\\Lambda,a,b)\\) be the least constant without \\(R^\\varepsilon\\), defined for every \\(\\Lambda\\geq1\\) and every positive margin \\(b-a\\). Group fine labels into \\(R^{-1/4}\\)-caps. After placing the common slab midpoint at zero, assign each fine tube to one parent\n\\[\n\\Box_{J,u}\n=\\{(x,t)\\in S:|x-2v_0t-u|_\\infty\\leq C_dR^{3/4}\\}.\n\\]\nThe entire supporting \\(aT\\) lies in its parent. The parents have bounded overlap for fixed \\(J\\), and assigned families are disjoint.</p><p>On a parent use\n\\[\nx'=R^{-1/4}(x-2v_0t-u),\\qquad\nt'=R^{-1/2}t,\\qquad\nv'=R^{1/4}(v-v_0).\n\\]\nThe new radius is \\(R'=R^{1/2}\\), while \\(\\Lambda\\) remains unchanged. Fine supports become the corresponding \\(R'\\)-supports; Jacobians cancel from the normalized inequality.</p><p>Apply decoupling on original \\(R^{1/2}\\)-cubes with neighborhood radius\n\\[\n\\Lambda^{1/4}R^{1/2}.\n\\]\nChoose the weight order after \\(\\vartheta\\), so missing neighborhoods and relative packet tails contribute a negligible \\(R^{-B/2}\\) error. There are \\(O_d(\\Lambda^{d/4})\\) neighboring cubes and boundedly many relevant rescaling cells per neighbor and parent.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nb'=b-C_d\\Lambda^{-3/4}.\n\\]\nA lower-scale cell meeting \\(b'T\\), and meeting a relevant neighbor of \\(Q\\), forces \\(bT\\) to meet \\(Q\\): changes of transverse residual are at most \\(C_d\\Lambda^{1/4}R^{1/2}\\). The common slab prevents an individual tube-endpoint mismatch.</p><p>Pigeonhole cube norms, parent packet counts, lower-cell occupancies \\(M'\\), and numbers \\(n\\) of retained triples. The cost is \\((\\log R)^{C_d}\\). Disjoint parent assignment and the geometric transfer give\n\\[\nnM'\\lesssim\\Lambda^{d/4}M.\n\\]\nCombining this with decoupling, the lower-scale refined inequality, and\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_{\\ell=1}^na_\\ell^2\\right)^{p/2}\n\\leq n^{p/2-1}\\sum_{\\ell=1}^na_\\ell^p\n\\]\nyields\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_d(R,\\Lambda,a,b)\n\\lesssim\nD(\\log R)^{C_d}R^{\\delta/2}\\Lambda^{d/8}\n\\mathcal A_d(R^{1/2},\\Lambda,a,b-C_d\\Lambda^{-3/4})+1.\n\\]\nThe two neighborhood counts contribute exactly \\(\\Lambda^{d/8}\\), with no additional power of \\(R\\).</p><p>Stop when \\(\\Lambda\\gtrsim R_j^{1/4}\\), where \\(R_j=R^{2^{-j}}\\). Bounded same-label overlap gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_d(R_j,\\Lambda,a,b_j)\\lesssim R_j^{k/4}\\lesssim\\Lambda^k.\n\\]\nThere are \\(j=O(\\log(1/\\vartheta))\\) steps. Their total collar drift is less than \\(1/4\\) for large \\(R\\). Iteration gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_d(R,\\Lambda,a,b)\n\\lesssim\n(\\log R)^{C_dj}R^{C_d\\delta}\\Lambda^{k+dj/8}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\vartheta\\) so that its contribution is below \\(\\varepsilon/3\\), then \\(\\delta\\), and finally absorb logarithms. This is the buffered induction of Singh–Singh Parmar [26, Appendix A], with the dimension-dependent bookkeeping displayed; no refined estimate is an input.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Local estimates without a scale loss", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the packet conventions of Proposition 3.", "statement": "<p>Use the packet conventions of Proposition 3.</p><p>For a band-limited or smooth scale-\\(N\\) field\n\\[\nV=\\sum_Tf_T,\\qquad B^2=\\sum_Ts_T^2w_T,\n\\]\nevery spacetime ball \\(D\\) of radius \\(N\\), and every \\(z_0\\in D\\),\n\\[\n\\|V\\|_{L^{q_k}(D)}\\leq CN^{k/2}B(z_0).\n\\]</p><p>For \\(k+1\\) such fields \\(V_j,B_j\\), suppose their enlarged frequency-label sets satisfy\n\\[\n|\\det((2v_1,1),\\ldots,(2v_{k+1},1))|\\geq\\delta&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor every fixed \\(p&gt;p_k\\), every radius-\\(N\\) ball \\(D\\), and arbitrary \\(z_j\\in D\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\prod_{j=1}^{k+1}|V_j|^{1/(k+1)}\\right\\|_{L^p(D)}\n\\leq\nC_p\\delta^{-C_p}N^{k/2}\n\\prod_{j=1}^{k+1}B_j(z_j)^{1/(k+1)}.\n\\]\nBoth conclusions admit unit suprema; in the multilinear conclusion these may be separate for each factor. There is no \\(N^\\varepsilon\\) loss.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\nEg(x,t)=\\int e^{i(x\\cdot\\xi-t|\\xi|^2)}g(\\xi)\\,d\\xi.\n\\]\nCenter \\(D\\) at \\(z_0=(x_0,t_0)\\). For each frequency slot \\(v\\), take an integral-normalized bump \\(g_v\\) of width \\(c/N\\), modulated to the center. Its extension equals its carrier times a function \\(H_v\\) differing from one by less than \\(1/2\\) on an enlarged \\(D\\). Both \\(H_v\\) and \\(H_v^{-1}\\) have bounded derivatives in \\(z/N\\), and\n\\[\n\\|g_v\\|_2\\leq CN^{k/2}.\n\\]\nIn the transverse case take \\(c\\) a fixed power of \\(\\delta\\), so supports remain \\(\\delta/2\\)-transverse.</p><p>Divide each demodulated packet profile by \\(H_v\\), cut off, and Fourier-expand in \\(z/N\\):\n\\[\nf_T(z)=\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb Z^{k+1}}\nc_{T,m}e^{i\\ell_m\\cdot(z-z_0)/N}Eg_{v(T)}(z),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n|c_{T,m}|\n\\leq C_Ls_T(1+d_T(z_0))^{-P''}(1+|m|)^{-L},\n\\quad P''&gt;P/2+k+1.\n\\]\nCombining same-slot coefficients and using Cauchy–Schwarz over native centers gives\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_Tc_{T,m}g_{v(T)}\\right\\|_2^2\n\\leq\nC_LN^k(1+|m|)^{-2L}\\sum_Ts_T^2w_T(z_0).\n\\]\nDistinct slots have bounded Fourier overlap; same-slot native tails are summable.</p><p>The global exact Strichartz estimate\n\\[\n\\|Eg\\|_{q_k}\\leq C\\|g\\|_2\n\\]\nfollows from the dispersive kernel, \\(TT^*\\), interpolation, and one-dimensional Hardy–Littlewood–Sobolev in time. Apply it mode by mode for the linear conclusion.</p><p>For the multilinear conclusion use Tao [30, Theorem 1.7] away from the endpoint. In ambient dimension \\(d=k+1\\), its product exponent yields geometric-mean exponent \\(p&gt;p_k\\). Domain size, graph derivatives, inverse determinant, and interior support distance are controlled by fixed powers of \\(\\delta^{-1}\\); subdividing and enlarging patches by polynomially small amounts in \\(\\delta\\) gives the stated polynomial dependence. Expand over modes using subadditivity of the \\(1/(k+1)\\)-power and Minkowski, choosing \\(L\\) sufficiently large.</p><p>Finally apply Lemma 3.1 separately to exact extension factors. A spacetime translation only multiplies their inputs by unimodular phases, so the global norm estimates are unchanged. Tonelli and summable convolution weights prove the separate-supremum assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Weighted transport through a hierarchy", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Take nested frequency grids at inverse widths \\(K<K'\\), rounded within fixed factors, in the packet setting of Proposition 3.", "statement": "<p>Take nested frequency grids at inverse widths \\(K&lt;K'\\), rounded within fixed factors, in the packet setting of Proposition 3. Assemble child fields in each parent cap and localize on the parent physical grid by fixed band-limited masks with summable Schwartz tails and bounded absolute sums.</p><p>The resulting amplitudes \\(a_T\\) may be chosen so that, whenever parent auxiliary weights satisfy\n\\[\nb_T^2\\geq c\n\\sum_{T'\\text{ in the parent cap}}b_{T'}^2w_{T'}(z_T),\n\\]\none has, for \\(2&lt;p\\leq q_k\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_TK^{k+2}a_T^pb_T^{2-p}\n\\leq\nC_\\varepsilon(K'/K)^\\varepsilon\n\\sum_{T'}(K')^{k+2}a_{T'}^pb_{T'}^{2-p}.\n\\]</p><p>The assertion persists after retaining any parent-cell subset. Modified child fields use fresh amplitudes in the same fixed masks. For subtrees supported in a codimension-one strip at final frequency precision, \\(p=q_{k-1}\\) is allowed at every transition; for \\(k=1\\), any fixed finite \\(p&gt;2\\) is allowed.</p><p>Finite products give the hierarchy estimate. Fixed polynomial macro weights and fixed additional edge decay are allowed. Constants may depend on the fixed finite grid, but original regularity and decay orders do not increase with depth.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a positive band-limited partition from \\(\\chi=|\\phi|^2\\), with \\(\\widehat\\phi\\) smooth and sufficiently small. Poisson summation normalizes \\(\\sum_j\\chi(X-j)=1\\). If \\(U\\) is the pre-cutoff parent-cap field, take\n\\[\na_T=C\\sup_z|U(z)|(1+d_T(z))^{-C_0},\n\\]\nwith a fixed excess supremum-decay reserve. Multiplication by the mask gives fixed enlarged Fourier support and the prescribed output decay.</p><p>In parent coordinates the children have scale \\(K'/K\\). Apply Proposition 3 and its unit-supremum form. The child square background varies polynomially over a parent cell; the reserve absorbs this variation. The negative exponent \\(2-p\\) permits the displayed domination by \\(b_T\\). The coordinate Jacobian supplies \\(K^{k+2}\\).</p><p>Deletion decreases the output sum. For modified inputs recompute the amplitudes rather than assuming old cancellations survive. The affine-plane part of Proposition 3 proves the strip version.</p><p>Lemma 5.1 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_Tw_T(z)\\sum_{T'}b_{T'}^2w_{T'}(z_T)\n\\leq C\\sum_{T'}b_{T'}^2w_{T'}(z),\n\\]\nwithout lowering the envelope exponent. A distant finite path has a large normalized displacement on some edge; reserve decay on that edge and sum over its possible location. This costs the number of edges, not a growing decay order.</p><p>For a positive macro weight \\(\\Psi\\) with both endpoint ratios bounded by \\(C(1+d)^J\\), conjugate\n\\[\n\\widetilde b_T=b_T\\Psi(z_T)^{-1/(p-2)},\n\\qquad Q=P+\\frac{2J}{p-2}.\n\\]\nThe stronger fixed child envelope of order \\(Q\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_T\\Psi(z_T)K^{k+2}a_T^pb_T^{2-p}\n\\leq\nC_\\varepsilon(K'/K)^\\varepsilon\n\\sum_{T'}\\Psi(z_{T'})(K')^{k+2}a_{T'}^pb_{T'}^{2-p}.\n\\]\nThe same reserve works on every edge. Summable macro partitions have bounded total overlap.</p><p>For separated scales, inverse Fourier widths added by localization form a geometric series. Treat comparable scales as a single transition. Thus Fourier enlargement and original regularity are fixed once, while constants may depend on the finite grid.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Sampling preserves the envelope exponent", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(K'\\geq K\\geq1\\), let a \\(K'\\)-cap lie in a \\(K\\)-cap, and let \\(T'\\) be a scale-\\(K'\\) child cell.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(K'\\geq K\\geq1\\), let a \\(K'\\)-cap lie in a \\(K\\)-cap, and let \\(T'\\) be a scale-\\(K'\\) child cell. For the parent grid \\(\\mathcal G_K\\), and sufficiently large \\(P&gt;k+2\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{T\\in\\mathcal G_K}w_T(z)w_{T'}(z_T)\n\\leq C_Pw_{T'}(z).\n\\]\nThe exponent \\(P\\) is unchanged, independently of \\(K'/K\\).</p><p>Restricted to \\(d_T(z)&gt;L\\), \\(L\\geq2\\), the sum is at most\n\\[\nC_P\\left(L^{-(P-k-1)}+(K/K')^{P-k-2}\\right)w_{T'}(z).\n\\]\nIf \\(d_{T'}(z)\\) is bounded, omit the second term.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(r=K/K'\\). In parent coordinates the child map has spatial coefficients \\(O(r)\\), time coefficient \\(r^2\\), determinant \\(r^{k+2}\\), and bounded normalized shear. Set \\(a=1+d_{T'}(z)\\).</p><p>Parent centers at child distance at least \\(a/2\\) contribute at most\n\\[\nCa^{-P}\\sum_Tw_T(z)\\leq Ca^{-P};\n\\]\nthe distance restriction adds the lattice-tail factor \\(L^{-(P-k-1)}\\).</p><p>For the other centers, \\(a\\gg1\\) and \\(d_T(z)\\geq ca/r\\), so their contribution is at most\n\\[\nC(a/r)^{-P}\\sum_Tw_{T'}(z_T)\n\\leq Ca^{-P}r^{P-k-2}.\n\\]\nThe last sum is bounded by the child-scale integral and a fixed boundary term. This proves both conclusions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Near-Cauchy large values", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\( V=\\sum_Tf_T,\\qquad B^2=\\sum_Ts_T^2w_T \\) be a band-limited scale-\\(N\\) field as in Proposition 3.", "statement": "<p>Let\n\\[\nV=\\sum_Tf_T,\\qquad B^2=\\sum_Ts_T^2w_T\n\\]\nbe a band-limited scale-\\(N\\) field as in Proposition 3. Suppose a finite collection \\(Y\\) of distinct unit cubes has witnesses \\(z_Q\\in Q\\) satisfying\n\\[\nB(z_Q)\\leq1,\\qquad |V(z_Q)|\\geq N^{k/2}/R,\\qquad R\\geq1.\n\\]\nFor every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n|Y|\\leq C_\\varepsilon R^{q_k+\\varepsilon}\\sum_Ts_T^2.\n\\]\nUnit suprema and fixed witness-cube enlargements are allowed. Fourier enlargement and original decay orders are fixed before \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix the packet class. Call \\(p\\) admissible if\n\\[\n|Y|\\leq C_pR^pS,\\qquad S=\\sum_Ts_T^2,\n\\]\nuniformly in that class. Lemma 6.1 makes the infimum \\(p_*\\) finite. Suppose \\(p_*&gt;q=q_k\\). There is a normalized countersequence with\n\\[\nR\\to\\infty,\\qquad |Y|\\geq R^{p_*-o(1)}S.\n\\]\nThe power-loss estimate from Proposition 3 forces\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log N}{\\log R}\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nEvery later subpower assertion means: fix finitely many tolerances and tests, take \\(R\\) sufficiently large, then send tolerances to zero. Use admissible exponents above \\(p_*\\), not \\(p_*\\) itself.</p><p>Delete fine packets whose \\(CR\\)-native enlargement contains more than \\(R^{C_1}\\) witnesses. Double counting gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mathrm{deleted}}s_T^2\\leq CR^{P-C_1}|Y|.\n\\]\nA fixed admissible estimate removes their high contribution on a negligible fraction if \\(C_1\\) is large. Surviving fine occupancy is at most \\(R^{C_1}\\).</p><p>Choose \\(A\\) large and set\n\\[\nK=N/R^A,\\qquad L=N/K=R^A.\n\\]\nAssemble into \\(K\\)-packets using Proposition 5, with backgrounds\n\\[\nb_T^2=C\\sum_{T'\\text{ in its cap}}\ns_{T'}^2(1+d_{T'}(z_T))^{-P-\\sigma}\n\\]\nfor a fixed extra \\(\\sigma&gt;0\\). Propositions 3 and 5 and Lemma 5.1 give\n\\[\n\\sum_Tb_T^2w_T\\leq CB^2,\\qquad\n\\sum_TK^{k+2}a_T^qb_T^{2-q}\\leq R^{o(1)}E.\n\\]\nCauchy gives \\(a_T/b_T\\leq CL^{k/2}\\). Ratios below \\(L^{k/2}R^{-2}\\) are negligible. Select one of the remaining \\(O(\\log R)\\) classes,\n\\[\na_T\\simeq rb_T,\n\\]\nretaining a subpower fraction and threshold. Applying an admissible estimate after division by \\(Cr\\) yields\n\\[\n|Y|\n\\leq R^{p_*+o(1)}S(rL^{-k/2})^{p_*-q}.\n\\]\nComparison with the countersequence forces\n\\[\nr=L^{k/2}R^{o(1)},\\qquad\n\\sum_{\\mathrm{selected}}b_T^2\\leq R^{o(1)}S.\n\\]\nThe same packet class persists at scale \\(K\\), since inherited Fourier enlargement contracts by \\(L^{-1}\\) before the fixed parent localizer is added.</p><p>Discard \\(b_T&gt;R^3K^{-k/2}\\); globally their field is bounded by\n\\[\nCrK^{k/2}R^{-3}\\sum_Tb_T^2w_T,\n\\]\nwhich is negligible. The selected square background is at least \\(R^{-o(1)}\\) on a retained subpower fraction: points where it is at most \\(R^{-\\eta}\\) have count bounded by\n\\[\nR^{p_*+o(1)}S\\,R^{-\\eta(p_*/2-1)}.\n\\]</p><p>Lemma 5.1 localizes this square mass to \\(d_T(z)\\leq R^{o(1)}\\). The extra \\(\\sigma\\) in the sampled fine weights also ensures that every retained incidence has a surviving fine descendant within \\(R^{o(1)}\\) fine native distance. Let \\(I_0(z,T)\\) denote these incidences and\n\\[\nm_0(z)=\\sum_Tb_T^2I_0(z,T).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nR^{-o(1)}\\leq m_0(z)\\leq R^{o(1)},\\quad\nb_T\\leq R^{3+o(1)}K^{-k/2},\\quad\n\\sum_Tb_T^2\\leq R^{o(1)}S.\n\\]\nDraw \\(T\\) at \\(z\\) with probability \\(b_T^2I_0/m_0\\), attaching a nearby surviving fine descendant. Its angular law obeys\n\\[\n\\mu_z(\\text{one }1/K\\text{ slot})\n\\leq R^{6+o(1)}K^{-k}.\n\\]</p><p>Restrict to typical directions satisfying\n\\[\n\\mu_z(B(v,s))\\geq R^{-1-o(1)}s^k\n\\qquad(K^{-1}\\leq s\\leq1).\n\\]\nA maximal-bad-cell argument in finitely many shifted dyadic grids removes only \\(O(R^{-1+o(1)})\\) probability, without a number-of-scales loss.</p><p>For \\(y\\in Y\\), \\(\\tau=|t_y-t_z|\\geq N\\), coarse co-incidence has probability at most\n\\[\nR^{6+o(1)}(K/\\tau)^k.\n\\]\nIf nonempty, a typical direction seeing \\(y\\) has a radius-\\(cN/\\tau\\) ball of original mass at least\n\\[\nR^{-1-o(1)}(N/\\tau)^k.\n\\]\nEvery draw in this ball has an attached fine packet seeing both points in its \\(CR\\)-enlargement: intercept, angular drift, parent–fine label discrepancy, and time-separation errors all fit that enlargement. Comparing probabilities and summing fine occupancies bounds expected far coarse occupancy by\n\\[\nR^{C_1+7-Ak+o(1)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\tau&lt;N\\), points lie in a \\(CN\\)-ball; Proposition 4 bounds them by \\(CR^q\\). Choose \\(Ak&gt;C_1+q+10\\). Expected coarse occupancy is therefore at most \\(R^{q+o(1)}\\).</p><p>Conversely, with \\(I\\) the restricted incidence relation and\n\\[\nn_T=\\#\\{z\\in Y:I(z,T)=1\\},\n\\]\naveraging occupancy and applying Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\frac{R^{-o(1)}}{|Y|}\\sum_Tb_T^2n_T^2\n\\geq\n\\frac{R^{-o(1)}}{|Y|}\n\\frac{(\\sum_Tb_T^2n_T)^2}{\\sum_Tb_T^2}\n\\geq R^{-o(1)}|Y|/S\n\\geq R^{p_*-o(1)}.\n\\]\nThis contradicts \\(p_*&gt;q\\). Hence \\(p_*\\leq q\\), so every \\(q+\\varepsilon\\) is admissible. Lemma 3.1 supplies the stated witness variants.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "A polynomial starting bound", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 6's hypotheses, there is a fixed finite \\(M_k\\) such that \\( |Y|\\leq C(2+R)^{M_k}\\sum_Ts_T^2. \\)", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 6's hypotheses, there is a fixed finite \\(M_k\\) such that\n\\[\n|Y|\\leq C(2+R)^{M_k}\\sum_Ts_T^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(S=\\sum_Ts_T^2\\). Proposition 3 with \\(b_T=s_T\\), \\(p=q_k\\), gives\n\\[\n|Y|\\leq C_\\varepsilon N^\\varepsilon R^{q_k}S.\n\\]\nThus any range \\(N\\leq(2+R)^D\\), for fixed \\(D\\), is already polynomial. Assume \\(N\\) exceeds a sufficiently large fixed power of \\(2+R\\).</p><p>Discard \\(s_T&gt;CRN^{-k/2}\\). Their field is at most\n\\[\n(CRN^{-k/2})^{-1}\\sum_Ts_T^2w_T\n\\leq C^{-1}N^{k/2}/R.\n\\]\nFor the remainder \\(s_T\\leq s_0=CRN^{-k/2}\\). Partition frequency into bins of side \\(P_1/N\\), where\n\\[\nP_1=(C(2+R))^{10},\n\\]\nand write the remaining field as \\(\\sum_bV_b\\). Cauchy–Schwarz implies\n\\[\n\\sum_b|V_b(z_Q)|^2\\geq cP_1^k/R^2.\n\\]\nRemove pairs of labels separated by at most \\(C'/N\\). Their contribution is \\(O(B^2)=O(1)\\). The remaining product sum \\(G=\\sum_bG_b\\) therefore satisfies \\(|G(z_Q)|\\geq1\\).</p><p>If \\(v_b\\) is the bin center, then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{supp}\\widehat G_b\n\\subset\n\\left\\{c/N\\leq|\\zeta|\\leq CP_1/N,\\\n|\\sigma+2v_b\\cdot\\zeta|\\leq CP_1^2/N^2\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThese supports overlap at most \\(CN^{k-1}P_1^{C_k}\\) times. Within a bin, effective packet multiplicity is \\(O(P_1^k)\\). Plancherel, the amplitude bound, and envelope integration give\n\\[\n\\|G\\|_2^2\\leq CP_1^{C_k}R^2N^{k+1}S.\n\\]\nThe spacetime Fourier support of \\(G\\) has size \\(O(P_1/N)\\). The rescaled submean inequality of Lemma 3.1 shows that only \\((2+R)^{C_k}S\\) cubes of side \\(N/P_1^{C_2}\\) can have \\(\\sup|G|\\geq1\\). Each lies in an \\(N\\)-ball. If such a ball contains a witness, Proposition 4 bounds its high unit-cube count by \\(CR^{q_k}\\). Multiply the two bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Frame estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the packet coordinates, envelopes, and multiplicity conventions of Proposition 3.", "statement": "<p>Use the packet coordinates, envelopes, and multiplicity conventions of Proposition 3. Fix \\(k\\geq2\\), \\(c&gt;0\\), and fixed packet bounds \\(C_0\\). There is a finite \\(P_0\\) such that, for every fixed \\(P\\geq P_0\\), there are a finite derivative order \\(J_0\\) and a constant \\(C\\), depending only on \\(k,c,C_0,P\\) and the packet conventions, with the following property.</p><p>Let \\(h\\geq2\\), \\(m=k+1\\), and let \\(V_1,\\ldots,V_m\\) be scale-\\(h\\) packet sums on one time interval of length at most \\(C_0h^2\\). Their normalized compact profiles have derivatives through order \\(J_0\\) bounded by \\(C_0\\). Their assigned frequency-label sets, with fixed cap enlargements included, satisfy\n\\[\n|\\det((2\\xi_1,1),\\ldots,(2\\xi_m,1))|\\geq\\delta&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor every distinct unit cube \\(Q\\in Y\\), suppose there are separate witnesses \\(z_{i,Q}\\in Q\\) such that\n\\[\n|V_i(z_{i,Q})|\\geq A_i\\geq h^c,\\qquad\nB_i(z_{i,Q})^2=\\sum_Ts_{i,T}^2w_T(z_{i,Q})\\leq1.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nH_i=A_i^{2/k},\\qquad\nH_g=\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^mH_i\\right)^{1/m},\\qquad\nD_i=h^{k+2}\\sum_Ts_{i,T}^2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n|Y|\\leq C\\delta^{-C}H_g^{-2}\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\frac{D_i}{A_i^2}.\n\\]\nA packet mask is multiplication by a selected sum of smooth packet cutoffs at its own scale; restrictions may retain arbitrary subsets. Such sums have bounded absolute value.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 7.1 work with exact packets. If the estimate fails, choose counterexamples violating every fixed constant and inverse determinant power. Then\n\\[\nh\\to\\infty,\\qquad\\delta=h^{-o(1)}.\n\\]\nAbsolute Cauchy gives \\(H_i\\lesssim h\\); Proposition 3 gives\n\\[\n|Y|\\leq h^{o(1)}D_iH_i^{-k-2}.\n\\]\nComparison with the failed frame bound yields\n\\[\nH_i/H_g=h^{o(1)},\\qquad |Y|H_i^{k+2}/D_i=h^{o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>At inverse width \\(H_i\\), select original transition masks with\n\\[\na_T^{\\mathrm{orig}}\\simeq S_iH_i^{-k/2},\\qquad S_i\\gtrsim1.\n\\]\nThreshold splitting and countable pigeonholing retain\n\\[\n|Y|\\geq(\\log(2+S))^{-C}H_g^{-2}\\sum_iD_iH_i^{-k},\n\\quad S=\\max_iS_i,\n\\]\nwith arbitrarily large additional constant and inverse determinant power. Spatial orthogonality and local Bernstein give\n\\[\nH_i^{k+2}\\sum_{\\mathrm{selected}}(a_T^{\\mathrm{orig}})^2\\lesssim D_i.\n\\]\nThe selected transition packets have square background \\(O(S_i)\\) in amplitude. Proposition 6 gives all balance ratios bounded by \\(S^C\\); therefore \\(S\\to\\infty\\) and \\(\\delta=S^{-o(1)}\\).</p><p>Pass to the power case \\(\\log S\\gtrsim\\log h\\), \\(\\Lambda=h\\), or the subpower case \\(S=h^{o(1)}\\), \\(\\Lambda=S\\). In the latter, Lemma 7.2 supplies clipped square backgrounds. Retain only original \\(H_i\\)-masks centered where \\(e_b^2\\leq M_c\\). The discarded bins are few by the clipped sum and cannot supply a significant original-amplitude contribution. Sampling retained centers gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{T\\text{ retained in }b}e_b(z_T)^2w_T(z)\n\\lesssim\\min(e_b(z)^2,M_c).\n\\]\nUse clipped squares at strict ancestors and full squares below eligible transition bins. Propositions 3, 5, and 6 then improve the balances to\n\\[\n|Y|\\leq S^{o(1)}D_iH_i^{-k-2},\\qquad\nH_i/H_g=S^{o(1)},\\qquad\n|Y|H_i^{k+2}/D_i=S^{o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>Apply Lemmas 7.3–7.4 to construct finite-grid profiles, preserving the original transition masks and amplitudes as separate charging records. Lemmas 7.5–7.9 force binary slopes. Lemma 7.10 locates the power-case full-slope interval: its length is\n\\[\ns_H=\\lim\\log_hH_i&gt;0,\n\\]\nthe same for every field, and cross-field halving makes all left endpoints zero. Hence \\(f_i(s_H)=0\\). Lemma 7.12 gives \\(f_i(0)=0\\) in the subpower case. Thus the recomputed transition ratio is \\(\\Lambda^{o(1)}\\) in either case.</p><p>Make one final occupancy deletion at transition scale. For a fixed arbitrarily small \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), enlarge cells by \\(\\Lambda^\\eta\\); in the subpower case count only eligible points with the corresponding polynomial allowance. Incident squares sum to at most \\(\\Lambda^{C_2\\eta+o(1)}|Y|\\). Cells with more than \\(\\Lambda^{C_1'\\eta}\\) visits have negative-power weighted \\(q\\)-charge when \\(C_1'&gt;C_2\\) is sufficiently large. Lemma 7.3 discards their root contribution without reselecting ratios.</p><p>Fixed decay now makes tails negligible. At each retained root point, threshold\n\\[\nA_i\\Lambda^{-o(1)}=H_i^{k/2}\\Lambda^{-o(1)}\n\\]\nand transition square sum \\(\\Lambda^{o(1)}\\) require, by weighted Cauchy and bounded ancestor-mask sums, at least \\(H_i^k\\Lambda^{-o(1)}\\) nearby masks. Double counting visits, then letting \\(\\eta\\) be arbitrarily small, gives at least\n\\[\n|Y|H_i^k\\Lambda^{-o(1)}\n\\]\ndistinct surviving original transition masks.</p><p>Choose \\(i\\) with \\(S_i=S\\). Their original amplitudes and original energy budget imply\n\\[\nD_i\\gtrsim |Y|H_i^{k+2}S^2\\Lambda^{-o(1)}.\n\\]\nThis contradicts the power-case balances because \\(S\\) is a fixed positive power of \\(h=\\Lambda\\), and the subpower balances because \\(S=\\Lambda\\). Lemma 7.1 transfers back to compact profiles with finite regularity. The countersequence choice yields the uniform \\(C\\delta^{-C}\\) form.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Reduction to exact packets", "depth": 1, "snippet": "It suffices to prove Proposition 7 for translated exact paraboloid extensions with smooth data in bounded native frequency boxes, multiplied by a common Schwartz function \\( \\chi_h(t)=\\chi((t-t_0)/h^2) \\) that is …", "statement": "<p>It suffices to prove Proposition 7 for translated exact paraboloid extensions with smooth data in bounded native frequency boxes, multiplied by a common Schwartz function\n\\[\n\\chi_h(t)=\\chi((t-t_0)/h^2)\n\\]\nthat is positive on the test interval and has compact Fourier support. All required exact-packet seminorms can be fixed in advance.</p>", "proof": "<p>Cut off on a fixed enlargement of the test interval. Fourier-transform each compact profile in native space, partition into unit frequency boxes centered at \\(a\\in\\mathbb Z^k\\), remove the quadratic evolution, and Fourier-expand the remaining native-time dependence. The layers, indexed by \\((a,\\nu)\\in\\mathbb Z^{k+1}\\), have exact data satisfying\n\\[\n\\|\\operatorname{data}_{a,\\nu}\\|_{C^J}\n\\leq C_{J,M}(1+|a|+|\\nu|)^{-M}\n\\]\nprovided finitely many original derivatives are bounded.</p><p>The physical center shifts by \\(a/h\\); recentering trajectories costs only a fixed polynomial in \\(1+|a|\\). Normalize layers by\n\\[\nc_{a,\\nu}=(1+|a|+|\\nu|)^{-M/2}\n\\]\nand allocate threshold fractions proportional to \\((1+|a|+|\\nu|)^{-M/4}\\). In the \\(i\\)-th energy term the threshold dependence is\n\\[\nD_iA_i^{-2}\\prod_{j=1}^mA_j^{-4/(km)},\n\\]\nso every layer variable has a negative threshold power and the mode sums converge for large \\(M\\).</p><p>Modes \\(|a|&gt;h^{1/2}\\) are arbitrarily negative-power errors by weighted Cauchy. Remaining shifts preserve transversality when \\(\\delta\\geq h^{-\\varepsilon_0}\\), with small fixed \\(\\varepsilon_0\\). If \\(\\delta&lt;h^{-\\varepsilon_0}\\), an elementary polynomial-in-\\(h\\) bound is absorbed by \\(\\delta^{-C}\\).</p><p>Exact packets have only polynomial native-time growth in the finitely many localization seminorms. Multiplying by the common positive-on-the-slab Schwartz \\(\\chi_h\\) makes all relevant spacetime integrals finite. Its upper and lower bounds on the test slab change thresholds only by fixed factors.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Clipped smoothing", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose an exact-packet countersequence to Proposition 7 has been normalized as follows:", "statement": "<p>Suppose an exact-packet countersequence to Proposition 7 has been normalized as follows:\n\\[\nH_i/H_g=h^{o(1)},\\qquad |Y|H_i^{k+2}/D_i=h^{o(1)}.\n\\]\nAt inverse width \\(H_i\\), fix selected original transition masks with amplitudes\n\\[\na_T^{\\mathrm{orig}}\\simeq S_iH_i^{-k/2},\\qquad\nS=\\max_iS_i\\to\\infty,\n\\]\nand original energy budget\n\\[\nH_i^{k+2}\\sum_{\\mathrm{selected}}(a_T^{\\mathrm{orig}})^2\\lesssim D_i.\n\\]\nAssume the subpower case \\(S=h^{o(1)}\\), with\n\\[\nH_i/H_g+H_g/H_i+\nD_i/(|Y|H_i^{k+2})+\n|Y|H_i^{k+2}/D_i\\lesssim S^C.\n\\]\nDelete transition packets with more than \\(S^{C_1}\\) visits in a fixed power enlargement, as in the proof below.</p><p>Fix one field and write \\(H=H_i\\), \\(D=D_i\\). For sufficiently large fixed \\(C_3\\), there is a fixed \\(L_0\\) such that, if\n\\[\nK_0=HS^{L_0}\\leq h,\n\\]\neach inverse-width-\\(H\\) cap field \\(V_b\\) has a scale-\\(K_0\\) representation of integrated square energy at most \\(CD\\), with\n\\[\ne_b(z)^2=\\sum_{\\gamma\\in b}s_\\gamma^2w_\\gamma^{\\mathrm{work}}(z)\n\\]\nsatisfying\n\\[\n\\sum_b\\min(e_b(z)^2,M_c)\\leq S^{o(1)},\\qquad\nM_c=H^{-k}S^{C_3},\n\\]\noutside \\(o(|Y|)\\) witnesses. Here\n\\[\nw_\\gamma^{\\mathrm{work}}=(1+d_\\gamma)^{-P_{\\mathrm{work}}},\n\\qquad P_{\\mathrm{work}}&gt;P,\n\\]\nhas one fixed order for all subsequent subpower-tree levels.</p><p>If \\(K_0&gt;h\\), use unchanged scale-\\(h\\) leaves and their original exponent \\(P\\), whose square envelope is already bounded. All orders are finite and independent of later grid depth.</p>", "proof": "<p>The occupancy deletion is cheap by Proposition 6. Each remaining high point needs at least \\(H^k/S^C\\) nearby cap slots. Counting transition-cell incidences in a ball gives\n\\[\n\\#(Y\\cap B_r)\\lesssim S^C(r/H)^k(1+r/H^2)\n\\qquad(r\\geq H).\n\\]</p><p>Write the original uncut cap field as \\(U_b\\), so \\(V_b=\\chi_hU_b\\). Define\n\\[\n\\rho=H^{k+2}\\sum_{z\\in Y}\\delta_z,\\qquad\nF_b(x,t)=w_h(t)|U_b(x,t)|^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nw_h(t)=\\left(1+\\frac{(t-t_0)^2}{h^4}\\right)^{-J}.\n\\]\nSpatial unitarity, cap overlap, and the original fine-packet Gram estimate give\n\\[\n\\sum_b\\int F_b\\lesssim D.\n\\]</p><p>Choose even \\(Q\\) large relative to \\(P,k\\), and put\n\\[\nj_K(x)=c_QK^{-k}(1+|x/K|^2)^{-Q/2}.\n\\]\nConvolve in space by heat variance \\(K^2\\) and independent symmetric jumps of density \\(j_r\\), intensity \\(dr/r\\), for \\(r\\leq K\\). The resulting \\(J_K\\) satisfies\n\\[\nC^{-1}j_K\\leq J_K\\leq Cj_K,\\qquad\n|\\nabla J_K|\\leq CK^{-1}j_K,\\qquad\n|\\widehat J_K(\\xi)|\\leq e^{-cK^2|\\xi|^2}.\n\\]\nFor the lower tail bound request one jump in \\([K/2,K]\\) and bounded remaining displacement. For the upper bound group jumps dyadically, sum candidates using bounded Poisson moments, and take \\(Q&gt;2k\\). Differentiating a broader Gaussian gives the gradient bound.</p><p>In coordinates moving with \\(2b\\), also convolve in time by a Gaussian of width\n\\[\nT(K)=KH/P_K,\\qquad P_K=\\min(H,K/H)^\\theta,\\quad0&lt;\\theta&lt;1/4.\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\rho_{K,b},F_{K,b}\\) for the smoothed functions. The logarithmic spatial generator is, up to heat conventions,\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_K=K^2\\Delta_x+j_K*-I.\n\\]\nThe ball count implies\n\\[\n\\int_{|\\zeta|\\lesssim1/r}|\\widehat\\rho(\\zeta)|^2\\,d\\zeta\n\\lesssim S^C\\|\\rho\\|_1(1+H^2/r)\\qquad(r\\geq H).\n\\]\nOn spatial frequency \\(1/r\\), the generator multiplier is bounded by\n\\[\nC_N(K/r)^2(1+K/r)^{-N}.\n\\]\nThe time Gaussian restricts \\(|\\tau+2b\\cdot\\xi|\\lesssim T^{-1}\\); only a fraction \\(O(r/T+H^{-1})\\) of the \\(H^{-1}\\)-grid cap centers can obey this. Dyadic summation gives, for some \\(c'&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nH^{-k}\\sum_b\\|\\mathcal L_K\\rho_{K,b}\\|_2^2\n\\lesssim\nS^C\\|\\rho\\|_1\\min(H,K/H)^{-c'}.\n\\]</p><p>The exact equation and weighted Bernstein give\n\\[\n|\\partial_{t,b}F_{K,b}|\\lesssim(HK)^{-1}F_{K,b},\n\\qquad \\partial_{t,b}=\\partial_t+2b\\cdot\\nabla_x.\n\\]\nIndeed\n\\[\n\\partial_{t,b}|U_b|^2\n=-2\\nabla_x\\cdot\\operatorname{Im}\n\\bigl(\\overline{U_b}(\\nabla_x-ib)U_b\\bigr),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\int j_K(x-y)|(\\nabla-ib)U_b(y,t)|^2\\,dy\n\\lesssim H^{-2}\\int j_K(x-y)|U_b(y,t)|^2\\,dy.\n\\]\nReproduction at width \\(H\\), polynomial weight comparison, and weighted Young prove the latter. The time weight contributes \\(O(h^{-2})\\), no larger than \\((HK)^{-1}\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\phi(u)=M_c(1-e^{-u/M_c}),\\qquad\nI(K)=\\sum_b\\int\\rho_{K,b}\\phi(F_{K,b}).\n\\]\nConcavity gives \\(\\phi'(F)\\mathcal LF\\geq\\mathcal L\\phi(F)\\). Differentiate \\(I\\), pair generators with \\(\\rho\\), use Cauchy–Schwarz and \\(\\phi(F)^2\\leq M_cF\\), and integrate the time error by parts. The total error from \\(K_0\\) to \\(h\\) is at most\n\\[\nS^C\\|\\rho\\|_1\\{S^{-cL_0}+H^{-c}\\log h\\}\n=o(\\|\\rho\\|_1).\n\\]\nHere \\(H\\geq h^{2c/k}\\) and \\(S=h^{o(1)}\\).</p><p>At \\(K=h\\), drop clipping. The original fine-packet Gram estimate gives\n\\[\n\\sum_b\\int j_h(x-x_z-2b\\tau)|U_b(x,t_z+\\tau)|^2\\,dx\n\\lesssim\n\\langle\\tau/(Hh)\\rangle^{M_1}\\sum_Ts_T^2w_T(z).\n\\]\nGaussian moments and \\(T(h)\\leq Hh\\) imply \\(I(h)\\lesssim\\|\\rho\\|_1\\). At \\(K_0\\), kernel comparability and the comoving derivative estimate show that \\(I(K_0)\\) dominates a constant multiple of\n\\[\nH^{k+2}\\sum_{z\\in Y}\\sum_b\\min(F_{K_0,b}(z),M_c).\n\\]\nThus this clipping sum has bounded mean.</p><p>Recover actual packets by partitioning \\(U_b\\) into width-\\(K_0^{-1}\\) caps and localizing \\(\\chi_hP_vU_b\\) on scale-\\(K_0\\) grids. Weighted suprema give\n\\[\nK_0^{k+2}\\sum_{v,\\lambda}s_{b,v,\\lambda}^2\n\\lesssim\\sum_v\\int|\\chi_hP_vU_b|^2\n\\lesssim\\int w_h|U_b|^2.\n\\]\nIn native scale, a propagation kernel obeys\n\\[\n|L_\\sigma(r)|\\leq C_N\\langle\\sigma\\rangle^N\\langle r\\rangle^{-N}.\n\\]\nTaking \\(N=Q+k+2\\), \\(P_{\\mathrm{work}}=3N+3\\), and a fixed excess supremum order, sum packet sizes first in native time and then space. This bounds their square envelope by a spatial \\(j_{K_0}\\)-energy at any central smoothing time.</p><p>Use weighted Bessel,\n\\[\n\\sum_v\\int j_{K_0}(x-x_0)|P_vf(x)|^2\\,dx\n\\lesssim\n\\int j_{K_0}(x-x_0)|f(x)|^2\\,dx.\n\\]\nIt follows by positive-polynomial-weight synthesis, symbol differentiation, bounded cap overlap, Plancherel, and duality. Averaging in the time Gaussian yields \\(e_b^2\\lesssim F_{K_0,b}\\). Markov with a slowly diverging subpower threshold proves the assertion. No reconstructed fine squares are compared with the original square envelope.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Finite-grid selection and discard", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Continue the normalized countersequence of Lemma 7.2, or its power-case analogue.", "statement": "<p>Continue the normalized countersequence of Lemma 7.2, or its power-case analogue. In the power case use\n\\[\n\\Lambda=h,\\qquad \\kappa_i(s)=h^s,\\quad0&lt;s&lt;e_i=1.\n\\]\nIn the subpower case use\n\\[\n\\Lambda=S,\\qquad\n\\kappa_i(s)=H_iS^s,\\quad\n-\\infty&lt;s&lt;e_i,\\qquad\ne_i=\\min\\{L_0,\\lim\\log_S(h/H_i)\\}.\n\\]\nInclude the actual fine endpoint and the original transition masks as distinct layers when needed.</p><p>On each fixed finite grid one may select amplitude-to-background ratios \\(r_{i,s}\\), retain a subpower fraction of high cubes, and retain masks such that, with\n\\[\nM_{i,s}=\\kappa_i(s)^{k+2}(r_{i,s}/A_i)^q,\\qquad\nq=\\frac{2(k+2)}k,\n\\]\nthe originally selected amplitudes satisfy \\(a_T\\simeq r_{i,s}b_T\\), and\n\\[\nQ_{i,s}:=\\sum_T|T|a_T^qb_T^{2-q}\n=\\Lambda^{o(1)}D_i,\n\\]\n\\[\nE_{i,s}:=\\sum_T|T|a_T^2\n=\\Lambda^{o(1)}D_ir_{i,s}^{2-q},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{T\\text{ active}}b_T^2\n\\leq\\Lambda^{o(1)}|Y|/M_{i,s}.\n\\]\nAn active cell has at most \\(\\Lambda^{o(1)}M_{i,s}\\) eligible witnesses in its prescribed subpower enlargement.</p><p>All witnesses are eligible in the power case and at strict transition ancestors. Within a subpower transition bin \\(b\\), eligibility means\n\\[\ne_b(z)^2\\leq\\Lambda^{o(1)}M_c.\n\\]</p><p>More generally, actual outputs or later error summands in fixed masks with weighted \\(q\\)-budget at most \\(\\Lambda^{-\\varepsilon}D_i\\), for fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), have negligible root contribution.</p>", "proof": "<p>First fix a small tolerance \\(\\beta_0&gt;0\\) bounding fixed-grid constants, norm losses, logarithmic sums, and balance errors. Choose distance, eligibility/enlargement, and occupancy tolerances successively larger than the fixed comparison multiples of their predecessors; send all to zero along a slow diagonal.</p><p>For an actual error \\(U\\) of budget \\(Q_U\\), Propositions 3 and 5 give in the power case\n\\[\n\\#\\{z\\in Y:|U(z)|&gt;tA_i\\}\n\\leq\\Lambda^{C\\beta_0}t^{-q}A_i^{-q}Q_U.\n\\]\nOne may jump directly from the current layer to the root, retaining already fixed transition restrictions. In the subpower case propagate to the gap edge, sort fresh amplitude ratios, and apply Proposition 6 across the gap. Its exponent slack contributes only \\(S^{C\\eta}\\). Choose \\(\\eta\\) after the desired saving. This proves the discard assertion without assuming saturation.</p><p>Assemble from leaves to root with original transition masks fixed. Cauchy bounds nonnegligible ratios by fixed powers of \\(\\Lambda\\); very small ratios are harmless. Select among \\(O(\\log\\Lambda)\\) dyadic classes at each of finitely many stages.</p><p>If \\(n_T\\) counts eligible witnesses, local weight comparison and clipped smoothing give\n\\[\n\\sum_Tb_T^2n_T\\leq\\Lambda^{\\beta'}|Y|.\n\\]\nDelete cells with \\(n_T&gt;\\Lambda^\\gamma M_{i,s}\\), choosing \\(\\gamma&gt;\\beta'+C\\beta_0\\). Their charge satisfies\n\\[\nQ_{\\mathrm{bad}}\n\\lesssim\nr_{i,s}^q\\kappa_i(s)^{k+2}\\sum_{\\mathrm{bad}}b_T^2\n\\leq\\Lambda^{\\beta'-\\gamma+C\\beta_0}D_i.\n\\]\nDiscard them before final class selection. Supremum sizes realized only far from a center are handled by the same budget argument using the fixed excess decay margin.</p><p>Upper \\(Q_{i,s}\\)-bounds follow by transport. Lower bounds follow by applying the discard estimate to the whole selected field, which still reaches its root threshold. The formulas for \\(E_{i,s}\\) and total background follow algebraically. Taking the stated diagonal proves all subpower conclusions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "Profiles", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After subsequences and finer finite rational grids, there are nonnegative, nonincreasing, \\(k/2\\)-Lipschitz functions \\(f_i\\) such that \\( r_{i,s}=\\Lambda^{f_i(s)+o(1)},\\qquad f_i(e_i)=0. \\) In the power case …", "statement": "<p>After subsequences and finer finite rational grids, there are nonnegative, nonincreasing, \\(k/2\\)-Lipschitz functions \\(f_i\\) such that\n\\[\nr_{i,s}=\\Lambda^{f_i(s)+o(1)},\\qquad f_i(e_i)=0.\n\\]\nIn the power case,\n\\[\nf_i(0)=\\lim\\log_hA_i.\n\\]\nIn the subpower case,\n\\[\n0\\leq d_i(s):=f_i(s)+ks/2\\leq ke_i/2\n\\qquad(s\\leq0).\n\\]\nLater tests always use a fixed finite grid before the sequence limit.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(s&lt;s'\\), weighted Cauchy gives\n\\[\nr_{i,s}\\leq\\Lambda^{o(1)}\n(\\kappa_i(s')/\\kappa_i(s))^{k/2}r_{i,s'}.\n\\]\nIntegrated spatial \\(L^2\\) orthogonality gives\n\\[\nE_{i,s}\\leq C_{\\mathrm{grid}}E_{i,s'}.\n\\]\nTo retain masks, remove the outermost mask by its bounded sup norm, separate its immediate children by bounded Fourier overlap, and repeat in that order; do not commute all masks past a final fine decomposition. Polynomial spatial weights are retained using translated band-limited cutoffs. Since \\(q&gt;2\\), saturation implies\n\\[\nr_{i,s}\\geq\\Lambda^{-o(1)}r_{i,s'}.\n\\]\nThe fine endpoint ratio is one.</p><p>Root sampling and local constancy give\n\\[\n|Y|A_i^2\\lesssim\\Lambda^{o(1)}E_{i,s}.\n\\]\nTogether with saturation this bounds \\(r_{i,s}\\) above by \\(\\Lambda^{o(1)}A_i\\) in the power case. Root Cauchy gives the reverse endpoint comparison as \\(s\\downarrow0\\). In the subpower case it gives \\(f_i(s)+ks/2\\geq0\\), while the fine endpoint and Lipschitz bound give the upper bound.</p><p>A diagonal subsequence on rational parameters gives the continuous profiles. Grid constants are absorbed only after the grid is fixed; no slope-convergence rate is assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.5", "orig": "Restricted path law", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use a selected finite tree from Lemma 7.3, either initially or after a directional restriction that retains the root threshold and has a fresh weighted-norm budget at most \\(\\Lambda^{o(1)}D_i\\).", "statement": "<p>Use a selected finite tree from Lemma 7.3, either initially or after a directional restriction that retains the root threshold and has a fresh weighted-norm budget at most \\(\\Lambda^{o(1)}D_i\\).</p><p>Expand positive square propagation along surviving paths\n\\[\n\\pi=(T_0,\\ldots,T_\\ell),\\qquad\ns_\\gamma^2\\prod_{j=0}^{\\ell}k_j(z;\\pi),\n\\quad\\gamma=T_\\ell,\n\\]\nwhere ordinary edge factors are the sampled weights from Proposition 5 with fixed normalization and decay reserve. Sum paths with terminal direction \\(\\omega\\) to obtain \\(J_z(\\omega)\\), and put\n\\[\nW(z)=\\sum_\\omega J_z(\\omega).\n\\]\nReference cap squares \\(b_\\tau(z)^2\\) retain available descendant squares: full squares in the power case, proportionally clipped bins at strict subpower transition ancestors, and full finer squares only in eligible bins.</p><p>After removing distant paths and renormalizing, one may draw a uniform root \\(Z\\) from a subpower fraction of \\(Y\\), then paths with terminal directions \\(\\Omega_i\\), independently across fields conditional on \\(Z\\). Intermediate active cells are at subpower normalized distance from \\(Z\\).</p><p>At typical sampled labels,\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\tau\\mid Z)=\\Lambda^{o(1)}b_\\tau(Z)^2,\n\\]\nand, for the disjoint scale-\\(\\kappa_i(s)\\) lattice tilted by \\(\\Omega_i\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Pr(T_s(Z,\\Omega_i)\\mid\\Omega_i)}\n{\\Pr(Z\\mid\\Omega_i)}\n=\\Lambda^{o(1)}M_{i,s}.\n\\]\n“Typical” means outside probability tending to zero for every fixed finite test and prescribed positive exponent tolerance.</p>", "proof": "<p>The fresh weighted root integrand is\n\\[\n|V(z)|^qW(z)^{1-q/2}.\n\\]\nThe count of high points with \\(W\\leq\\Lambda^{-\\alpha}\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\Lambda^{o(1)-\\alpha(q-2)/2}D_iA_i^{-q}.\n\\]\nAcross the subpower gap use Proposition 6 with slack chosen smaller than this saving. Thus low surviving mass is exceptional; full-background convolution and eligibility give \\(W\\leq\\Lambda^{o(1)}\\).</p><p>Reserve a fixed extra polynomial power on every edge. A path having an intermediate center farther than \\(\\Lambda^\\alpha\\) has a comparably large normalized edge displacement. Dropping the reserve on that edge leaves a full-background convolution, so distant path mass is \\(o(W)\\). Remove it and normalize afresh.</p><p>For available leaf square weights \\(a_\\omega(z)\\), near-path comparisons give\n\\[\np_z(\\omega)=J_z(\\omega)/W(z)\\leq\\Lambda^{o(1)}a_\\omega(z).\n\\]\nSumming descendants proves the cap upper bound. At fixed \\(z\\), sampled caps with\n\\[\np_z(\\tau)&lt;\\Lambda^{-\\varepsilon}b_\\tau(z)^2\n\\]\nhave total probability at most \\(\\Lambda^{-\\varepsilon+o(1)}\\), proving the typical reverse comparison. This is not a uniform lower bound for every cap.</p><p>For a fixed terminal direction \\(\\omega\\) and tilted cell \\(C\\), put\n\\[\n\\mu(\\omega,C)=|Y|^{-1}\\sum_{z\\in Y\\cap C}p_z(\\omega).\n\\]\nThe occupancy ratio equals\n\\[\n|Y|\\mu(\\omega,C)/p_z(\\omega).\n\\]\nIts upper bound follows from the frozen eligible-occupancy deletion and reference-weight comparability in a subpower enlargement of \\(C\\).</p><p>For the lower bound, let \\(J_T^{\\mathrm{dom}}(\\omega)\\) be the original selected positive descendant-path charge from an active \\(s\\)-mask \\(T\\), before angular deletion, retaining transition clipping and center restrictions. Positive convolution gives\n\\[\n\\sum_\\omega J_T^{\\mathrm{dom}}(\\omega)\\lesssim C_{\\mathrm{grid}}b_T^2.\n\\]\nLet \\(a_{\\omega,C}\\) sum these charges over active masks whose subpower enlargements meet \\(C\\). Bounded normalized shear implies only subpower many such \\(C\\)'s for each \\((T,\\omega)\\), and\n\\[\np_z(\\omega)\\leq\\Lambda^{o(1)}a_{\\omega,C},\\qquad\n\\sum_{\\omega,C}a_{\\omega,C}\n\\leq\\Lambda^{o(1)}|Y|/M_{i,s}.\n\\]\nFibers with\n\\[\n\\mu(\\omega,C)\n&lt;|Y|^{-1}\\Lambda^{-\\varepsilon}M_{i,s}a_{\\omega,C}\n\\]\nhave probability at most \\(\\Lambda^{-\\varepsilon+o(1)}\\). All others give the desired lower bound. Bounded-overlap coverings and subpower distortions change sampled logarithms by \\(o(1)\\), using the same neighbor-mass argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.6", "orig": "Conditioning directions", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Inside a fixed cap \\(\\tau_l\\), let \\(C\\) be a physical cell over which every descendant velocity drifts by at most the endpoint width and whose time range is at most the endpoint duration.", "statement": "<p>Inside a fixed cap \\(\\tau_l\\), let \\(C\\) be a physical cell over which every descendant velocity drifts by at most the endpoint width and whose time range is at most the endpoint duration.</p><p>Adding an independently redrawn descendant direction to the conditioning changes logarithmic position information inside \\(C\\) by \\(o(1)\\) at a typical draw. The cell may depend on another conditionally independent direction. The statement holds for subpartitions and subpower distortions. In the power case it also holds for a direction from another field if that field's full weights obey the same drift bound on \\(C\\).</p><p>For finite labels, information is evaluated at the sampled value:\n\\[\nI_\\Lambda(A\\mid B)=-\\log_\\Lambda\\Pr(A\\mid B).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize endpoint square weights at the center of \\(C\\) within \\(\\tau_l\\) as a reference direction law, clipping whole transition bins proportionally at strict ancestors. The drift bound makes these weights comparable throughout \\(C\\).</p><p>Lemma 7.5 and path domination bound the conditional direction density relative to this reference by \\(\\Lambda^{o(1)}\\) at typical data; its averaged conditional density is at least \\(\\Lambda^{-o(1)}\\) at a typical sampled direction by the same reference-mass summation. Bayes' identity therefore gives a subpower likelihood ratio for position.</p><p>For the remaining one-sided comparison, if \\(p,p'\\) are realized probabilities before and after additional conditioning,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[p/p']\\leq1.\n\\]\nMarkov implies \\(p/p'\\leq\\Lambda^\\varepsilon\\) outside probability \\(\\Lambda^{-\\varepsilon}\\). Apply this to the finite chain of partitions and combine with the reference comparison.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.7", "orig": "Angular test and original broad witnesses", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix one field, a parameter \\(l\\) away from endpoints and the transition seam, and a finite mesh of \\(0<v\\leq u\\), with \\(l+Cu<e_i\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix one field, a parameter \\(l\\) away from endpoints and the transition seam, and a finite mesh of \\(0&lt;v\\leq u\\), with \\(l+Cu&lt;e_i\\). Suppose\n\\[\nf_i(l)-f_i(l+v)\\geq xv\n\\]\non this mesh, for some \\(x&gt;0\\).</p><p>For some \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) depending only on \\(x,k\\), restrict the frozen tree so that the new conditional direction law at typical \\((Z,\\tau_l)\\) assigns mass at most\n\\[\n\\Lambda^{-\\eta v+o(1)}\n\\]\nto every affine hyperplane strip of width \\(\\Lambda^{-v}\\) in native \\(\\tau_l\\)-frequency coordinates.</p><p>At an arbitrarily small mesh precision \\(v_0&gt;0\\), sampled active \\(l\\)-cells may also have original, pre-modification broad witnesses: \\(k+1\\) cap groups at \\(l+v_0\\), transverse throughout their supports with determinant at least \\(\\Lambda^{-C_kv_0}\\), each of magnitude at least\n\\[\n\\Lambda^{-C_kv_0}r_{i,l}b_{\\tau_l}(Z)\n\\]\nwithin native distance \\(\\Lambda^{C_kv_0}\\) of the cell.</p><p>The root high set loses only a vanishing fraction. Lemmas 7.5 and 7.6 apply to the modified tree with the same recorded occupancy scales. The construction is for one fixed block at a time.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use native macro boxes of side \\(\\Lambda^{3u}\\). Endpoint descendant weights, normalized by the cap weight, vary boundedly on these boxes; clip whole transition bins proportionally when necessary.</p><p>For each mesh \\(v\\), greedily remove all \\((l+v)\\)-caps meeting enlarged hyperplane neighborhoods with reference mass at least \\(\\Lambda^{-2\\eta v}\\). There are \\(O(\\Lambda^{2\\eta v})\\) removals. Perform them coarse to fine.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nq'=\\frac{2(k+1)}{k-1}&gt;q.\n\\]\nThe recorded original ratio class gives\n\\[\nQ_{q',l+v}\\leq\\Lambda^{o(1)}r_{l+v}^{q'-q}D.\n\\]\nThe strip form of Propositions 3 and 5 transports each omitted hyperplane contribution. Summing removals and applying Hölder on active \\(l\\)-masks, with measure \\(b_T^2|T|\\), yields\n\\[\nQ_{q,l}(\\mathrm{error})\n\\leq\nD\\Lambda^{2\\eta qv+o(1)}\n(r_{l+v}/r_l)^{q(q'-q)/q'}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(2\\eta&lt;x(q'-q)/(2q')\\). This is a negative fixed-power budget, so Lemma 7.3 discards it at the root. During propagation use fresh actual amplitudes; charge restricted inputs to their original fine budgets.</p><p>For macro-dependent omissions, assign each \\(l\\)-packet by its center, modify before its Schwartz cutoff, and use summable macro-offset decay. Equivalently conjugate denominators by\n\\[\nb_T\\mapsto b_T\\Psi(z_T)^{-1/(p-2)}\n\\]\nfor \\(p=q,q'\\), as in Proposition 5. Fixed extra edge decay pays macro variation uniformly through the finite hierarchy. This proves the fresh restricted-tree norm bound needed by Lemma 7.5.</p><p>For broad witnesses use smaller macros of side \\(\\Lambda^{3v_0}\\) before directional modifications. Mark groups whose original contribution has sufficiently large supremum. Unmarked groups cannot supply an active-cell witness. A maximal-simplex test either produces \\(k+1\\) groups with successive affine widths \\(\\gtrsim\\Lambda^{-v_0}\\), or puts all marked centers in an enlarged hyperplane strip. The latter is cheap by the same \\(q'\\)-calculation, so nonbroad masks are discarded.</p><p>Reconstruct and renormalize the positive path law using Lemma 7.5. Its fresh norm estimate excludes low path mass; surviving reference weights and the typical reverse cap comparison give the angular bound. Broad witnesses remain records of original fields, not assertions that old amplitudes survive deletion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.8", "orig": "Ball inflation in a cap", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Work in one cap \\(\\tau_l\\) from Lemma 7.7.", "statement": "<p>Work in one cap \\(\\tau_l\\) from Lemma 7.7. Suppose, on a required finite mesh in \\([0,2u]\\),\n\\[\nf(l+v)-f(l)=-xv+O(\\varepsilon u).\n\\]\nPut \\(d_0=1-2x/k\\). For the isotropic radius-\\(\\Lambda^v\\) cell \\(Q_v\\) in native cap coordinates, define\n\\[\nN_v=\\Pr(Q_v(Z)\\mid\\tau_l)/\\Pr(Z\\mid\\tau_l).\n\\]\nFor mesh values \\(u\\leq v\\leq2u\\),\n\\[\n\\log_\\Lambda(N_v/M_l)\\leq(k+1)d_0v+O(\\varepsilon u).\n\\]\nThe error is made arbitrarily small by reducing \\(v_0/u\\), refining the finite mesh, and then taking the sequence limit. All tests remain on one side of the transition seam.</p>", "proof": "<p>A cap-conditioned point is near an active \\(l\\)-cell, whose eligible occupancy is at most \\(\\Lambda^{o(1)}M_l\\). Path domination and typical cap comparison reduce the assertion to counting active cells in an isotropic ball.</p><p>Use their original broad witnesses and pigeonhole the separated tuple, costing \\(\\Lambda^{O_k(v_0)}\\). For each broad group let \\(\\mathcal E_{j,s}\\) be its local square envelope of original selected amplitudes. At counted cells,\n\\[\nr_lb_{\\tau_l}(Z)\n\\lesssim\n\\Lambda^{O_k(v_0)+o(1)}\n\\prod_{j=1}^{k+1}\\mathcal E_{j,v_0}^{1/(2(k+1))}.\n\\]\nFor relative inverse widths \\(\\kappa_s=\\Lambda^s\\), \\(s&lt;t\\leq2s\\), Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\n\\frac1{|B_{\\kappa_t}|}\\int_{B_{\\kappa_t}}\n\\prod_{j=1}^{k+1}\\mathcal E_{j,s}^{1/k}\n\\lesssim\n\\Lambda^{O_k(v_0)}\n\\prod_{j=1}^{k+1}\n\\left(\\frac1{|CB_{\\kappa_t}|}\n\\int_{CB_{\\kappa_t}}\\mathcal E_{j,s}\\right)^{1/k}.\n\\]\nThe scale-\\(s\\) envelopes are positive weighted sums on tubes of width \\(\\kappa_s\\) and duration \\(\\kappa_s^2\\geq\\kappa_t\\), so they may be replaced on the ball by crossing columns.</p><p>Each last average is bounded by \\(\\mathcal E_{j,t}\\), without an aspect-ratio loss. Reproduce the coarse local suprema, average spatially at width \\(T_0=\\kappa_t\\), remove each bounded mask before separating its immediate child caps, and continue to scale \\(t\\). A coarse displacement has form\n\\[\n(S_0U+2v_sS_0^2w,S_0^2w),\n\\]\nwith integrable polynomial density, \\(S_0=\\kappa_s\\). In a final-cap frame its normalized shift is\n\\[\n((S_0/T_0)(U+O(1)w),(S_0/T_0)^2w).\n\\]\nLemma 5.1 preserves the final envelope order under this contraction.</p><p>At the terminal scale,\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{j,v}\\lesssim\n\\Lambda^{o(1)}r_{l+v}^2b_{\\tau_l}(Z)^2.\n\\]\nIterate along a fixed chain from \\(v_0\\) to \\(v\\). The cell count is at most\n\\[\n\\Lambda^{(k+1)v+O_k(v_0\\log(v/v_0))+o(1)}\n(r_{l+v}/r_l)^{p_k}.\n\\]\nSince \\(p_kx=(k+1)(1-d_0)\\), the conclusion follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.9", "orig": "Binary slope rigidity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every profile from Lemma 7.4 satisfies \\( f_i'(s)\\in\\{0,-k/2\\} \\qquad\\text{for almost every }s. \\)", "statement": "<p>Every profile from Lemma 7.4 satisfies\n\\[\nf_i'(s)\\in\\{0,-k/2\\}\n\\qquad\\text{for almost every }s.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose a differentiability point away from the seam has slope \\(-x\\), \\(0&lt;x&lt;k/2\\). Take a short rational block and finite mesh with\n\\[\nf(l+v)-f(l)=-xv+O(\\varepsilon u).\n\\]\nSet \\(d_0=1-2x/k\\in(0,1)\\), and apply Lemma 7.7 with broad precision \\(v_0\\ll u\\).</p><p>Condition on \\(\\tau_l,Q_{2u}\\), normalize the parent isotropically, and put \\(\\Delta=\\Lambda^{-u}\\). Fine isotropic-cell information is\n\\[\n\\log_\\Lambda(N_{2u}/N_u)+o(1);\n\\]\nprojection along a drawn direction has information\n\\[\n\\log_\\Lambda(N_{2u}/M_{l+u})+o(1).\n\\]\nThe projected fibers are clipped tubes of width \\(\\Lambda^u\\) and length \\(\\Lambda^{2u}\\). Lemmas 7.5–7.6 justify these formulas and the addition of independent directions.</p><p>Draw \\(k+1\\) directions independently under cap conditioning. Angular nonconcentration makes their lifted vectors a basis with inverse norm \\(\\Lambda^{O(\\varepsilon u)}\\) with probability tending to one. Chain rule and limiting conditioning give the finite-partition Loomis–Whitney comparison\n\\[\n\\log_\\Lambda(N_{2u}/M_{l+u})\n\\geq\\frac{k}{k+1}\\log_\\Lambda(N_{2u}/N_u)-O(\\varepsilon u).\n\\]\nIts sampled form uses Markov on finitely many likelihood ratios.</p><p>Let \\(g(v)=\\log_\\Lambda(N_v/M_l)\\). Since\n\\[\n\\log_\\Lambda(M_{l+u}/M_l)=(k+2)d_0u+O(\\varepsilon u),\n\\]\nthe comparison becomes\n\\[\ng(2u)+kg(u)\\geq(k+1)(k+2)d_0u-O(\\varepsilon u).\n\\]\nLemma 7.8 gives upper bounds whose weighted sum is exactly this right-hand main term. Both bounds are therefore saturated.</p><p>Extract one \\(\\Delta\\)-separated set \\(A\\) of typical fine isotropic cells in a fixed successful cap and parent. Its cardinality is\n\\[\n\\Delta^{-\\alpha+O(\\varepsilon)}\n\\leq|A|\\leq\\Delta^{-\\alpha-O(\\varepsilon)},\n\\qquad \\alpha=(k+1)d_0.\n\\]\nCoarse mesh instances of ball inflation, divided by the saturated parent occupancy, give the required set nonconcentration.</p><p>Restrict first to successful positions, then to directions with conditional success bounded below. Both retain fixed mass. For each retained direction its successful positions occupy a sufficiently large subset of this same \\(A\\), but project to at most\n\\[\n\\Delta^{-k\\alpha/(k+1)-O(\\varepsilon)}\n\\]\ncells. The angular test is exactly Schubert nonconcentration for lifted directions \\((2\\xi,1)\\). Proposition 2 supplies a fixed positive gain for every sufficiently large subset for some retained direction, contradicting that upper bound once mesh and error tolerances are small.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.10", "orig": "Midpoint closure", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Call differentiability points with derivative \\(-k/2\\) full-slope points and those with derivative zero zero-slope points.", "statement": "<p>Call differentiability points with derivative \\(-k/2\\) full-slope points and those with derivative zero zero-slope points.</p><p>For almost every pair \\(l&lt;a\\) of full-slope points in one field, \\((l+a)/2\\) is full slope. Hence each full-slope set is an interval modulo null sets.</p><p>In the power case, a full-slope point \\(a\\) in one field forces \\(a/2\\) to be full slope in every other field, for almost every such \\(a\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take differentiability parameters avoiding the seam, put \\(b=(l+a)/2\\), and choose\n\\[\n0&lt;\\eta\\ll\\sigma\\ll a-l.\n\\]\nLemma 7.7 gives two independently drawn directions inside \\(\\tau_l\\) with\n\\[\n\\kappa_i(l)^{-1}\\Lambda^{-\\eta}\n\\lesssim|\\Delta v|\\lesssim\\kappa_i(l)^{-1}.\n\\]\nInside the second direction's parent tube at \\(b+\\sigma/2\\), adding the first direction costs no logarithmic information by Lemma 7.6. The relevant identity is\n\\[\n\\kappa_i(b)^2=\\kappa_i(l)\\kappa_i(a).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(b\\) were zero slope, its occupancy increment across \\(b\\pm\\sigma/2\\) would be \\((k+2)\\sigma+o(\\sigma)\\). Spatial labels account for at most \\(k\\sigma+o(\\sigma)\\), leaving time information at least \\(2\\sigma+o(\\sigma)\\).</p><p>But the first direction's intercept at \\(a-\\sigma\\) determines this time label to accuracy\n\\[\nC\\frac{\\kappa_i(a-\\sigma)}{|\\Delta v|}\n\\leq C\\kappa_i(b-\\sigma/2)^2\\Lambda^\\eta.\n\\]\nOnly \\(\\Lambda^{\\eta+o(1)}\\) smaller time labels remain. The second parent meets boundedly many first parents at \\(a+\\sigma\\). Full slope at \\(a\\) makes the first-path information increment \\(o(\\sigma)\\), so the chain rule bounds time information by \\(\\eta+o(\\sigma)\\), a contradiction.</p><p>For different fields in the power case use \\(l=0\\); root transversality gives \\(|\\Delta v|\\geq\\Lambda^{-o(1)}\\), and the other field's full-weight conditioning satisfies the same geometry.</p><p>Finally, for a measurable set obeying midpoint closure almost everywhere, convolution of high-density pieces around two density points is positive near their midpoint. Fubini makes a neighborhood of that midpoint belong to the set modulo null sets. Taking equal density points gives openness modulo null sets; repeated midpoints and openness fill the interval between any two density points.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.11", "orig": "Cross-field one-step inflation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(K\\leq R\\leq K^2\\) be two inverse widths in a fixed finite packet tree.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(K\\leq R\\leq K^2\\) be two inverse widths in a fixed finite packet tree. At scale \\(K\\), let \\(W_j\\), \\(1\\leq j\\leq m=k+1\\), be the original selected fields testing the root high set, with no outer smaller-inverse-width mask. Each is assembled from exactly its selected scale-\\(R\\) descendants through frozen bounded masks and \\(K\\)-localizers.</p><p>Suppose enlarged label sets are \\(\\delta\\)-transverse. Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{j,K}=\\sum_Ta_{j,T}^2w_T\n\\]\nand let \\(\\mathcal E_{j,R}\\) be the descendant square envelopes in the same tree. Let \\(Y\\) have separate witnesses at thresholds \\(A_j\\). Assume on every \\(K\\)-ball meeting \\(Y\\),\n\\[\nK^{k/2}\\mathcal E_{j,K}(z)^{1/2}/A_j\\leq U,\\qquad U\\geq1,\n\\]\nat one, hence every, point, up to fixed comparisons.</p><p>For each fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), every root-centered \\(R\\)-ball satisfies\n\\[\n\\#(Y\\cap B_R(z))\n\\lesssim_{\\varepsilon,\\mathcal G}\n\\delta^{-C_\\varepsilon}U^\\varepsilon R^{k+1}\n\\prod_{j=1}^{m}\n(\\mathcal E_{j,R}(z)/A_j^2)^{1/k}.\n\\]\nThe constant may depend on the finite grid \\(\\mathcal G\\), not on \\(R/K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Cover by \\(K\\)-balls. Proposition 4 at \\(p=p_k+\\varepsilon\\) bounds the high count in each by\n\\[\nC_\\varepsilon\\delta^{-C_\\varepsilon}K^{k+1}\n\\prod_j(\\mathcal E_{j,K}/A_j^2)^{1/k}\n\\left[K^{k/2}\\prod_j(\\mathcal E_{j,K}/A_j^2)^{1/(2m)}\\right]^\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nThe bracket is at most \\(U\\). Sum the balls and replace the sum by an integral using envelope comparability.</p><p>Proposition 1 gives the local weighted cylinder inequality\n\\[\n\\frac1{|B_R|}\\int_{B_R}\\prod_j\\mathcal E_{j,K}^{1/k}\n\\lesssim\n\\delta^{-C}\\prod_j\n\\left(\\frac1{|CB_R|}\\int_{CB_R}\\mathcal E_{j,K}\\right)^{1/k}.\n\\]\nColumns have radius \\(K\\) and duration at least \\(R\\). The normalization is exact: the Kakeya factor \\(K^{k+1}\\) and the \\(m\\) average factors \\((R/K)^{k+1}\\) produce \\(R^{k+1}\\).</p><p>The ordered mask-removal and spatial-\\(L^2\\) argument in Lemma 7.8 gives\n\\[\n|CB_R(z)|^{-1}\\int_{CB_R(z)}\\mathcal E_{j,K}\n\\lesssim_{\\mathcal G}\\mathcal E_{j,R}(z),\n\\]\nwith contracted normalized shifts and unchanged envelope order. Combine the estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.12", "orig": "Vanishing left limit", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the subpower case, \\( \\lim_{s\\to-\\infty}d_i(s)=0 \\qquad\\text{for every }i. \\) Consequently, \\( f_i(0)=0, \\) including when \\(e_i=0\\).", "statement": "<p>In the subpower case,\n\\[\n\\lim_{s\\to-\\infty}d_i(s)=0\n\\qquad\\text{for every }i.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nf_i(0)=0,\n\\]\nincluding when \\(e_i=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Retain path attachments to original surviving \\(H_i\\)-masks. Fix \\(H=H_i\\), \\(K=HS^s\\). Clipped cap weights and path domination give\n\\[\n\\mu_z(B(\\omega,r))\\lesssim S^{C+o(1)}r^k\n\\qquad(r\\geq K^{-1}).\n\\]\nA maximal-bad-dyadic-cell argument also gives, at typical sampled directions and all relevant radii,\n\\[\n\\mu_z(B(\\omega,r))\\gtrsim S^{-1}r^k.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(y\\in Y\\), \\(\\tau=|t_y-t_z|\\), enlarged \\(K\\)-tube co-incidence has probability at most\n\\[\nS^{C+o(1)}(K/\\tau)^k.\n\\]\nIf this event is nonempty, directions within \\(c\\min(1,H/\\tau)\\) of a realizing typical direction attach to enlarged original \\(H\\)-masks containing \\(y\\). The original draw therefore gives that event probability at least\n\\[\nS^{-1-o(1)}\\min(1,H/\\tau)^k.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\tau\\geq KS^{C_4}\\), compare these bounds and sum once over \\(y\\). Original-mask occupancy is at most \\(S^{C_1}\\), so expected far occupancy is at most\n\\[\nS^{C+C_1+1+o(1)}\n(S^{-kC_4}+S^{ks})=o(1)\n\\]\nfor large fixed \\(C_4\\) and \\(s&lt;-2C_4\\).</p><p>Lemma 7.5 turns effective occupancy into ordinary count up to subpowers. Thus a ball of radius \\(O(KS^{C_4})\\) contains at least\n\\[\nM_{i,s}S^{-o(1)}=S^{qd_i(s)-o(1)}\n\\]\nroot points; the identity uses \\(kq/2=k+2\\).</p><p>For the upper count take \\(s'=s+C_4+1&lt;0\\), insert common widths within subpower factors across fields, and start from a first gap-edge parameter \\(s_{\\min}\\leq s\\), with no earlier masks. Clipped squares give \\(U\\leq S^C\\). Because \\(H_i\\) grows faster than every fixed power of \\(S\\),\n\\[\n\\kappa(s_{\\min})^2/\\kappa(s')\\to\\infty,\n\\]\nso Lemma 7.11 applies once. At the terminal root point,\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{j,s'}\\leq S^{o(1)}r_{j,s'}^2.\n\\]\nIt follows, after sending its fixed \\(\\varepsilon\\) to zero, that\n\\[\nqd_i(s)\\leq\\frac{p_k}{k+1}\\sum_jd_j(s+C_4+1).\n\\]\nEach \\(d_i\\) is bounded, nonnegative, and nondecreasing. Let \\(L_i=\\lim_{s\\to-\\infty}d_i(s)\\). Summing the limiting inequalities gives\n\\[\n(q-p_k)\\sum_iL_i\\leq0.\n\\]\nThus every \\(L_i=0\\).</p><p>By Lemmas 7.9–7.10, the full-slope set is an interval. It must extend to \\(-\\infty\\), or \\(d_i\\) would become negative. There \\(d_i'=0\\), so \\(d_i=0\\). After its right endpoint \\(f_i\\) is constant and its fine endpoint makes that constant zero. Continuity places the transition at \\(s=0\\), or gives \\(e_i=0\\) if the full-slope interval fills the domain. Hence \\(f_i(0)=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Weighted fractal norm and occupancy gain", "depth": 0, "snippet": "From now on fix spatial dimension \\(n\\geq3\\), and put \\( \\beta=\\frac2{n+1},\\qquad e=\\frac{n^2}{n+1},\\qquad \\gamma_n=\\frac{2n}{(n+1)(n+2)}. \\) For bounded-frequency \\(g\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^n)\\), define …", "statement": "<p>From now on fix spatial dimension \\(n\\geq3\\), and put\n\\[\n\\beta=\\frac2{n+1},\\qquad\ne=\\frac{n^2}{n+1},\\qquad\n\\gamma_n=\\frac{2n}{(n+1)(n+2)}.\n\\]\nFor bounded-frequency \\(g\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^n)\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathcal Eg(x,t)=(2\\pi)^{-n}\n\\int e^{ix\\cdot\\xi-it|\\xi|^2}g(\\xi)\\,d\\xi.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mu\\) be supported in a ball of radius \\(CS\\), \\(S\\geq1\\), and satisfy\n\\[\n\\mu(B(z,r))\\leq mr^n\\qquad(r\\geq1).\n\\]\nAssume also\n\\[\n\\mu(B(z,\\sqrt S))\\leq dmS^{n/2},\n\\qquad S^{-A_0}\\leq d\\leq1.\n\\]\nFor half-open unit cubes \\(q\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_q\\mu(q)^\\beta\\sup_q|\\mathcal Eg|^2\n\\leq C_{\\varepsilon,A_0}\nm^\\beta S^{n/(n+1)+\\varepsilon}d^{\\gamma_n}\\|g\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(d=1\\), growth alone suffices.</p><p>The compact-array conventions used subsequently are those of original Definition 4.1:\n\\[\nU(x,t)=\\sum_\\alpha\nc_\\alpha L^{-n/2}e^{i(x\\cdot\\omega_\\alpha-t|\\omega_\\alpha|^2)}\n\\varphi_\\alpha\\left(\\frac{x-y_\\alpha-2t\\omega_\\alpha}{L},\n\\frac{t}{L^2}\\right).\n\\]\nProfiles have fixed compact native support and a sufficiently large finite uniform derivative bound; labels \\((L\\omega_\\alpha,y_\\alpha/L)\\) have bounded unit phase-space multiplicity; frequencies lie in a fixed bounded box. The coefficient energy is\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(U)=\\sum_\\alpha|c_\\alpha|^2.\n\\]\nAt scale \\(L\\), the measure has a fixed native time range and, when specified, tube moments\n\\[\n\\int\\left(1+\\frac{|x-y_\\alpha-2\\omega_\\alpha t|}{L}\\right)^{-M}\\,d\\mu\n\\leq\\frac{mL^{n+1}}D.\n\\]\nSubarrays retain original profiles and labels. One has\n\\[\n\\|U\\|_\\infty\\lesssim\\mathcal E(U)^{1/2},\n\\]\nand growth implies the moment condition with \\(D=1\\), after fixed inflation of \\(m\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize \\(m=\\|g\\|_2=1\\). Sort cubes by \\(a&lt;\\mu(q)\\leq2a\\). This class has counting parameter \\(Ca^{-1}\\) and square-root occupancy \\(CdS^{n/2}a^{-1}\\). Lemma 8.1, multiplied by \\((2a)^\\beta\\), gives\n\\[\nC_\\varepsilon S^{n/(n+1)+\\varepsilon}d^{\\gamma_n},\n\\]\nbecause\n\\[\n\\beta-\\frac4{(n+1)(n+2)}-\\gamma_n=0,\n\\qquad\n(1+n/2)\\gamma_n=\\frac n{n+1}.\n\\]\nThere is no positive \\(a\\)-power to sum. Since bounded frequency gives \\(\\|\\mathcal Eg\\|_\\infty\\lesssim1\\) and only \\(O(S^{n+1})\\) cubes are relevant, cubes of mass at most \\(S^{-A}\\) contribute at most \\(CS^{n+1-A\\beta}\\). Choose \\(A\\) in terms of \\(A_0\\) and an error power. The remaining \\(O_A(1+\\log S)\\) mass classes are absorbed into the requested loss. Restore homogeneity.</p><p>For the array conventions, only \\(O(L^n)\\) labels can meet a point, so coefficient Cauchy cancels \\(L^{-n/2}\\). To obtain the \\(D=1\\) moment, cover the bounded-time tube annulus of transverse radius \\(r=2^aL\\) by \\(C(1+L^2/r)\\) radius-\\(Cr\\) balls. Its mass is at most\n\\[\nCm(r^n+L^2r^{n-1}),\n\\]\nand the \\(2^{-aM}\\)-sum is \\(O(mL^{n+1})\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Du–Zhang input in squared form", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\mathcal Q\\) be distinct unit lattice cubes in a radius-\\(S\\) ball.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal Q\\) be distinct unit lattice cubes in a radius-\\(S\\) ball. Suppose\n\\[\n\\#\\{q\\in\\mathcal Q:q\\cap B(z,r)\\neq\\varnothing\\}\\leq\\Gamma r^n\n\\qquad(r\\geq1),\n\\]\nand at most \\(\\lambda_0\\) cubes occur in every radius-\\(\\sqrt S\\) ball. Then\n\\[\n\\sum_{q\\in\\mathcal Q}\\sup_q|\\mathcal Eg|^2\n\\leq\nC_\\varepsilon S^\\varepsilon\n\\Gamma^{4/((n+1)(n+2))}\n\\lambda_0^{\\gamma_n}S^{\\gamma_n}\\|g\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nFixed enlargements and translated lattices are allowed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Du–Zhang [12, Theorem 1.6], with counting exponent \\(n\\), states on a union \\(X\\) whose occupied square-root parents have comparable occupancy \\(\\lambda\\),\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal Eg\\|_{L^2(X)}\n\\leq\nC_\\varepsilon\n\\Gamma^{2/((n+1)(n+2))}\n\\lambda^{n/((n+1)(n+2))}\nS^{n/((n+1)(n+2))+\\varepsilon}\\|g\\|_2.\n\\]\nA fixed parabolic dilation and bounded coverings handle the fixed frequency box and translated balls. Square and rename the loss.</p><p>Group whole square-root parents by dyadic occupancy. Each occupancy is at most \\(C\\lambda_0\\); the geometric sum of occupancy powers is bounded by \\(C\\lambda_0^{\\gamma_n}\\), removing equal occupancy.</p><p>For suprema reproduce \\(\\mathcal Eg\\) with a Schwartz kernel equal to one on the compact paraboloid patch. Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\sup_q|\\mathcal Eg|^2\n\\leq C_A\\int(1+|h|)^{-A}\\int_q|\\mathcal Eg(z+h)|^2\\,dz\\,dh.\n\\]\nFor each \\(h\\), translation modulates the input without changing norm or support. Apply the integral bound on the same cube collection and integrate \\(h\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Power-loss norm for compact arrays", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(U\\) be a compact array of scale \\(L\\) with the conventions of Proposition 8, and let \\(\\mu\\) satisfy \\( \\mu(B(z,r))\\leq mr^n\\qquad(r\\geq1) \\) in a spacetime box of side \\(O(L^2)\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(U\\) be a compact array of scale \\(L\\) with the conventions of Proposition 8, and let \\(\\mu\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\mu(B(z,r))\\leq mr^n\\qquad(r\\geq1)\n\\]\nin a spacetime box of side \\(O(L^2)\\). For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), with sufficiently many fixed profile derivatives,\n\\[\n\\sum_q\\mu(q)^\\beta\\sup_q|U|^2\n\\leq C_\\varepsilon m^\\beta L^{2n/(n+1)+\\varepsilon}\\mathcal E(U).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 9.1, discarding \\(|k|&gt;L^\\eta\\), \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\), with a sufficiently negative power. Retained layer supports lie in a fixed bounded box.</p><p>Apply Proposition 8 with \\(S=L^2,d=1\\) to every normalized layer. Minkowski in the weighted \\(\\ell^2\\) norm of unit-cube suprema, the subarray input norm bound, and\n\\[\n\\sum_k\\int\\rho(k,u)\\,du&lt;\\infty\n\\]\ngive the conclusion. The exterior time modulation has modulus one. The discarded error is negligible because masses, cube counts, and the domain are polynomially bounded. Rename the loss after \\(S=L^2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Exact Fourier layers of compact profiles", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For prescribed finite derivative and native spatial-decay orders, sufficiently high finite profile regularity gives \\( U(x,t)=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^n}\\int_{\\mathbb R} \\rho(k,u)e^{iut/L^2}\\mathcal Eg_{k,u}(x,t)\\,du, \\) …", "statement": "<p>For prescribed finite derivative and native spatial-decay orders, sufficiently high finite profile regularity gives\n\\[\nU(x,t)=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^n}\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n\\rho(k,u)e^{iut/L^2}\\mathcal Eg_{k,u}(x,t)\\,du,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\rho(k,u)\\leq C_A(1+|k|+|u|)^{-A}\\) for any prescribed \\(A\\).</p><p>For every subarray \\(\\mathcal A\\),\n\\[\n\\|g_{k,u}^{\\mathcal A}\\|_2^2\n\\leq C\\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\mathcal A}|c_\\alpha|^2.\n\\]\nA label's exact input is supported within \\(O(L^{-1})\\) of \\(\\omega_\\alpha+k/L\\), with uniform native-frequency seminorms. On a fixed native-time range its extension has the prescribed spatial decay about the original trajectory after extracting \\(\\rho\\). The shift and time modulation are common to the layer and every subarray.</p><p>For fixed \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), layers \\(|k|&gt;L^\\eta\\) have arbitrarily negative-power supremum error, and hence are negligible in polynomial-size, polynomial-mass tests.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fourier-transform \\(\\varphi_\\alpha\\) in native variables \\((\\eta,\\zeta)\\), partition \\(\\eta\\) into bounded boxes about \\(k\\), and change variables\n\\[\nu=\\zeta+|\\eta|^2.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\((k,u)\\), define\n\\[\ng_{\\alpha,k,u}(\\omega_\\alpha+\\eta/L)\n=\nCc_\\alpha L^{n/2}e^{-iy_\\alpha\\cdot\\eta/L}\n\\vartheta_k(\\eta)\\widehat\\varphi_\\alpha(\\eta,u-|\\eta|^2).\n\\]\nFourier inversion gives the identity. Compact support and finitely many derivatives supply any prescribed polynomial Fourier decay and frequency seminorms. Extract the common \\(\\rho\\).</p><p>Input bumps interact only when \\(|L(\\omega_\\alpha-\\omega_{\\alpha'})|\\leq C\\). Integration by parts gives prescribed decay in \\(|y_\\alpha-y_{\\alpha'}|/L\\). Phase-space counting bounds Gram row and column sums; Schur proves the uniform subarray norm bound.</p><p>For bounded native time, integrate by parts in the extension phase \\(z\\cdot\\eta-\\tau|\\eta|^2\\). Decay about the original trajectory costs a fixed polynomial in \\(k\\), already included in \\(\\rho\\). Cauchy over labels and rapid spatial decay bound normalized layers by a fixed polynomial in \\((k,u)\\mathcal E(U)^{1/2}\\). Increasing \\(A\\) proves the tail assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Exact packet localization on a time slab", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(T\\geq1\\), and let \\(g\\) have bounded frequency support.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(T\\geq1\\), and let \\(g\\) have bounded frequency support. On a time interval of length \\(O(T)\\), \\(\\mathcal Eg\\) can be localized to spatial boxes of side \\(T\\), with retained input energies summing to \\(C\\|g\\|_2^2\\).</p><p>Errors have arbitrary negative powers of \\(T\\), with summable local squared budgets. No number-of-spatial-boxes factor occurs.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(R=\\sqrt T\\) and absorb the initial time into \\(g\\). Partition frequency into \\(R^{-1}\\)-caps and Fourier-expand each rescaled density on a larger box. Exact packets are\n\\[\nc_{\\theta,m}R^{-n/2}e^{i(x\\cdot\\omega_\\theta-t|\\omega_\\theta|^2)}\nH_\\theta\\left(\\frac{x-\\gamma Rm-2t\\omega_\\theta}{R},\\frac t{R^2}\\right),\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\theta,m}|c_{\\theta,m}|^2\\leq C\\|g\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nParseval requires no derivatives of \\(g\\). The fixed-bump extensions and derivatives decay rapidly spatially on bounded native time. The Gram estimate also bounds any retained subinput by its coefficient energy.</p><p>For a side-\\(T\\) spatial box retain trajectories coming within \\(CT\\). A bounded-velocity trajectory of duration \\(O(T)\\) belongs to only boundedly many such boxes. Omitted packets have native distance at least \\(c\\sqrt T\\). Extract arbitrary decay and retain a further box-distance weight. Their squared supremum is bounded by\n\\[\nC_AT^{-A}\\sum_{\\theta,m}|c_{\\theta,m}|^2\n\\left(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{dist}(B,\\operatorname{trajectory}_{\\theta,m})}{T}\\right)^{-n-2}.\n\\]\nThese budgets sum boundedly over boxes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Codimension-two strip saving", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\(0<\\alpha<1/8\\), and choose \\( \\beta<b<2/n. \\) Suppose \\(g\\) has bounded frequency support within \\(O(S^{-\\alpha})\\) of an affine \\((n-2)\\)-plane.", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1/8\\), and choose\n\\[\n\\beta&lt;b&lt;2/n.\n\\]\nSuppose \\(g\\) has bounded frequency support within \\(O(S^{-\\alpha})\\) of an affine \\((n-2)\\)-plane. Let \\(\\mathcal Q\\) be unit cubes in a radius-\\(S\\) ball, with at most \\(m_0r^n\\) cubes in every radius-\\(r\\) ball, \\(r\\geq1\\).</p><p>There is \\(c_0&gt;0\\), depending only on the fixed dimensional choices and independent of \\(\\alpha\\), such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{q\\in\\mathcal Q}\\sup_q|\\mathcal Eg|^2\n\\leq\nC_{\\alpha,\\varepsilon}\nm_0^bS^{n/(n+1)-c_0\\alpha+\\varepsilon}\\|g\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nFinite disjoint subinputs are allowed, with their squared norms summed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a sufficiently large fixed integer \\(J\\) and dyadic \\(K\\) such that\n\\[\nH=K^J\\asymp S^\\alpha.\n\\]\nSince \\(b&lt;2/n\\), \\(P=2/(1-b)&lt;q_{n-2}\\). Assign unit mass to cube centers. Local constancy and Hölder bound the initial squared-supremum sum by \\(\\mathcal T_b\\), with a fixed \\(K^C\\) endpoint cost.</p><p>Iterate Lemmas 10.1–10.2 for \\(J\\) levels. Before the last step the rescaled strip width is at most \\(O(K^{-1})\\). The transport product is\n\\[\nH^{2-(n+2)b},\n\\]\nthe final growth parameter is \\(m'\\leq C_Jm_0H^{n+1}\\), and child squared norms sum to \\(C_J\\|g\\|_2^2\\). Choose decoupling losses sufficiently small.</p><p>Final time length is \\(T=S/H^2\\). The spatial domain can be larger than \\(T\\); Lemma 9.2 localizes it to side-\\(T\\) boxes with summable energy and error budgets.</p><p>On each such box, move every coarse-cube mass to a near-maximal unit witness and replace the field norm by its volume factor times the witness supremum. The moved measure has growth \\(CK^{2n}m'\\) and individual mass at most that amount. Since \\(b\\geq\\beta\\), compare its \\(b\\)-powers to its \\(\\beta\\)-powers and apply Proposition 8:\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_b(V',\\nu')\n\\leq C_\\varepsilon K^{C_*}\nT^{n/(n+1)+\\varepsilon}(m')^b\n\\|\\text{child input}\\|_2^2,\n\\]\nwith \\(C_*\\) independent of \\(J\\). This holds for each translated child measure; translations are absorbed into exact-input modulations, so there is no spatial-box or shift-count loss.</p><p>The net \\(H\\)-power is\n\\[\nH^{2-(n+2)b}H^{(n+1)b}H^{-2n/(n+1)}\n=H^{\\beta-b}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(J\\) so \\(C_*/J&lt;(b-\\beta)/2\\), and any \\(c_0&lt;(b-\\beta)/2\\). This yields the saving. Disjoint subinputs are charged by their summed energies.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Decoupling inside a strip", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For dyadic \\(K\\geq2\\), group \\(V=\\sum_\\theta V_\\theta\\) by frequency-label bins of side \\(K^{-1}\\).", "statement": "<p>For dyadic \\(K\\geq2\\), group \\(V=\\sum_\\theta V_\\theta\\) by frequency-label bins of side \\(K^{-1}\\). Suppose each support lies in a fixed bin enlargement and within vertical Fourier distance \\(O(K^{-2})\\) of the paraboloid.</p><p>If labels lie within \\(O(K^{-1})\\) of an affine \\(j\\)-plane, \\(1\\leq j\\leq n\\), and\n\\[\n2\\leq P\\leq q_j=\\frac{2(j+2)}j,\n\\]\nthen for side-\\(K^2\\) spacetime cubes \\(Q\\),\n\\[\n\\|V\\|_{L^P(Q)}^2\n\\leq C_{\\varepsilon,A}K^\\varepsilon\n\\sum_\\theta\\sum_{a\\in\\mathbb Z^{n+1}}\n\\langle a\\rangle^{-A}\n\\|V_\\theta\\|_{L^P(Q+K^2a)}^2.\n\\]\nThe subfields may be arbitrary prescribed fields with these supports. Without a strip, replace \\(C_\\varepsilon K^\\varepsilon\\) by \\(CK^n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Rotate into tangent and normal coordinates, remove the normal carrier, and shear the normal linear phase. Remaining normal frequencies are \\(O(K^{-1})\\), so their quadratic contribution is \\(O(K^{-2})\\). On each normal physical slice the tangential support lies near the positive \\(j\\)-paraboloid.</p><p>Multiply by a Schwartz cutoff positive on \\(Q\\), of Fourier width \\(O(K^{-2})\\), and apply Bourgain–Demeter decoupling. Smooth tangential projections have bounded \\(L^1\\) kernels; only boundedly many original bins meet each projected cap. Bounded overlap and Minkowski in normal variables give a Schwartz-weighted square-sum estimate. Decompose the weight into translates of \\(Q\\). Without the strip there are \\(O(K^n)\\) bins, so Cauchy–Schwarz suffices.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "Coarse norm transport", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(0<b<1\\), \\(P=2/(1-b)\\), define \\( \\mathcal T_b(V,\\nu)= \\sum_{Q\\in\\mathcal Q_{K^2}}\\nu(Q)^b\\|V\\|_{L^P(Q)}^2. \\) Let \\( F=X_{K,v}(x,t)=((x-2vt)/K,t/K^2) \\) with bounded \\(v\\), and …", "statement": "<p>For \\(0&lt;b&lt;1\\), \\(P=2/(1-b)\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_b(V,\\nu)=\n\\sum_{Q\\in\\mathcal Q_{K^2}}\\nu(Q)^b\\|V\\|_{L^P(Q)}^2.\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nF=X_{K,v}(x,t)=((x-2vt)/K,t/K^2)\n\\]\nwith bounded \\(v\\), and\n\\[\nV(z)=K^{-n/2}e^{i\\phi(z)}V'(Fz),\\qquad |e^{i\\phi}|=1.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(a\\in\\mathbb Z^{n+1}\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_Q\\nu(Q)^b\\|V\\|_{L^P(Q+K^2a)}^2\n\\leq\nCK^{2-(n+2)b}\\sum_{r,s\\in\\mathcal R}\n\\mathcal T_b(V',\\nu_{a,r,s}),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal R\\) is fixed finite and\n\\[\n\\nu_{a,r,s}=\n(\\tau_{F(K^2a)+K^2(s-r)})_\\#F_\\#\\nu.\n\\]\nThe conclusion holds for any parent-cube subset. Translations have size at most \\(CK^2(1+|a|)\\).</p><p>A growth parameter \\(m\\) becomes \\(CmK^{n+1}\\). Direct pushforward preserves the packet moment with the same \\(D\\), from scale \\(L\\) to \\(L/K\\). Translated measures preserve that moment up to a fixed factor only when their native displacements are bounded.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Jacobian gives, for \\(A_Q=F(Q+K^2a)\\),\n\\[\n\\|V\\|_{L^P(Q+K^2a)}^2\n=\nK^{2-(n+2)b}\\left(\\int_{A_Q}|V'|^P\\right)^{1-b}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\nu_a=(\\tau_{F(K^2a)})_\\#F_\\#\\nu\\); then \\(\\nu_a(A_Q)=\\nu(Q)\\). The disjoint \\(A_Q\\)'s have diameter \\(O(K)\\). Group them by the child \\(K^2\\)-cube containing their centers. Hölder within each group bounds its contribution by total mass to power \\(b\\) times the norm on a fixed enlargement. Subadditivity of \\(b\\) and \\(1-b\\) introduces separate neighbor indices \\(r,s\\); reindexing gives the stated measures.</p><p>The inverse image of a radius-\\(r\\) ball has transverse widths \\(O(Kr)\\) and length \\(O(K^2r)\\), covered by \\(O(K)\\) radius-\\(CKr\\) balls. For moments, with\n\\[\nL'=L/K,\\quad\\omega'=K(\\omega-v),\\quad y'=y/K,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\frac{x'-y'-2\\omega't'}{L'}\n=\\frac{x-y-2\\omega t}{L},\\qquad\n(mK^{n+1})(L/K)^{n+1}=mL^{n+1}.\n\\]\nArbitrary translations are harmless for growth, but moment applications truncate Schwartz shifts while child widths dominate their displacement; the discarded tail is paid by choosing the weight decay last.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Transverse saving", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\(n\\geq3\\) and a bounded frequency region.", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(n\\geq3\\) and a bounded frequency region. There are \\(c&gt;0,L_0&lt;\\infty\\) such that no configuration below exists for \\(L\\geq L_0\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal Q\\) be distinct unit cubes in a radius-\\(L^2\\) ball, with\n\\[\n\\#\\mathcal Q\\geq L^{2n-c},\\qquad\n\\#\\{q\\in\\mathcal Q:q\\cap B(z,r)\\neq\\varnothing\\}\n\\leq L^cr^n\\quad(r\\geq1).\n\\]\nLet \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_{n+1}\\) have norms at most one, support in the fixed frequency region, and\n\\[\n\\sup_q|\\mathcal Ef_i|\\geq L^{-e-c}\n\\qquad(q\\in\\mathcal Q,\\ 1\\leq i\\leq n+1).\n\\]\nIt is impossible that every support choice satisfies\n\\[\n|\\det((2\\xi_1,1),\\ldots,(2\\xi_{n+1},1))|\n\\geq L^{-c}.\n\\]</p><p>In the supporting lemmas, a countersequence means \\(c\\downarrow0\\), \\(L\\to\\infty\\); every \\(o(1)\\) is along such a sequence after fixed finite tests and subsequences.</p>", "proof": "<p>A countersequence yields the pruned incident model of Lemma 11.2. Lemmas 11.3–11.6 produce one common limiting information profile and the two angular marginals. Lemmas 11.7–11.10 force a full-to-empty block with arbitrarily small fixed tolerance. Lemma 11.11 forbids it. Thus no countersequence exists, giving fixed \\(c&gt;0\\) and a large-scale threshold.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Multilinear estimates with polynomial dependence", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(n+1\\) bounded-frequency exact inputs transverse with determinant at least \\(\\nu\\in(0,1]\\), and every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) …", "statement": "<p>For \\(n+1\\) bounded-frequency exact inputs transverse with determinant at least \\(\\nu\\in(0,1]\\), and every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{q\\subset B_S}\n\\prod_{i=1}^{n+1}(\\sup_q|\\mathcal Ef_i|)^{2/n}\n\\leq\nC_\\varepsilon\\nu^{-M_\\varepsilon}S^\\varepsilon\n\\prod_i\\|f_i\\|_2^{2/n}.\n\\]\nThe same holds with an integral.</p><p>For transverse width-\\(\\delta\\) tubes in a fixed bounded region and nonnegative weights,\n\\[\n\\int\\prod_{i=1}^{n+1}\n\\left(\\sum_Tw_i(T)1_T\\right)^{1/n}\n\\leq\nC_\\varepsilon\\nu^{-M_\\varepsilon}\\delta^{n+1-\\varepsilon}\n\\prod_i\\left(\\sum_Tw_i(T)\\right)^{1/n}.\n\\]\nFixed enlargements are permitted; subpower enlargements and subpower inverse determinants cost only subpowers.</p>", "proof": "<p>Subdivide directions into neighborhoods of polynomially small radius in \\(\\nu\\). There are \\(\\nu^{-O_n(1)}\\) tuples. Send representative directions to coordinate axes; the map and inverse have polynomial \\(\\nu^{-1}\\) bounds. Multilinear Kakeya [2,16] proves the tube estimate.</p><p>For restriction, let \\(A_B(S,\\nu)\\) be the normalized integral constant. Exact packet localization on \\(\\sqrt S\\)-boxes, with \\(S^\\theta\\sqrt S\\)-padding and negligible tails, followed by Kakeya gives\n\\[\nA_B(S,\\nu)\n\\leq C\\nu^{-M_\\sigma}S^a\nA_{B+1}(C_B\\sqrt S,\\nu/2)+O_N(S^{-N}),\n\\quad a=(n+1)\\theta+\\sigma/2.\n\\]\nUse subadditivity for product exponent \\(2/n\\leq1\\). Choose a fixed number \\(J\\) with \\((n+1)2^{-J}&lt;\\varepsilon/2\\), then \\(2a&lt;\\varepsilon/2\\). Iterate \\(J\\) times and use the terminal trivial bound. If the scale is too small to preserve transversality at every step, the trivial bound is absorbed by a larger \\(\\nu^{-M_\\varepsilon}\\).</p><p>For suprema, a Peetre weighted-maximal argument works also for exponent \\(2/n&lt;1\\): reproduction and\n\\[\nM_AF(x)=\\sup_y|F(x-y)|(1+|y|)^{-A}\n\\]\ngive a Schwartz-average bound for \\(M_AF(x)^{2/n}\\). Apply it separately to factors, integrate the product over cubes, and use the integral estimate for independently translated exact fields. Input norms and supports are unchanged.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Pruned incident-tube model", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A countersequence to Proposition 11 produces a set \\(\\mathcal P\\) of centers of distinct \\(L^{-1}\\)-cubes in a bounded region with …", "statement": "<p>A countersequence to Proposition 11 produces a set \\(\\mathcal P\\) of centers of distinct \\(L^{-1}\\)-cubes in a bounded region with\n\\[\n\\#\\mathcal P=L^{n+o(1)},\\qquad\n\\#(\\mathcal P\\cap B(z,r))\n\\leq L^{n+o(1)}r^n\n\\quad(L^{-1}\\leq r\\leq1),\n\\]\nand \\(n+1\\) transverse weighted tube families of width \\(L^{-1+o(1)}\\), with\n\\[\n\\sum_Tw_i(T)\\lesssim1.\n\\]\nAt every \\(x\\in\\mathcal P\\), a retained incident subfamily has weight\n\\[\nw_i^*(x)=L^{-e+o(1)}.\n\\]\nIts normalized law satisfies, for each fixed rational \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1/8\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{\\xi(T_i)\\in N_{L^{-\\alpha}}(H)\\mid X=x\\}\n\\leq L^{-c_1\\alpha+o(1)}\n\\]\nuniformly over affine \\((n-2)\\)-planes \\(H\\). The fixed \\(c_1&gt;0\\) is independent of \\(\\alpha\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Sort unit cubes by occupancy in physical \\(L\\)-boxes. A dyadic class retains \\(L^{2n-o(1)}\\) cubes. Proposition 8 at radius \\(L^2\\) would give a fixed power improvement if occupancy were at most \\(L^{n-\\delta}\\); hence retained occupancies are \\(L^{n+o(1)}\\). Divide coordinates by \\(L^2\\). Counting original cubes in enlarged balls and dividing by minimum occupancy gives the asserted \\(\\mathcal P\\)-growth.</p><p>Exact packets at radius \\(L^2\\) have bounded total coefficient weight. On each retained \\(L\\)-box keep nearby padded trajectories. Proposition 8 at radius \\(L\\), applied to its \\(L^{n+o(1)}\\) selected unit cubes, gives\n\\[\nL^{n-2e-o(1)}\\leq L^{n/(n+1)+o(1)}w_i(x),\n\\]\nso \\(w_i(x)\\geq L^{-e-o(1)}\\). Counting Kakeya bounds\n\\[\n\\sum_x\\prod_iw_i(x)^{1/n}\\leq L^{o(1)}.\n\\]\nDeleting exceptional large weights leaves \\(w_i(x)=L^{-e+o(1)}\\).</p><p>Choose \\(c_1&lt;c_0/2\\), where \\(c_0\\) is from Proposition 10. For a finite rational list of \\(\\alpha\\), greedily remove strips carrying remaining weight more than \\(L^{-c_1\\alpha}w_i(x)\\). At most \\(L^{c_1\\alpha}\\) removals occur at each scale. Charge a packet to its first removal, so removed energies sum to \\(O(w_i(x))\\). Proposition 10 and Cauchy bound their combined local squared-supremum sum by\n\\[\nL^{n/(n+1)-(c_0-c_1)\\alpha+o(1)}w_i(x),\n\\]\nnegligible compared with the required local norm. The unrestricted fractal estimate forces surviving weight \\(L^{-e+o(1)}\\). Normalize and diagonalize over finite rational lists. Pruning may depend on \\(x\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "Limiting information calculus", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Draw \\(X\\) uniformly from \\(\\mathcal P\\).", "statement": "<p>Draw \\(X\\) uniformly from \\(\\mathcal P\\). Conditional on \\(X\\), draw \\(T_i,T_i'\\) independently from each retained incident law. Let \\(X_s\\) be nested position-grid labels of side comparable to \\(L^{-s}\\), \\(s\\in[0,1]\\cap\\mathbb Q\\), trivial at zero. At sampled labels put\n\\[\nI_L(Y\\mid V)=-\\log_L\\Pr(Y=Y(\\omega)\\mid V).\n\\]</p><p>For countably many rational block and prefix tests, these information variables have a common subsequential limiting joint law. Conditional informations are uniformly integrable and bounded in the limit. Chain rule is exact; conditioning lowers information almost surely in the limiting law.</p><p>If, on a specified event and given \\((Z,V)\\), a label \\(Y\\) has at most \\(L^{a+o(1)}\\) possible values, then\n\\[\nI(Y\\mid Z,V)\\leq a\n\\]\non that event. Event indicators may be included; finite systems holding with positive limiting probability pass to finite scale with fixed positive slack.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the dimension-free limiting-information lemma of [24, Section 3]. For at most \\(L^{C+o(1)}\\) labels,\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{I_L(Y\\mid V)&gt;C+a\\}\\leq L^{-a+o(1)},\n\\]\nuniformly in conditioning, which gives uniform integrability. If \\(p=\\Pr(Y\\mid V)\\), \\(q=\\Pr(Y\\mid V,W)\\), then \\(\\mathbb E[p/q]\\leq1\\); hence\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{I_L(Y\\mid V)-I_L(Y\\mid V,W)&lt;-a\\}\\leq L^{-a}.\n\\]\nThis proves limiting, not finite-scale pointwise, monotonicity. Summing over permitted labels proves restricted determination. Tightness and a diagonal subsequence give the joint law; include costs and restricted-event indicators and pass open implications with slack.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.4", "orig": "Block comparison, additivity, and coordinate subsets", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Define \\( F(s)=I(X_s),\\qquad G_i(s)=I(X_s\\mid T_i),\\qquad G_i'(s)=I(X_s\\mid T_i'). \\) These are nondecreasing Lipschitz profiles, with constants \\(n+1,1,1\\), vanish at zero, and satisfy …", "statement": "<p>Define\n\\[\nF(s)=I(X_s),\\qquad\nG_i(s)=I(X_s\\mid T_i),\\qquad\nG_i'(s)=I(X_s\\mid T_i').\n\\]\nThese are nondecreasing Lipschitz profiles, with constants \\(n+1,1,1\\), vanish at zero, and satisfy\n\\[\nF(s)\\geq ns,\\quad F(1)=n,\\quad\nG_i(1),G_i'(1)\\geq n/(n+1).\n\\]</p><p>For a rational block \\([s,s+b]\\), let \\(C=X_s\\). Let \\(D\\) contain direction bins of precision \\(L^{-b_*}\\), \\(b_*\\geq b\\), for a fixed tube sublist including one reference per family. Use deterministic bin representatives as a coordinate basis. For prefix \\(t\\leq b\\), let \\(Z=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_{n+1})\\) be coordinate labels at relative resolution \\(L^{-t}\\), with \\(\\widehat Z_i\\) the other coordinates. Put\n\\[\nJ=F(s+t)-F(s),\\qquad J_i=G_i(s+t)-G_i(s),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ell=I(X_{s+t}\\mid C)-I(X_{s+t}\\mid C,D),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ell_i=I(X_{s+t}\\mid C,T_i)\n-I(X_{s+t}\\mid C,T_i,D).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\sum_i(J_i-\\ell_i)\n\\leq\\sum_iI(Z_i\\mid\\widehat Z_i,C,D)\n\\leq I(Z\\mid C,D)=J-\\ell.\n\\]\nFor disjoint block or prefix lists using the same bins and exact-tube conditioning, each cost's expected sum is \\(O_n(b_*)\\). Almost surely, for every reference choice,\n\\[\nF=\\sum_{i=1}^{n+1}G_i,\\qquad\nG_i'=G_i,\\qquad G_i(1)=\\frac n{n+1}.\n\\]\nFor any coordinate subset \\(A\\),\n\\[\nI((Z_i)_{i\\in A}\\mid C,D)\n=\\sum_{i\\in A}J_i+O_n\\!\\left(\\sum_i\\ell_i\\right).\n\\]\nUnused coordinates are not added to this conditioning.</p>", "proof": "<p>A tube intersects at most \\(L^{t+o(1)}\\) children inside a parent, giving conditional Lipschitz constants. Unrestricted grid counts give the \\(n+1\\) constant. The \\(\\mathcal P\\)-growth gives \\(F(s)\\geq ns,F(1)=n\\).</p><p>Incident weights give\n\\[\n\\Pr(X=x,T_i=T)\\leq L^{-n+e+o(1)}w_i(T).\n\\]\nTubes whose marginal is less than \\(L^{-a}w_i(T)\\) have total probability \\(O(L^{-a})\\). Otherwise conditional point mass is at most \\(L^{-n/(n+1)+a+o(1)}\\), proving endpoint lower bounds.</p><p>A reference tube determines all off-axis coordinates to subpower ambiguity. Given \\(C,D\\), spatial child and coordinate labels determine each other to subpower ambiguity. Therefore\n\\[\nJ_i-\\ell_i\n=I(Z_i\\mid C,D,T_i)\n=I(Z_i\\mid\\widehat Z_i,C,D,T_i)\n\\leq I(Z_i\\mid\\widehat Z_i,C,D).\n\\]\nChain rule in any coordinate ordering proves the comparison.</p><p>At finite scale expected costs are conditional mutual informations divided by \\(\\log L\\). They telescope on disjoint spatial blocks and total at most\n\\[\nH(D)/\\log L=O_n(b_*).\n\\]\nUniform integrability passes to the limit, as in [24, “Block comparison and telescoping costs”].</p><p>Partition an interval into blocks with \\(b=b_*\\downarrow0\\). Expected positive excess of \\(\\sum_i\\Delta G_i\\) over \\(\\Delta F\\) tends to zero. Endpoint lower bounds force equality on \\([0,1]\\), hence on all subintervals. Repeat for reference replacements to obtain \\(G_i'=G_i\\). For a coordinate subset, put its coordinates first in chain rule; every term is at least \\(J_i-\\ell_i\\), and total surplus is at most \\(\\sum_i\\ell_i\\). This proves the subset formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.5", "orig": "Restriction of two direction equations", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let a basis of \\(\\mathbb R^{n+1}\\) consist of bounded vectors \\(v(\\xi_i)=(2\\xi_i,1)\\), with inverse norm \\(L^{o(1)}\\).", "statement": "<p>Let a basis of \\(\\mathbb R^{n+1}\\) consist of bounded vectors \\(v(\\xi_i)=(2\\xi_i,1)\\), with inverse norm \\(L^{o(1)}\\). Normalize the \\(i\\)-th coefficient of another bounded \\(v(\\xi)\\) to one, with coefficient and inverse \\(L^{o(1)}\\), and let \\(a_j,a_k\\) be two other coefficient ratios.</p><p>A radius-\\(L^{-u}\\) ball in these two ratios pulls back, on bounded frequencies, into an \\(L^{-u+o(1)}\\)-neighborhood of an affine \\((n-2)\\)-plane. The same holds when both ratios are \\(L^{-u}\\)-small.</p>", "proof": "<p>For dual basis rows \\(r_\\ell\\), ratio equations are\n\\[\n(r_j-a_jr_i)\\cdot v=0,\\qquad\n(r_k-a_kr_i)\\cdot v=0.\n\\]\nTheir row map has smallest singular value \\(L^{-o(1)}\\), since the coefficients of \\(r_j,r_k\\) remain the combination coefficients. Center at a realized ratio, so both rows vanish at bounded \\(v_0=(2\\xi_0,1)\\). A row combination \\((a,b)\\) then satisfies \\(b=-2a\\cdot\\xi_0\\), so its spatial restriction retains the same conditioning. The time-one slice is an affine \\((n-2)\\)-plane; approximate equations give the distance claim. For small ratios center at zero, where the rows vanish at the \\(i\\)-th reference direction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.6", "orig": "One common profile, and original equation (5.11)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Almost surely, \\( G_i=G_i'=G,\\qquad F=(n+1)G, \\) \\( 0\\leq\\dot G\\leq1,\\qquad G(1)=\\frac n{n+1},\\qquad (n+1)G(s)\\geq ns. \\) For family one, use off-axis ratios \\(a_2,a_3\\) of \\(T_1'\\) in the exact reference basis.", "statement": "<p>Almost surely,\n\\[\nG_i=G_i'=G,\\qquad F=(n+1)G,\n\\]\n\\[\n0\\leq\\dot G\\leq1,\\qquad\nG(1)=\\frac n{n+1},\\qquad\n(n+1)G(s)\\geq ns.\n\\]</p><p>For family one, use off-axis ratios \\(a_2,a_3\\) of \\(T_1'\\) in the exact reference basis. Conditional on exact \\(X\\) and exact references, redraw \\(T_1'\\) from its original law. Let \\(h_j(u)\\) be the limiting negative base-\\(L\\) logarithm of the mass of the radius-\\(L^{-u}\\) ball centered at sampled \\(a_j\\). Then \\(h_2,h_3\\) are nonnegative and nondecreasing, and\n\\[\nh_2(u)+h_3(u)\\geq c_1u\n\\qquad(0&lt;u&lt;1/8,\\ u\\in\\mathbb Q).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize the alternative direction's \\(i\\)-th coefficient. Replacement transversality and Cramer's rule bound all ratios by subpowers. Include\n\\[\nr_j=\\lim\\min\\{1,\\max(0,-\\log_L|a_j|)\\}\n\\]\nin the joint law. At most one of the \\(n\\) off-axis exponents is positive: two positive exponents force a codimension-two strip by Lemma 11.5, contrary to Lemma 11.2 and conditional independence.</p><p>On \\(r_j=0\\), bins approximate the \\(j\\)-slope from below by \\(L^{-a}\\). Given \\(T_i',Z_j,C,D\\), that coordinate parametrizes the tube with at most \\(L^{O_n(a)+o(1)}\\) child labels. Apply restricted determination before limits and let \\(a\\downarrow0\\):\n\\[\nG_i(s+t)-G_i(s)-\\ell_i'\n\\leq I(Z_j\\mid C,D)\n\\leq G_j(s+t)-G_j(s)+O_n\\!\\left(\\sum_k\\ell_k\\right).\n\\]\nNo hidden exact reference directions are added to conditioning.</p><p>Distribute costs over dyadic blocks. Their expected integrals are \\(O_n(b)\\), so dyadic summability makes cost densities vanish almost everywhere. Thus \\(\\dot G_i\\leq\\dot G_j\\) whenever \\(r_j=0\\). Equal endpoint integrals force profile equality for such pairs. Each profile agrees with at least \\(n-1\\) others; each equivalence class has at least \\(n\\) members, and two classes would require \\(2n&gt;n+1\\) members. Hence there is one common profile.</p><p>For the angular sum, a joint ratio ball pulls back to a codimension-two strip by Lemma 11.5, so its information is at least \\(c_1u\\). Joint information is at most the sum of marginal informations by chain rule and limiting conditioning. Centered-ball and grid information agree: cells whose neighbor mass exceeds their own by \\(L^a\\) have total sampled mass \\(O(L^{-a})\\). This proves the displayed sum bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.7", "orig": "Robust planar projection", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(0<d<1\\) and \\(\\rho>0\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;d&lt;1\\) and \\(\\rho&gt;0\\). There exist \\(\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{pr}},\\eta_{\\mathrm{pr}}&gt;0\\) such that, for sufficiently small \\(\\Delta\\), if \\(A\\subset B(0,1)\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\Delta^{-2d+\\eta_{\\mathrm{pr}}}\n\\leq\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A)\n\\leq\\Delta^{-2d-\\eta_{\\mathrm{pr}}},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A\\cap B(z,r))\n\\leq\\Delta^{-\\eta_{\\mathrm{pr}}}r^\\rho\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A)\n\\quad(\\Delta\\leq r\\leq1),\n\\]\nand a probability \\(\\sigma\\) on projection lines satisfies\n\\[\n\\sigma(B(V,r))\\leq\\Delta^{-\\eta_{\\mathrm{pr}}}r^\\rho,\n\\]\nthen some \\(V\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\sigma\\) obeys\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_\\Delta(\\pi_VA')\\geq\\Delta^{-d-\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{pr}}}\n\\]\nfor every \\(A'\\subset A\\) with\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A')\\geq\n\\Delta^{\\eta_{\\mathrm{pr}}}\\mathcal N_\\Delta(A).\n\\]\nTolerance and gain may be decreased.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply He [21, Theorem 1] with ambient dimension two, projection dimension one, and set exponent \\(2d\\). Its Grassmannian condition is angular-ball nonconcentration. The conclusion is simultaneous for large subsets, outside a direction set of probability a positive power of \\(\\Delta\\). Reduce the tolerance.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.8", "orig": "Projection block test", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(0<d<1\\), \\(d_0>0\\), \\(\\kappa_0>0\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;d&lt;1\\), \\(d_0&gt;0\\), \\(\\kappa_0&gt;0\\). For sufficiently small \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\), the following cannot hold with positive limiting probability on a rational block \\([s,s+b]\\subset[0,1]\\), \\(b&lt;1/8\\), with direction precision \\(b_*\\geq b\\).</p><p>Let \\(D_0\\) be reference bins and \\(D_1\\) additionally contain the bin of \\(T_1'\\), using the same bin-defined coordinate basis. For some \\(j\\in\\{2,3\\}\\), require:\n\\[\n|G(s+b)-G(s)-db|\\leq\\zeta b,\n\\]\n\\[\nG(s+u)-G(s)\\geq d_0u-\\zeta b;\n\\]\nevery endpoint and tested-prefix cost for \\(D_0,D_1\\), reference tubes, and \\(T_1'\\) is at most \\(\\zeta b\\); and\n\\[\nh_j(u)\\geq\\kappa_0u-\\zeta b.\n\\]\nPrefixes need only range over one fixed finite rational grid containing \\(b\\), of sufficiently small relative mesh. Constants and relative meshes are independent of \\(s,b,b_*\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(K=L^b\\), \\(Y_u=(Z_1,Z_j)\\). Lemmas 11.4 and 11.6 give, for \\(a=0,1\\),\n\\[\nI(Y_b\\mid C,D_a)=2db+O_n(\\zeta b),\\qquad\nI(Y_u\\mid C,D_a)\\geq2d_0u-O_n(\\zeta b).\n\\]\nLet \\(\\widetilde a_j\\) be the alternative slope determined by \\(D_1\\), and \\(\\Pi\\) the resolution-\\(K^{-1}\\) label of \\(z_j-\\widetilde a_jz_1\\). Given \\(T_1',C,D_1\\), \\(\\Pi\\) has subpower ambiguity and coordinate one parametrizes the tube. Thus\n\\[\nI(Y_b\\mid\\Pi,C,D_1)\\geq db-O_n(\\zeta b),\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nI(\\Pi\\mid C,D_1)\\leq db+O_n(\\zeta b).\n\\]</p><p>Pass the finite system to finite scale with slack and fix a parent/reference-bin fiber with success probability \\(p\\geq p_*&gt;0\\). Let \\(\\mu\\) be its law. For successful labels, with \\(\\alpha=O_n(\\zeta)+o(1)\\),\n\\[\nK^{-2d-\\alpha}\\leq\\mu(Y_b=y),\\ \\mu(Y_b=y\\mid A_{\\mathrm{bin}}=a)\n\\leq K^{-2d+\\alpha},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mu(Y_u=y_u)\\leq K^\\alpha L^{-2d_0u},\\qquad\n\\mu(\\Pi=\\pi\\mid A_{\\mathrm{bin}}=a)\\geq K^{-d-\\alpha}.\n\\]\nThe common set \\(A\\) of endpoint pair cells with a successful realization therefore has\n\\[\npK^{2d-\\alpha}\\leq|A|\\leq K^{2d+\\alpha},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathcal N_{K^{-1}}(A\\cap B(z,r))}\n{\\mathcal N_{K^{-1}}(A)}\n\\lesssim p^{-1}K^{O_n(\\zeta)+o(1)}r^{d_0}.\n\\]</p><p>Retain alternative bins with conditional success at least \\(p/2\\). Each supplies a subset \\(A_a\\subset A\\) of size at least \\((p/2)K^{2d-\\alpha}\\), projecting in direction \\((-\\widetilde a_j,1)\\) to at most \\(K^{d+O_n(\\zeta)+o(1)}\\) cells.</p><p>Construct one direction law by restricting actual samples to success and retained bins, normalizing by total mass at least \\(p/2\\), and pushing to these projection lines. Condition first on exact points and references. If a slope ball meets success, its successful centered-mass test bounds the entire original ball probability. Restriction decreases it. Integration and final normalization give\n\\[\n\\sigma(B(V,r))\n\\lesssim p^{-1}K^{O_n(\\zeta)+o(1)}r^{\\kappa_0}.\n\\]\nSubpower slope ranges and condition numbers permit rescaling \\(A\\) to a unit ball.</p><p>Choose \\(0&lt;\\rho&lt;\\min(d_0,\\kappa_0)\\). Lemma 11.7 applies for small \\(\\zeta\\), but every direction in the support has its own sufficiently large subset of this same \\(A\\) with projection below the guaranteed gain. Contradiction. No unused coordinates were frozen.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.9", "orig": "Finite angular alternatives", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For fixed \\(d\\in(0,1)\\) and \\(d_0>0\\), there is a fixed finite list of positive rational ratios, bounded away from zero, such that for every sufficiently small rational \\(b_0\\), almost every path admits a length \\(b'\\) …", "statement": "<p>For fixed \\(d\\in(0,1)\\) and \\(d_0&gt;0\\), there is a fixed finite list of positive rational ratios, bounded away from zero, such that for every sufficiently small rational \\(b_0\\), almost every path admits a length \\(b'\\) from this list times \\(b_0\\), and \\(j=2\\) or \\(3\\), satisfying the angular hypothesis of Lemma 11.8 with spare slack for every tested block length in \\([b'/4,b']\\).</p><p>Each alternative has a fixed positive angular slope, tolerance, and finite relative mesh independent of \\(b_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the block-test tolerance \\(\\zeta_2\\) for angular slope \\(c_1/4\\), choose rational \\(a\\ll c_1\\zeta_2\\), then a reduced tolerance \\(\\zeta_1\\) for slope \\(a/2\\).</p><p>Test \\(h_2(u)\\geq au\\) on a fine fixed grid from \\(c\\zeta_1b_0\\) to \\(b_0\\). If all pass, monotonicity fills gaps and nonnegativity handles smaller radii; use \\(j=2,b'=b_0\\). Otherwise some tested \\(v\\) has \\(h_2(v)&lt;av\\), and\n\\[\nh_3(u)\\geq c_1u-h_2(u)&gt;c_1u-av\\geq(c_1/2)u\n\\quad(2av/c_1\\leq u\\leq v).\n\\]\nUse \\(j=3,b'=v\\). The choice of \\(a\\) makes omitted smaller radii harmless for all block lengths in \\([v/4,v]\\). The tested ratios form the required finite list.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.10", "orig": "Binary derivative and finite alternation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Almost surely, \\( \\dot G\\in\\{0,1\\}\\quad\\text{almost everywhere}, \\) and \\( E=\\{s:\\dot G(s)=1\\} \\) is, modulo null sets, a finite union of intervals.", "statement": "<p>Almost surely,\n\\[\n\\dot G\\in\\{0,1\\}\\quad\\text{almost everywhere},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nE=\\{s:\\dot G(s)=1\\}\n\\]\nis, modulo null sets, a finite union of intervals. There is an interior full-to-empty transition.</p><p>Consequently, for every fixed \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), some rational block \\([s,s+b]\\), \\(0&lt;b&lt;1/8\\), satisfies with positive probability\n\\[\nG(s+b)-G(s)=b/2+O(\\eta b),\\qquad\nG(s+b/2)-G(s)=b/2+O(\\eta b).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the one-dimensional profile mechanism from [24, Section 3], with hypotheses now supplied by Lemmas 11.4, 11.6, 11.8, and 11.9.</p><p>If interior derivatives occur on positive path-times-scale measure, choose \\(d\\in(0,1)\\), \\(d_0&lt;d\\) capturing positive measure. At dyadic \\(b_0\\downarrow0\\), form rational candidate meshes for every finite angular alternative, including all prefix tests, with \\(b_*=b_0\\). Candidate families have bounded overlap and can be colored into finitely many disjoint lists. If \\(C_I\\) is the sum of their costs,\n\\[\n\\sum_I\\mathbb EC_I\\lesssim_n b_0.\n\\]\nThe corresponding cost densities have expected integral \\(O_n(b_0)\\); dyadic summability makes them vanish almost everywhere. At differentiability points all nearby increments have their linear approximation. An angular alternative then produces a successful forbidden block test. Countably many rational tests contradict Lemma 11.8. Hence derivatives are binary.</p><p>For the deterministic balanced-interval step, let \\(a&lt;b\\) be density points of \\(E,E^c\\). For every sufficiently small \\(r\\), there is \\([x,y]\\subset(a,b)\\) with\n\\[\nr\\leq y-x\\leq2r,\\quad G(y)-G(x)=(y-x)/2,\n\\]\n\\[\nG(x+u)-G(x)\\geq u/2\\quad(0\\leq u\\leq y-x).\n\\]\nSet \\(H(s)=G(s)-s/2\\). Density gives a closed hill. Choose the highest level with a closed above-level interval of length at least \\(r\\). Components strictly above it have length at most \\(r\\), so the level set's first return at distance at least \\(r\\) lies within \\(2r\\), proving the claim.</p><p>If paths had arbitrarily many ordered full/empty density pairs on positive probability, these hills and Lemma 11.9 would give arbitrarily many disjoint spatially and angularly successful candidates at common sufficiently small scales. Lemma 11.8 forces each to fail a cost test, costing a fixed multiple of \\(b_0\\). The expected cost bound makes the expected number of failures uniformly bounded, contrary to Fatou. Thus alternation is finite, so \\(E\\) is a finite union of intervals modulo null sets.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n|E|=n/(n+1)&lt;1,\\qquad (n+1)|E\\cap[0,s]|\\geq ns.\n\\]\nThe latter excludes an initial empty interval; the former forces a later empty interval. A rational block centered sufficiently near an interior full-to-empty jump gives the stated positive-probability event by countability.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.11", "orig": "No full-to-empty block", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently small fixed \\(\\eta>0\\), the positive-probability block event in Lemma 11.10 is impossible.", "statement": "<p>For sufficiently small fixed \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), the positive-probability block event in Lemma 11.10 is impossible.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix the block, put \\(K=L^b\\), and fix a parent \\(C=X_s=c\\) with conditional success at least \\(p_*&gt;0\\). For endpoint labels write\n\\[\np_c(z)=\\Pr(X_{s+b}=z\\mid C=c),\\qquad\nq_T(z)=\\Pr(X_{s+b}=z\\mid T_1=T,C=c).\n\\]\nA pair is good if it has a successful realization. For good labels,\n\\[\nK^{-(n+1)/2-O_n(\\eta)}\n\\leq p_c(z)\\leq K^{-(n+1)/2+O_n(\\eta)},\n\\]\n\\[\nq_T(z)\\leq K^{-1/2+O_n(\\eta)}.\n\\]\nThe set \\(Z\\) of good endpoint centers satisfies\n\\[\n|Z|\\leq K^{(n+1)/2+O_n(\\eta)},\\qquad\n\\#(Z\\cap B(z,CK^{-1/2}))\\leq K^{O_n(\\eta)}.\n\\]\nGood incidences have central-line error \\(\\delta=K^{-1}L^{o(1)}\\).</p><p>Polynomial-partition \\(Z\\subset\\mathbb R^{n+1}\\) with degree \\(O_n(P)\\),\n\\[\nP=K^{1/2-\\varepsilon}.\n\\]\nEach open cell contains at most \\(K^{(n+1)\\varepsilon+O_n(\\eta)}\\) endpoints. Take a wall containing the incidence error. At resolution \\(r=K^{-1/2}\\), Wongkew's bound gives at most\n\\[\nO_n\\!\\left(\\sum_{a=1}^{n+1}P^ar^{a-(n+1)}\\right)\n=O_n(PK^{n/2})\n\\]\nwall boxes. The discarded good mass is\n\\[\nK^{-\\varepsilon+O_n(\\eta)}=o(1)\n\\]\nwhen \\(\\eta\\ll_n\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>A line outside the zero set visits \\(O_n(P)\\) cells. Draw \\(T\\) with its original conditional marginal, then \\(z,z'\\) independently with law \\(q_T\\). Cauchy over visited cells and Jensen give\n\\[\n\\Pr(z,z'\\text{ good off-wall in one cell}\\mid C=c)\n\\gtrsim P^{-1}=K^{-1/2+\\varepsilon}.\n\\]</p><p>Near pairs, \\(|z-z'|\\lesssim K^{-1/2}\\), contribute at most \\(K^{-1/2+O_n(\\eta)}\\). For farther pairs, an incident line direction lies in caps of radius \\(K^{-1/3}\\) for large \\(L\\). Under double sampling, \\((T,z)\\) has its original joint law, and\n\\[\n\\Pr(T\\in A\\mid z,C=c)\n=\\sum_{x:X_{s+b}(x)=z}\n\\Pr(X=x\\mid z,C=c)\\Pr(T\\in A\\mid X=x).\n\\]\nA cap is contained in a codimension-two strip. Lemma 11.2 at exponent \\(b/3\\) bounds its probability by \\(K^{-c_1/3+o(1)}\\). For a fixed first endpoint, its same-cell candidate list has at most \\(K^{(n+1)\\varepsilon+O_n(\\eta)}\\) members and is fixed before drawing \\(T\\). Bound \\(q_T(z')\\) on good incidences and then the cap indicator. Far pairs contribute at most\n\\[\nK^{-1/2-c_1/3+(n+1)\\varepsilon+O_n(\\eta)+o(1)}.\n\\]\nDivide by the lower bound. Choosing \\(\\varepsilon\\ll_n c_1\\), then \\(\\eta\\ll_n\\varepsilon\\), makes both relative contributions tend to zero, a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Sparse estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is \\(c_s>0\\), depending only on \\(n\\) and fixed packet boxes, such that the following holds.", "statement": "<p>There is \\(c_s&gt;0\\), depending only on \\(n\\) and fixed packet boxes, such that the following holds.</p><p>Fix \\(\\tau&gt;0\\) and \\(L^\\tau\\leq D\\leq L\\). Let \\(U\\) be a compact array of scale \\(L\\), coefficient energy \\(\\mathcal E\\), and let \\(\\mu\\) have the fixed native-time range, growth\n\\[\n\\mu(B(z,r))\\leq mr^n\\qquad(r\\geq1),\n\\]\nand, for every nonzero label,\n\\[\n\\int\\left(1+\\frac{|x-y_\\alpha-2t\\omega_\\alpha|}{L}\\right)^{-M}\\,d\\mu\n\\leq mL^{n+1}/D.\n\\]\nWith sufficiently many bounded profile derivatives,\n\\[\n\\sum_q\\mu(q)^\\beta\\sup_q|U|^2\n\\leq C_\\tau m^\\beta L^{2n/(n+1)}D^{-c_s}\\mathcal E.\n\\]\nThe same holds for any fixed finite derivative list if original profiles have sufficiently many additional derivatives. The exponent \\(c_s\\) is independent of \\(\\tau\\); constants and finite differentiability may depend on \\(\\tau\\). Submeasures and fixed native-time enlargements are allowed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose dyadic \\(K\\asymp L^v\\), with \\(v\\) later, and \\(P=2/(1-\\beta)=q_{n-1}\\). Unit-cube Sobolev and Hölder reduce the desired norm to finitely many\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_\\beta(\\partial^aU,\\mu).\n\\]\nDerivatives preserve labels and cost comparable energy. Localize initially to side-\\(L^2\\) spatial boxes, with bounded packet-energy overlap. Use Lemma 9.1 to pass to exact layers, remove the common exterior modulation, and discard \\(|k|&gt;L^\\gamma\\) with sufficiently high decay. Minkowski returns the final estimate.</p><p>At every node apply Lemma 12.1. Broad cubes gain by Lemma 12.2. On narrow cubes Lemma 10.1 gives\n\\[\n\\|V\\|_{L^P(Q)}^2\n\\lesssim_\\varepsilon K^\\varepsilon\n\\sum_{\\theta,a}(1+|a|)^{-N_1}\n\\|V_\\theta\\|_{L^P(Q+K^2a)}^2.\n\\]\nInactive bins are absorbed.</p><p>For child bin center \\(v_0\\), rescale by\n\\[\n\\Phi_{v_0}(x,t)=((x-2v_0t)/K,t/K^2),\\qquad l'=l/K.\n\\]\nCoefficient energy and native profiles are unchanged. Lemma 10.2 supplies factor\n\\[\nK^{2-(n+2)\\beta}=K^{-\\beta},\n\\]\nand child growth \\(Cm_0K^{n+1}\\). Direct pushforward preserves the same \\(D\\). Keep shifts and finite neighbors as separate weighted branches. Truncate \\(|a|\\leq L^\\eta\\), choosing decay last, and require\n\\[\nK^2L^\\eta\\ll l'\n\\]\nso translated measures also preserve moments and fixed time ranges.</p><p>At depth \\(j\\), with \\(h=K^j,l=L/h\\),\n\\[\nm_j\\leq C^jh^{n+1}m,\n\\]\nand the main normalization cancels:\n\\[\nh^{-\\beta}m_j^\\beta l^{2n/(n+1)}\n\\leq C^jm^\\beta L^{2n/(n+1)}.\n\\]</p><p>Stop before division by \\(K\\) would take \\(l&lt;D^{1/2}\\). Terminal scales satisfy\n\\[\nD^{1/2}\\leq l&lt;KD^{1/2}.\n\\]\nSpatial localization at each node has summable energy and negligible exact-packet tails, since \\(l\\geq L^{\\tau/2}\\).</p><p>At a terminal node retain coarse cubes within transverse distance \\(lL^\\eta\\) of some trajectory. A radius-\\(l\\) ball meeting retained support lies at normalized distance \\(O(L^\\eta)\\) from one such trajectory. Its single-label moment yields\n\\[\n\\nu(B(z,l))\\leq CL^{O(M\\eta)}m_jl^{n+1}/D.\n\\]\nSort coarse masses as \\(m_j\\chi\\), choose a unit supremum witness in each coarse cube, and apply Lemma 8.1 at \\(S\\asymp l^2\\). Witness counts have parameters\n\\[\n\\Gamma\\lesssim K^{2n}\\chi^{-1},\\qquad\n\\lambda_0\\lesssim L^{O(M\\eta)}\\chi^{-1}l^{n+1}/D.\n\\]\nThe mass exponent cancels because\n\\[\n\\beta-\\frac4{(n+1)(n+2)}-\\gamma_n=0.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_\\beta(V,\\nu)\n\\lesssim_\\varepsilon\nK^{C_n}L^{O(M\\eta)+\\varepsilon}\nm_j^\\beta l^{2n/(n+1)}(l/D)^{\\gamma_n}\n\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{node}}.\n\\]</p><p>Set\n\\[\na_*=\\min(c_b/2,\\gamma_n/2),\\qquad c_s=a_*/8.\n\\]\nBroad leaves gain at least \\(D^{-c_b/2}\\); terminal leaves gain\n\\[\n(l/D)^{\\gamma_n}\\leq K^{\\gamma_n}D^{-\\gamma_n/2}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(v\\) so\n\\[\nv&lt;\\tau c_b/4,\\qquad 2v&lt;\\tau/4,\\qquad\n(C_n+\\gamma_n)v&lt;\\tau a_*/16.\n\\]\nThe depth is now fixed. Choose decoupling/fractal losses and then \\(\\eta\\) so each allocated loss consumes at most \\(D^{a_*/8}\\), and \\(2v+\\eta&lt;\\tau/2\\). Choose \\(\\gamma&lt;\\tau/8\\), then tail orders and finite regularity last.</p><p>At each depth, bins partition energy, shift weights are summable, neighbor choices are finite, and spatial energy overlap is bounded. Summing the fixed number of depths costs only \\(C_\\tau\\). All ignored errors beat the polynomial total configuration size after their orders are chosen. Restore compact layers and Sobolev derivatives.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "The geometric alternative", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At packet scale \\(l\\), group an exact field \\(V=\\sum_\\theta V_\\theta\\) into bins of side \\(K^{-1}\\).", "statement": "<p>At packet scale \\(l\\), group an exact field \\(V=\\sum_\\theta V_\\theta\\) into bins of side \\(K^{-1}\\). Assume exact supports are within \\(O(l^{-1})\\) of labels plus one common shift, with \\(l\\gg K^2\\) and bounded shifted frequencies.</p><p>Call a bin active on a side-\\(K^2\\) cube \\(Q\\) if\n\\[\n\\|V_\\theta\\|_{L^P(Q)}\\geq K^{-A}\\|V\\|_{L^P(Q)},\n\\qquad A&gt;n+1,\n\\quad P=q_{n-1}.\n\\]\nFor a sufficiently large fixed \\(C_0\\), either all active centers lie within \\(C_0/K\\) of an affine hyperplane, or \\(n+1\\) active bins have determinant at least \\(cK^{-n}\\) throughout their exact supports. Common shifts do not change the alternative.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose centers greedily maximizing distance from the affine span of earlier choices. If a maximal height is at most \\(C_0/K\\), all centers lie near a hyperplane. Otherwise Gram–Schmidt heights \\(d_1\\geq\\cdots\\geq d_n&gt;C_0/K\\). In the resulting basis the difference matrix is upper triangular with row-\\(j\\) entries bounded by \\(d_j\\). Factoring row heights leaves a bounded unit upper-triangular matrix with bounded inverse, so the original inverse is \\(O_n(d_n^{-1})\\). Bin perturbations \\(O(K^{-1})\\) change determinant by a small relative amount for large \\(C_0\\). The determinant is \\(\\gtrsim\\prod_jd_j\\gtrsim K^{-n}\\). Common translation preserves the simplex determinant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Broad gain", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(c_b>0\\), depending only on \\(n\\) and the fixed frequency box, such that if \\(K\\leq l^{c_b}\\), then on every side-\\(O(l^2)\\) box …", "statement": "<p>There is \\(c_b&gt;0\\), depending only on \\(n\\) and the fixed frequency box, such that if \\(K\\leq l^{c_b}\\), then on every side-\\(O(l^2)\\) box,\n\\[\n\\sum_{Q\\text{ broad}}\\nu(Q)^\\beta\\|V\\|_{L^P(Q)}^2\n\\lesssim\nm_0^\\beta l^{2n/(n+1)-c_b}\\mathcal E_0.\n\\]\nHere \\(P=q_{n-1}\\), \\(\\nu(B(z,r))\\leq m_0r^n\\), and the total exact field and every subfield have squared input norm at most \\(C\\mathcal E_0\\). “Broad” is the second alternative in Lemma 12.1. The gain is independent of packet derivative and decay orders.</p>", "proof": "<p>If no gain exists, normalize \\(m_0=\\mathcal E_0=1\\) and take \\(l\\to\\infty,K=l^{o(1)}\\). Discard polynomially tiny masses and norm levels. Pigeonhole one active transverse tuple and levels\n\\[\n\\nu(Q)\\simeq\\chi,\\qquad \\|V\\|_{L^P(Q)}\\simeq A_0\n\\]\non \\(N\\) cubes, obtaining\n\\[\n\\chi^\\beta A_0^2N\\geq l^{2n/(n+1)-o(1)},\\quad\n\\chi\\leq l^{o(1)},\\quad \\chi N\\lesssim l^{2n}.\n\\]\nEach active field has a unit witness at least \\(K^{-A-(n-1)}A_0\\). Pigeonhole witness offsets and translate fields separately so witnesses lie in the same associated unit cube. Lemma 11.1 gives\n\\[\nA_0^{2(n+1)/n}N\\leq l^{o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>For limiting logarithms \\(x,y,z\\) of \\(\\chi,A_0,N\\),\n\\[\nx\\leq0,\\quad x+z\\leq2n,\\quad\n\\frac{2(n+1)}ny+z\\leq0,\\quad\n\\beta x+2y+z\\geq\\frac{2n}{n+1}.\n\\]\nThe chain\n\\[\n\\frac{2n}{n+1}\\leq\\beta x+2y+z\n\\leq\\frac{2x+z}{n+1}\n\\leq\\frac{2n+x}{n+1}\n\\leq\\frac{2n}{n+1}\n\\]\nforces\n\\[\n\\chi=l^{o(1)},\\qquad N=l^{2n+o(1)},\\qquad A_0=l^{-e+o(1)}.\n\\]\nWitness counts inherit \\(l^{o(1)}r^n\\)-growth from the disjoint coarse cubes of mass at least \\(l^{-o(1)}\\). The translated fields contradict Proposition 11. Decrease the gain so the same \\(c_b\\) works in hypothesis and conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Weak estimate with density and sparsity", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\(n\\geq3\\) and the compact-array, growth, and moment conventions of Proposition 8.", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(n\\geq3\\) and the compact-array, growth, and moment conventions of Proposition 8. There are\n\\[\np&gt;2,\\qquad \\sigma&gt;0,\\qquad\n0&lt;2a&lt;1-\\beta,\\qquad\n\\beta=\\frac2{n+1},\\quad e=\\frac{n^2}{n+1},\n\\]\ndepending only on dimension and fixed packet conventions, such that the following holds.</p><p>Let a scale-\\(L\\) compact array \\(U\\) have coefficient energy \\(\\mathcal E\\). Let \\(1\\leq D\\leq L\\). Suppose \\(\\mu\\) has growth and packet-moment parameter \\(m\\), fixed native-time range, support in a side-\\(O(L^2)\\) box, and\n\\[\n\\mu(B(z,1))\\leq m\\chi,\\qquad0&lt;\\chi\\leq1.\n\\]\nFor every \\(g&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mu\\{|U|\\geq\\chi^agL^{-e}\\}\n\\leq C_{\\mathrm{ind}}mL^{2n}g^{-p}D^{-\\sigma}\\mathcal E^{p/2}.\n\\]\nThe assertion holds for subarrays with their original native profiles.</p><p>The proof normalizes \\(\\mathcal E=1\\), inducts on dyadic \\(L\\)-ranges, and at fixed \\(L\\) inducts on the number of doublings of \\(D\\) needed to reach \\(L^\\kappa\\). Smaller \\(L\\) is an outer step; the same \\(L\\) with at least twice \\(D\\) is an inner step.</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize energy to one. Choose parameters in the paper's order.</p><p>First fix \\(b,\\delta\\) as in Lemma 13.1 and a small range \\(2&lt;p\\leq p_{\\max}\\); choose \\(J\\) large for its fixed gain \\(R^{-c_2}\\). Next fix a small density cutoff exponent \\(v_0\\), and fix frame orders using the positive margin\n\\[\nn-\\tfrac12-e=\\frac{n-1}{2(n+1)}&gt;0.\n\\]\nFrame, tuple, and witness costs are \\(R^{C_f}\\), with \\(C_f\\) independent of subsequent geometric powers. Choose \\(u\\) so \\(uC_f&lt;c_s/4\\), then \\(\\sigma\\ll uc_2,c_s\\).</p><p>Choose all finite geometric powers for inner sparsity, walls, plate counts/widths, \\(L/h\\), and tiny densities. Then choose \\(\\kappa\\) so \\(L\\) dominates these powers of \\(X=BD\\) when \\(D&lt;L^\\kappa\\), and \\(\\kappa\\sigma\\ll v_0(1-\\beta-2a)\\). Choose \\(p-2\\) only afterward; choose analytic losses and extra original regularity later, and finally \\(B\\), base scale, and \\(C_{\\mathrm{ind}}\\).</p><p>Since \\(\\|U\\|_\\infty\\lesssim1\\), nonempty levels have \\(g\\lesssim L^e\\chi^{-a}\\). Put \\(\\zeta=1-\\beta-2a&gt;0\\). If \\(D\\geq L^\\kappa\\), Proposition 12 and Chebyshev give\n\\[\n\\mu\\{|U|\\geq\\lambda\\}\n\\leq C_\\kappa mL^{2n}D^{-c_s}\\chi^\\zeta g^{-2}.\n\\]\nIts comparison with the desired bound is bounded for small \\(p-2\\). If \\(D&lt;L^\\kappa\\) but \\(\\chi\\leq L^{-v_0}\\), Proposition 9 gives the comparison\n\\[\nL^{\\varepsilon+\\kappa\\sigma+e(p-2)}\n\\chi^{\\zeta-a(p-2)},\n\\]\nalso bounded. Bounded scales are absorbed uniformly, and \\(g&lt;D^{-1}\\) follows from total mass. Thus assume the active range in Lemma 13.1.</p><p>Suppose the desired estimate fails. Lemmas 13.2–13.3 retain comparable high mass with broad heavy incidences near a polynomial wall and\n\\[\nN_Y\\geq X^{-C}L^n.\n\\]\nLemmas 13.4–13.6 turn this into a polynomial number of affine plates.</p><p>Assign original labels disjointly to plates as in Lemma 13.7. Unassigned labels have moment on \\(\\mu|_{Y_0}\\) at most \\(mL^{n+1}/D_1\\): truncate distant tails, and for a line close to a plate but with normal velocity exceeding \\(X^{C_9}/L\\), its near-plate time is at most\n\\[\nCL\\frac{X^{C_8}+X^{C_{\\mathrm{plate}}}}{X^{C_9}}.\n\\]\nCover intervals by width boxes and sum plate counts. Large \\(C_9\\) gives the moment bound. Inner induction removes the unassigned field on only a small mass fraction.</p><p>Choose \\(b_1=L/h\\asymp X^{C_{10}}\\) above all preceding width/count powers. Intersections of two enlarged plates with projective-normal separation at least \\(1/h\\) have mass at most\n\\[\nX^CL^{n-1}h\n\\]\nin tube coordinates. Paying all pairs, this is negligible relative to \\(N_Y\\). Group remaining plates by \\(1/h\\)-normal clusters. Only boundedly many contribute at a retained point, so one cluster is high. Cluster coefficient energies are disjoint, and each cluster lies within \\(C/h\\) of a single frequency hyperplane.</p><p>Within a cluster, call an \\(R^{-1}\\)-bin significant if it has an \\(O(R^4)\\)-nearby witness at least \\(cR^{-n}\\lambda\\). Nonsignificant bins cannot supply the high field. Except on small mass, significant tangential centers cannot be covered by \\(O(C_{11}\\log X)\\) affine \\((n-2)\\)-strips in tangential frequency: with the cluster's normal thickness, such a cover is a full-space codimension-two cover, excluded by Lemma 13.1.</p><p>Use the sizes and densities of Lemma 13.7. Only \\(O(\\log h)\\) density indices occur, by positivity of the density kernel and polynomial support/mass bounds. Threshold splitting chooses one band per significant bin. If any chosen band is tiny,\n\\[\nd_\\ell&lt;X^{-C_{11}},\n\\]\ncomplete that bin to a robust \\(n\\)-tuple by greedy affine heights. Otherwise there are only \\(O(C_{11}\\log X)\\) colors; one color must supply a robust tuple, or every color would lie in one forbidden strip. Thus remaining tuples either contain a tiny band or have one common non-tiny band.</p><p>Discard tuples of mass at most \\(mh^{2n-c_4}\\). Their number is \\(X^C(\\log h)^C\\), and relative discarded mass is at most\n\\[\nX^C(\\log h)^C(h/L)^{2n}h^{-c_4}=o(1).\n\\]\nFor the rest, Markov and \\(\\int w_T\\,d\\mu=mh^{n+1}d_{\\theta,T}\\) give Lemma 13.8's \\(B_i\\). Envelope comparability across macros is valid since \\(R^4\\ll h\\). The margin \\(n-\\frac12-e&gt;0\\), small \\(c_4,v_0\\), and later small \\(\\kappa\\) imply the frame cutoff \\(A_{\\ell_i}/B_i\\geq h^{c_3}\\).</p><p>Apply Lemma 13.8. For tiny-band tuples, the per-time-bin energy bound gives\n\\[\n\\left(\\prod_iE_i\\right)^{\\beta/n}\n\\left(\\sum_iE_i\\right)^{1-\\beta}\\lesssim h^{-n}.\n\\]\nCount polynomially many clusters and \\(O((L/h)^2)\\) time bins explicitly. The density series obeys\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\min_i d_{\\ell_i}&lt;X^{-C_{11}}}\n(1+\\textstyle\\sum_i\\ell_i)^C\\prod_id_{\\ell_i}^{\\beta/n}\n\\leq CX^{-\\beta C_{11}/(2n)}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(C_{11}\\) last among geometric powers to pay these counts and a desired negative \\(D\\)-power. There is no \\(\\log h\\) loss in this series.</p><p>For a common non-tiny band, the energy expression is at most\n\\[\nCd_\\ell^\\beta\\sum_iE_i.\n\\]\nEach packet has one density index and occurs in only \\(R^C\\) coarse tuple/witness choices; slabs and enlarged time bins have bounded overlap. Charge directly to the global weighted budget of Lemma 13.7, with no cluster/time count. Since \\(\\ell\\lesssim C_{11}\\log X\\), only fixed logarithmic powers remain. Scale factors cancel:\n\\[\nh^{n+2n/(n+1)}h^{-n}b_1^{2n/(n+1)}\n=L^{2n/(n+1)}.\n\\]\nThe choice of \\(u\\), then \\(B\\), pays frame and logarithmic costs from sparse saving. Hence\n\\[\n\\lambda^2M_Y\n\\leq C_Bm\\chi^{1-\\beta}L^{2n/(n+1)}D^{-c_s/2}.\n\\]</p><p>The incidence lower mass gives \\(M_Y\\geq cmL^{2n}X^{-C_{\\mathrm{geo}}}\\). Substitution yields\n\\[\ng^2\\leq C_B\\chi^\\zeta X^{C_{\\mathrm{geo}}}D^{-c_s/2}\n\\leq C_BX^{C_{\\mathrm{geo}}},\n\\]\nso \\(g\\leq C_BX^{C_g}\\), with \\(C_g\\) fixed before \\(p-2\\). Choose\n\\[\nC_g(p-2)+\\sigma&lt;c_s/2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nM_Y\\leq C_B'mL^{2n}g^{-p}D^{-\\sigma}.\n\\]\nAll induction applications removed small fractions independently of \\(C_{\\mathrm{ind}}\\); take \\(C_{\\mathrm{ind}}&gt;C_B'\\). This contradicts the hypothetical violation and closes both inductions. Restore energy homogeneity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Angular induction", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume the outer induction assertion of Proposition 13 at smaller array scales.", "statement": "<p>Assume the outer induction assertion of Proposition 13 at smaller array scales. Work in the active range\n\\[\n\\chi&gt;L^{-v_0},\\qquad D^{-1}\\leq g\\lesssim L^{e+av_0},\n\\qquad D&lt;L^\\kappa,\n\\]\nand put\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\chi^agL^{-e},\\quad X=BD,\\quad R=K^J\\asymp X^u.\n\\]\nChoose\n\\[\n\\beta&lt;b&lt;2/n,\\qquad 2a=1-b+\\delta,\\qquad\n\\delta&gt;0,\\quad b-\\beta-\\delta&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Fix a subarray of energy \\(\\mathcal E_0\\leq1\\). On each side-\\(R^4\\) macro cube prescribe whole original-axis \\(R^{-1}\\)-bins whose centers are covered by \\(O((1+\\log X)^{C_0})\\) sets of these types:\n\\[\n\\text{(a) an }O(R^{-1})\\text{ strip about an affine }(n-2)\\text{-plane};\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{(b) the }O(R^{-1})\\text{ neighborhood of }\n\\{v\\in H\\cap B:|Q_H(v)|\\leq R^{-4}\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(H\\) is an affine hyperplane, \\(B\\) bounded, and \\(Q_H\\) has coefficient norm one in orthonormal coordinates with bounded origin.</p><p>The cover may depend on the macro. If \\(U_{\\mathrm{sel}}\\) is its prescribed-bin field, then for fixed \\(c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mu\\{|U_{\\mathrm{sel}}|\\geq c\\lambda\\}\n\\leq\nCC_{\\mathrm{ind}}R^{-c_2}mL^{2n}g^{-p}\\mathcal E_0^{p/2}\n+C_NmL^{-N}.\n\\]\nHere \\(c_2&gt;0\\) depends only on dimensional exponent choices, not on \\(u,C_0\\); \\(N\\) is any fixed prescribed exponent with sufficiently many original derivatives.</p>", "proof": "<p>Assign each selected whole bin to one covering set. For a quadratic set, choose approximating points and sort \\(|\\nabla Q_H|\\) dyadically down to \\(R^{-1}\\).</p><p>For small gradient class \\(m_1\\), coefficient normalization and small polynomial value force a nontrivial quadratic part; one gradient component has a nontrivial linear part. Thus the class lies in an \\(O(m_1+R^{-1})\\) strip within \\(H\\). It is codimension-two narrow at child precision when\n\\[\ns\\leq c/(Km_1).\n\\]\nFor positive-gradient classes, Taylor expansion near a parent point places descendants within\n\\[\nC\\{m_1^{-1}(s^{-2}+R^{-4})+R^{-1}\\}\n\\lesssim(Ks)^{-1}\n\\]\nof a tangent affine \\((n-2)\\)-plane when\n\\[\nCK/m_1\\leq s\\leq R/K.\n\\]\nThe gap is only a factor \\(O(K^2)\\), so only boundedly many ratio-\\(K\\) steps are crude. The smallest-gradient class does not need the second regime.</p><p>Use \\(\\mathcal T_b\\), \\(P=2/(1-b)\\leq q_{n-2}\\). Lemma 10.1 handles good steps, Cauchy handles exceptional ones. The angular cost is\n\\[\nC_\\varepsilon K^CR^\\varepsilon,\n\\]\nwith \\(C\\) independent of \\(J\\).</p><p>For this nonrecursive calculation smoothly truncate compact native Fourier profiles at radius \\(L^{1/4}\\). Sufficient original regularity makes all errors arbitrarily negative powers of \\(L\\); choose \\(\\kappa\\) so the finite hierarchy fits the Fourier-support hypotheses. Return to original compact child fields at the terminal induction, preserving the recursive profile class.</p><p>Restrict \\(\\mu\\) to the tested set and call its mass \\(M_0\\). Unit density, Hölder, and reproduction bound \\(\\lambda^2M_0\\) by initial \\(\\mathcal T_b\\)-functionals times\n\\[\nC(m\\chi)^{1-b}(1+\\log X)^C.\n\\]\nAfter \\(J\\) transports the scale factor is\n\\[\nC_\\varepsilon K^CR^{\\varepsilon+2-(n+2)b}.\n\\]</p><p>For a fixed angular chain and complete shift sequence, the composed map is\n\\[\n\\tau_{z_{\\mathrm{sh}}}\\circ A_{R,V},\\qquad\nA_{R,V}(x,t)=((x-2Vt)/R,t/R^2),\n\\]\nwith bounded \\(V\\) and shift independent of the original macro. A terminal coarse cube pulls back to diameter at most \\(CR^2K^2\\leq CR^4\\), hence meets boundedly many original macros. Their transported measures recombine with a fixed factor, are dominated by one translated pushforward, have total mass at most \\(M_0\\), and growth\n\\[\nm'\\asymp CmR^{n+1}.\n\\]\nWhole-bin assignment makes the terminal field the same original compact bin field on every contributing macro; energy is charged once.</p><p>At terminal scale \\(l=L/R\\), move each coarse mass to a near-supremum witness, costing \\(K^C\\), and sort masses as \\(m'd\\). Unit covering gives\n\\[\nd\\leq CK^CR\\chi.\n\\]\nThe moved measure has density \\(\\min(1,Cd)\\), admissible \\(D=1\\), and controlled growth. Outer induction and distribution integration give\n\\[\n\\int|V|^2\\,d\\nu\n\\leq\nCK^CC_{\\mathrm{ind}}^{2/p}(m')^{2/p}\nl^{4n/p-2e}d^{2a}M_0^{1-2/p}\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{bin}}.\n\\]\nMultiply by \\((m'd)^{b-1}\\). The \\(d\\)-sum converges because \\(b-1+2a=\\delta&gt;0\\), costing at most \\(C(K^CR\\chi)^\\delta\\).</p><p>Approximation errors are also controlled for small tested mass:\n\\[\n\\sum_Q\\nu(Q)^b\\leq(\\#Q)^{1-b}M_0^b.\n\\]\nMake the total error at most \\(L^{-N'}M_0^b\\). It either absorbs into \\(\\lambda^2M_0\\), or implies \\(M_0\\) is an arbitrarily negative power, since \\(\\lambda\\geq L^{-C}\\).</p><p>After summing bin energies, \\(m,\\chi\\) occur exactly as \\(m^{2/p}\\chi^{2a}\\). The remaining \\(R\\)-exponent before solving for \\(M_0\\) is\n\\[\n\\Gamma(p,\\delta)\n=2-(n+2)b+(n+1)(b-1+2/p)-4n/p+2e+\\delta.\n\\]\nAt \\(p=2,\\delta=0\\), \\(\\Gamma=\\beta-b&lt;0\\). Choose \\(\\delta,p_{\\max}-2\\) small, then \\(J\\) large to pay \\(K^C=R^{C/J}\\), then small losses. Logarithms are absorbed by the final \\(B\\). This proves the gain.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Reduction to broad heavy incidences", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume the relevant outer and inner induction hypotheses and a hypothetical violation \\( Y=\\{|U|\\geq\\lambda\\},\\qquad M_Y=\\mu(Y)>C_{\\mathrm{ind}}mL^{2n}D^{-\\sigma}g^{-p}. \\) At a macro center \\(z=(x,t)\\), call an …", "statement": "<p>Assume the relevant outer and inner induction hypotheses and a hypothetical violation\n\\[\nY=\\{|U|\\geq\\lambda\\},\\qquad\nM_Y=\\mu(Y)&gt;C_{\\mathrm{ind}}mL^{2n}D^{-\\sigma}g^{-p}.\n\\]\nAt a macro center \\(z=(x,t)\\), call an \\(R^{-1}\\)-bin heavy if\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\alpha\\text{ in bin}}|c_\\alpha|^2\n\\left(1+\\frac{|x-y_\\alpha-2\\omega_\\alpha t|}{L}\\right)^{-M}\n\\geq W,\n\\qquad\nW=X^{-P_0}\\frac{mL^{n+1}}{M_Y}.\n\\]</p><p>There is \\(Y'\\subset Y\\), \\(\\mu(Y')\\geq cM_Y\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. the heavy-bin field has modulus at least \\(\\lambda/2\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. heavy centers are not all in a fixed-large-constant \\(R^{-1}\\) codimension-two strip, nor in one quadratic neighborhood of Lemma 13.1;</p><p class=\"li num\">3. each heavy bin supplies at least \\(W/2\\) of its weighted coefficient-square mass from trajectories within \\(X^{C_w}L\\).</p><p>In particular, some \\(n\\) heavy bins have direction \\(n\\)-volume at least \\(R^{-C}\\), uniformly throughout the bins.</p>", "proof": "<p>For each bin restrict \\(\\mu|_Y\\) to macros where it is light. Since \\(R^4\\ll L\\), its weight is comparable at the center and macro points; energy-weighted total moments are at most \\(CWM_Y\\).</p><p>Put \\(D_1=X^{P_1}\\). Split off labels with restricted moment exceeding \\(mL^{n+1}/D_1\\). Their bin energy is at most \\(CD_1X^{-P_0}\\). Remaining labels satisfy inner induction at \\(D_1\\), applied at threshold \\(cR^{-n}\\lambda\\). Total exceptional mass is at most\n\\[\nCC_{\\mathrm{ind}}mL^{2n}g^{-p}R^{np}D_1^{-\\sigma}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(P_1\\) so \\(D_1\\geq2D\\) and\n\\[\nunp_{\\max}-\\sigma P_1+\\sigma&lt;0.\n\\]\nIf \\(D_1\\geq L^\\kappa\\), use the already established large-\\(D\\) range.</p><p>For split-off bins, cap-rescale and use outer induction at \\(L/R\\), with growth \\(CmR^{n+1}\\), density at most \\(\\min(1,C\\chi R)\\), and level at least \\(gR^{-C}\\). Their total exceptional mass is bounded by\n\\[\nCC_{\\mathrm{ind}}mL^{2n}g^{-p}R^C(D_1X^{-P_0})^{p/2}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(P_0\\) sufficiently larger than \\(P_1\\). These bounds remove only small fractions; light-bin thresholds sum to at most \\(\\lambda/2\\).</p><p>Adverse heavy configurations in item 2 are exactly macro-prescribed fields of Lemma 13.1. Its relative cost is \\(CR^{-c_2}D^\\sigma\\), small because \\(\\sigma&lt;uc_2\\).</p><p>Tube-annulus covering gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\{|x-y_\\alpha-2\\omega_\\alpha t|&gt;X^{C_w}L\\}}\n\\left(1+\\frac{|x-y_\\alpha-2\\omega_\\alpha t|}{L}\\right)^{-M}d\\mu\n\\leq CmL^{n+1}X^{-A_w}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(C_w\\) so \\(A_w&gt;P_0\\), sum with \\(|c_\\alpha|^2\\), and use Markov. This proves item 3. Greedy affine heights now give \\(n\\) robustly independent directions; failure would give the forbidden codimension-two strip.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.3", "orig": "Incidence mass and a wall", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Pass to tube coordinates \\((x,t)/L\\), and normalize measure by \\( \\nu=\\mu/(mL^n) \\) in those coordinates.", "statement": "<p>Pass to tube coordinates \\((x,t)/L\\), and normalize measure by\n\\[\n\\nu=\\mu/(mL^n)\n\\]\nin those coordinates. Write \\(N_Y=\\nu(Y)\\). Then\n\\[\n\\nu(B(z,r))\\leq Cr^n\\quad(r\\geq1),\\qquad\nW=X^{-P_0}L/N_Y.\n\\]\nThe incidences have width \\(X^{C_w}\\) in a domain of diameter \\(O(L)\\).</p><p>One has\n\\[\nN_YW^{n/(n-1)}\\leq X^C,\\qquad\nN_Y\\geq X^{-C}L^n.\n\\]\nMoreover, a subset of \\(Y'\\) of comparable mass lies within distance \\(X^C\\) of the zero set of a polynomial of degree at most \\(X^{C_{\\mathrm{part}}}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a robust \\(n\\)-tuple project onto an \\(n\\)-space where its determinant is at least \\(R^{-C}\\), and refine angularly so one projection works throughout the families. Apply endpoint \\(n\\)-linear Kakeya.</p><p>Divide into columns at incidence width. Each tube meets polynomially many width boxes over a projected column, uniformly in the label, because projected velocity is bounded away from zero. Each box has mass at most \\(X^C\\). If \\(a_{i,j}\\) are incident weights in vertical box \\(j\\), Hölder and sequence-norm monotonicity give\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\prod_{i=1}^na_{i,j}^{1/(n-1)}\n\\leq\\prod_{i=1}^n\\left(\\sum_ja_{i,j}\\right)^{1/(n-1)}.\n\\]\nTotal coefficient weights are at most one. Polynomial tuple and determinant costs prove the first bound. Substitute \\(W=X^{-P_0}L/N_Y\\) for the lower mass bound; growth also gives \\(N_Y\\lesssim L^n\\).</p><p>Polynomial-partition \\(\\nu|_Y\\) with parameter \\(P_2=X^{C'}\\). The measure version follows by smoothing, polynomial bisection, coefficient normalization of factors, and subsequential limits: compact subsets off the limiting zero set have stable signs and retain the region mass bound \\(CN_Y/P_2^{n+1}\\). Mass on the zero set is wall mass.</p><p>A line not contained in the wall visits \\(O(P_2)\\) sign regions. If \\(E_O\\) is visiting coefficient energy,\n\\[\n\\sum_OE_O\\lesssim P_2.\n\\]\nRegions with \\(E_O&gt;C_*P_2^{-n}\\) have small total mass. Away from the enlarged wall, nearby line points lie in the same region. In every remaining region local-energy Kakeya bounds off-wall mass by\n\\[\nX^CW^{-n/(n-1)}P_2^{-n^2/(n-1)}\n\\leq X^{C''}N_YP_2^{-n^2/(n-1)}.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\frac{n^2}{n-1}-(n+1)=\\frac1{n-1}&gt;0,\n\\]\nlarge \\(C'\\) pays all regions. A positive fraction must lie near the wall.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.4", "orig": "Regular graph pieces and derivative bounds", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Divide tube coordinates by \\(L\\), so the domain is bounded and the wall/incidence error is \\( \\Delta\\leq X^{C_4}/L. \\) Let \\(Z\\) be the square-free polynomial wall of Lemma 13.3.", "statement": "<p>Divide tube coordinates by \\(L\\), so the domain is bounded and the wall/incidence error is\n\\[\n\\Delta\\leq X^{C_4}/L.\n\\]\nLet \\(Z\\) be the square-free polynomial wall of Lemma 13.3.</p><p>There is a semialgebraic exceptional subset of dimension at most \\(n-1\\), complexity \\(X^C\\), such that outside its \\(C\\rho\\)-neighborhood, every relevant point of \\(Z\\) has a local graph coordinate with bounded slope and second and third graph derivatives bounded by a fixed power of \\(\\rho^{-1}\\), independently of polynomial coefficients.</p><p>Its discarded neighborhood has tube-coordinate \\(\\nu\\)-mass at most\n\\[\nX^C\\rho L^n.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use fixed-dimensional semialgebraic bounds [1]: a bounded dimension-\\((n-1)\\) set of complexity \\(X^C\\) is covered by \\(X^{C'}\\rho^{-(n-1)}\\) diameter-\\(O(\\rho)\\) boxes; and a bounded semialgebraic set admits a polynomial-complexity partition whose cells have connecting paths of polynomially bounded length. The first follows by a generic mesh, component bounds, and induction on grid faces. The second follows by compatible cylindrical decomposition, lifted base paths, graph or middle-sector sections, and bounded coordinate variation on polynomially many monotonicity pieces.</p><p>Square-freeness makes the singular set dimension at most \\(n-1\\). Partition the regular wall into graph pieces with one omitted coordinate and bounded slope. Choose finitely many base directions sufficient to polarize derivatives through order three, and refine by signs of their fourth directional derivatives. Implicit differentiation makes these tests semialgebraic with polynomial complexity. Include singular sets, frontiers, and lower-dimensional pieces in the exceptional set.</p><p>At distance \\(C\\rho\\), two-sided base segments of length \\(c\\rho\\) remain on the same graph/sign piece: a stopped lift would reach a frontier or singularity too soon, or continue by the implicit function theorem.</p><p>On a segment \\(g(t)=\\varphi(u+te)\\), \\(|g'|\\leq C\\) and \\(g''''\\) has fixed sign, so \\(g'''\\) is monotone. If \\(|g'''(0)|=A\\), one half-segment has constant sign and magnitude at least \\(A\\); a second difference of \\(g'\\) bounds \\(A\\rho^2\\) by a constant. Hence \\(|g'''|\\lesssim\\rho^{-2}\\), and an interior difference quotient gives \\(|g''|\\lesssim\\rho^{-1}\\). Polarize.</p><p>The covering bound and \\(n\\)-growth give discarded mass\n\\[\nX^C\\rho^{-(n-1)}(\\rho L)^n=X^C\\rho L^n.\n\\]\nTake \\(\\rho=X^{-C_5}\\) with large \\(C_5\\), require \\(\\Delta\\ll\\rho\\), and use Lemma 13.3 to make this a small fraction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.5", "orig": "Line tests", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For any prescribed large \\(A\\), there is sufficiently large fixed \\(C_6\\) such that, for every label, outside polynomially many macroscopic time intervals of total length at most \\(X^{-A}\\), incidence with any eligible …", "statement": "<p>For any prescribed large \\(A\\), there is sufficiently large fixed \\(C_6\\) such that, for every label, outside polynomially many macroscopic time intervals of total length at most \\(X^{-A}\\), incidence with any eligible regular wall point \\(p\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|v_j-\\nabla\\varphi(p)\\cdot\\overline v|\\leq X^{C_6}/L,\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\nabla^2\\varphi(p)[\\overline v,\\overline v]|\n\\leq X^{C_6}/L,\\qquad v=(2\\omega,1).\n\\]\nHere \\(x_j=\\varphi(\\overline x)\\) is the eligible graph coordinate from Lemma 13.4. The interval-count exponent is independent of \\(A,C_6\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lift the projected nearby line point to the graph. It is \\(O(\\Delta)\\) from \\(p\\), and first and second derivative tests differ by \\(O(\\Delta X^C)\\).</p><p>For the macroscopic line \\(\\ell(s)\\), set\n\\[\nH(s)=\\ell_j(s)-\\varphi(\\overline\\ell(s)).\n\\]\nAt incidence \\(|H|\\lesssim\\Delta\\). Failure forces \\(|H'|\\) or \\(|H''|\\gtrsim X^{C_6}/L\\). Cylindrical decomposition orders regular roots along the parameter, then refines by derivative signs and thresholds into polynomially many graph-branch intervals.</p><p>On an interval with \\(|H|\\lesssim\\Delta\\), a fixed-sign first derivative of size at least \\(q\\) permits length \\(O(\\Delta/q)\\). A fixed-sign second derivative of size at least \\(q\\) permits length \\(O((\\Delta/q)^{1/2})\\), by midpoint second differences. Take \\(q=cX^{C_6}/L\\) and increase \\(C_6\\). Thresholds alter coefficients, not degrees or quantified-variable counts; existential quantification over eligible nearby points preserves the complexity bound.</p><p>In tube coordinates, bad intervals contribute at most\n\\[\nX^C(LX^{-A}+X^C)\n\\]\nto one enlarged tube's \\(\\nu\\)-mass. Choose \\(A\\), then \\(C_6\\), then let \\(L\\) dominate the fixed powers. Summing with coefficient squares and comparing with \\(WN_Y=LX^{-P_0}\\), Markov leaves each heavy bin with positive mass of labels passing both tests simultaneously at retained wall points.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.6", "orig": "Affine plate covering", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the retained heavy-incidence and wall hypotheses of Lemmas 13.2–13.5, there is \\(Y_0\\subset Y'\\) with \\( \\mu(Y_0)\\gtrsim M_Y \\) contained in at most \\(X^C\\) affine hyperplane plates, each of tube-coordinate …", "statement": "<p>Under the retained heavy-incidence and wall hypotheses of Lemmas 13.2–13.5, there is \\(Y_0\\subset Y'\\) with\n\\[\n\\mu(Y_0)\\gtrsim M_Y\n\\]\ncontained in at most \\(X^C\\) affine hyperplane plates, each of tube-coordinate thickness \\(X^C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At a retained regular point, let \\(\\mathbf n=(n_x,n_t)\\) be a unit normal. A supplied bounded frequency satisfies\n\\[\n|n_x\\cdot2\\omega+n_t|\\lesssim X^{C_6}/L,\n\\]\nso \\(|n_x|\\gtrsim1\\). Thus the exact tangent-frequency hyperplane is well conditioned.</p><p>If \\(\\|\\nabla^2\\varphi(p)\\|&gt;X^{C_7}/L\\), normalize the homogeneous tangent-space quadratic form and restrict it to the time-one tangent-frequency slice. This restriction has coefficient norm comparable to one: the slice's bounded direction space and a bounded point span tangent space with bounded condition number, and\n\\[\nQ(y,t)=y^TCy+2tb\\cdot y+at^2\n\\]\nrestricts at \\(t=1\\) to all coefficients \\(C,b,a\\). Bounded graph slope preserves comparison.</p><p>Move supplied frequencies to the exact tangent hyperplane. The line tests make the normalized quadratic value at most\n\\[\nCX^{C_6-C_7}+CX^{C_6}/L.\n\\]\nChoose \\(C_7\\) large and \\(L\\) dominant so this is below \\(R^{-4}\\). All heavy bins then lie in a forbidden quadratic neighborhood from Lemma 13.2. Therefore\n\\[\n\\|\\nabla^2\\varphi(p)\\|\\leq X^{C_7}/L.\n\\]</p><p>Partition this exact small-Hessian semialgebraic subset into polynomially many cells with polynomial-length connecting paths. Along such a path, with base coordinate \\(u\\),\n\\[\ndg_j=\\nabla^2\\varphi_j\\,du,\\qquad dp_j=g_j\\cdot du.\n\\]\nFor path length \\(D_0\\leq X^C\\), gradient change is at most \\(X^{C_7}D_0/L\\), and height error from the starting tangent plane at most \\(X^{C_7}D_0^2/L\\). Paths remain inside the small-Hessian subset, including possible projected loops. Add the attachment error and return to tube coordinates. Each cell lies in one asserted plate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.7", "orig": "Energy budgets after flattening", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assign original compact packet labels disjointly to the affine plates, keeping those whose central lines come within \\(X^{C_8}\\) and whose normal velocity error is at most \\(X^{C_9}/L\\); enlarge assigned plates to …", "statement": "<p>Assign original compact packet labels disjointly to the affine plates, keeping those whose central lines come within \\(X^{C_8}\\) and whose normal velocity error is at most \\(X^{C_9}/L\\); enlarge assigned plates to contain their supports. Let\n\\[\nb_1=L/h\\asymp X^{C_{10}}\n\\]\nwith dyadic \\(h\\) nested with \\(R\\), and group plates by projective-normal resolution \\(1/h\\), after discarding intersections of separated-normal plates as in the proposition proof.</p><p>Within each cluster, frequencies lie within \\(C/h\\) of one affine hyperplane. For each fine original-axis \\(h^{-1}\\)-bin \\(\\theta\\) centered at \\(v\\), write\n\\[\nU_\\theta(z)=h^{-n/2}e^{i\\phi_\\theta(z)}\nU_\\theta'(A_{h,v}z),\n\\]\nwhere \\(U_\\theta'\\) has scale \\(b_1\\). Localize to compact unit native cells \\(T\\), with fixed enlargement \\(T^*\\), and set\n\\[\ns_{\\theta,T}\n=Ch^{-n/2}\\max_{|\\alpha|\\leq N_*}\\sup_{T^*}|\\partial^\\alpha U_\\theta'|,\n\\]\n\\[\nd_{\\theta,T}\n=(mh^{n+1})^{-1}\n\\int(1+\\operatorname{dist}(A_{h,v}z,T))^{-P_*}\\,d\\mu(z).\n\\]\nFix \\(P_*&gt;M+n+2\\) above the required frame envelope order, and \\(N_*\\) above the frame and nonrecursive localization orders. Use \\(P_*\\) also for \\(w_T\\). Then\n\\[\n\\int w_T\\,d\\mu=mh^{n+1}d_{\\theta,T},\\qquad d_{\\theta,T}\\lesssim1.\n\\]</p><p>For each physical time bin of length \\(h^2\\), with fixed enlargement,\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mathrm{bin}}s_{\\theta,T}^2\\lesssim h^{-n}.\n\\]\nGlobally over clusters, fine bins, cells, and time bins with bounded overlap,\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\theta,T}d_{\\theta,T}^\\beta s_{\\theta,T}^2\n\\lesssim_B h^{-n}b_1^{2n/(n+1)}D^{-c_s}.\n\\]\nThe first estimate is only per time bin.</p>", "proof": "<p>A physical \\(h^2\\)-interval is bounded in child time. At each time, child atoms and their required derivatives have summable Gram rows: integrate by parts in scaled carrier separation, use compact spatial profiles for intercept decay, and transported phase-space counting. Child frequencies are bounded, so derivatives cost no positive \\(b_1\\)-power. Thus\n\\[\n\\sum_\\theta\\|\\partial^\\alpha U_\\theta'(\\cdot,t)\\|_2^2\n\\lesssim\\sum_\\theta\\mathcal E_\\theta\\leq1.\n\\]\nUnit-cell Sobolev summed over space and bounded child time gives the first budget.</p><p>For the weighted budget apply Proposition 12, including fixed derivative versions, to each full child array. Pushforward growth is \\(Cmh^{n+1}\\); its packet moment retains \\(D\\), since\n\\[\nh^{n+1}b_1^{n+1}=L^{n+1}.\n\\]\nThe choice \\(b_1\\asymp(BD)^{C_{10}}\\) makes \\(D\\) a fixed positive power of \\(b_1\\), except bounded cases paid by \\(C_B\\), and \\(D\\leq b_1\\).</p><p>Convolve the pushed measure with the polynomial kernel defining \\(d_{\\theta,T}\\), restricting to a fixed enlarged native time range. Growth and moments persist because \\(P_*&gt;M+n+2\\). The convolved measure has cell mass at least a constant times \\(mh^{n+1}d_{\\theta,T}\\). Proposition 12 and the size normalization give the second budget. Disjoint cluster energies and bounded time overlap make it global.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.8", "orig": "A tuple bound", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use Lemma 13.7's flattened arrays.", "statement": "<p>Use Lemma 13.7's flattened arrays. Sort densities into dyadic bands \\(d_\\ell=C_d2^{-\\ell}\\). For significant coarse bins, choose band witnesses with thresholds\n\\[\nA_\\ell=c'R^{-n}\\lambda(1+\\ell)^{-2}.\n\\]\nA retained ordered \\(n\\)-tuple consists of tangentially transverse coarse bins, determinant at least \\(cR^{-(n-1)}\\), one band index \\(\\ell_i\\) each, in one cluster and one enlarged physical \\(h^2\\)-time bin.</p><p>Let \\(M_0\\) be the assigned tested mass and\n\\[\nE_i=\\sum_{\\text{\\(i\\)-th part}}s_{\\theta,T}^2.\n\\]\nAssume the tuple mass exceeds \\(mh^{2n-c_4}\\), for fixed small dimensional \\(c_4&gt;0\\). Retain half its mass where the full square envelopes at all separate witnesses are bounded by\n\\[\nB_i=C(mh^{n+1}d_{\\ell_i}E_i/M_0)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nParameter choices ensure\n\\[\nB_i\\lesssim h^{(1-2n+c_4)/2},\\qquad\nA_{\\ell_i}/B_i\\geq h^{c_3}\n\\]\nfor fixed \\(c_3&gt;0\\).</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\lambda^2M_0\n\\leq\nCR^C(1+\\textstyle\\sum_i\\ell_i)^C\nm\\chi^{1-\\beta}h^{n+2n/(n+1)}\n\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^nd_{\\ell_i}E_i\\right)^{\\beta/n}\n\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^nE_i\\right)^{1-\\beta}.\n\\]\nThe \\(R\\)-exponent is independent of the plate-cluster and time-bin counts and of \\(C_{10},C_{11}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(k=n-1\\). Partition the sheared normal spatial coordinate into width-\\(h\\) slabs. Each projected tangential unit cube has mass at most \\(Cm\\chi h\\), by unit covering in the normal coordinate.</p><p>Only nearby normal and time labels contribute in a fixed slab/time bin. After recentering time, residual normal frequency \\(O(h^{-1})\\) drifts only \\(O(h)\\). Projected tangential packets have bounded multiplicity, and their envelopes are bounded by full envelopes.</p><p>Fourier-expand cutoff profiles in normal coordinate divided by \\(h\\), incorporating bounded residual normal carrier and time phase. Mode amplitudes and envelopes gain\n\\[\n\\rho_m\\lesssim(1+|m|)^{-A_*}.\n\\]\nSplit thresholds by \\(\\gamma_m=c(1+|m|)^{-2}\\). The frame cutoff improves under \\(\\gamma_m/\\rho_m\\).</p><p>Translate each family's tangential packets by its integer witness offset \\(O(R^4)\\), so all witnesses occur in the tested projected cube. All choices cost only \\(R^C\\). Apply Proposition 7 normalized by \\(B_i\\), then multiply by \\(Cm\\chi h\\). Its mode factors include\n\\[\n\\prod_i(\\rho_{m_i}/\\gamma_{m_i})^{4/(kn)}\n\\]\nand, in the \\(j\\)-th energy term, \\((\\rho_{m_j}/\\gamma_{m_j})^2\\). Every independent mode sum converges for fixed large \\(A_*\\).</p><p>Sum normal slabs before solving the mass inequality, keeping the same global \\(B_i\\) and placing only nearby packet energies in the linear sum. Bounded slab overlap gives\n\\[\nM_0\\leq\nCR^Cm\\chi h\n\\prod_i(B_i/A_{\\ell_i})^{4/(kn)}\n\\sum_i\\frac{h^{k+2}E_i}{A_{\\ell_i}^2}.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(B_i,A_{\\ell_i}\\). Up to the stated \\(R\\) and band-index factors,\n\\[\nM_0^{1+2/k}\n\\leq\nCm^{1+2/k}\\chi\nh^{n+2+2(n+1)/k}\\lambda^{-2-4/k}\n\\left(\\prod_id_{\\ell_i}E_i\\right)^{2/(kn)}\n\\sum_iE_i.\n\\]\nRaise to \\(k/(k+2)=1-\\beta\\). The \\(\\lambda\\)-power becomes exactly \\(-2\\), giving the conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Lossless short-time weak estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Put \\( s=\\frac{n}{2(n+1)}. \\) Let \\(p>2\\) be supplied by Proposition 13.", "statement": "<p>Put\n\\[\ns=\\frac{n}{2(n+1)}.\n\\]\nLet \\(p&gt;2\\) be supplied by Proposition 13. If \\(N\\geq1\\) and \\(f_N\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^n)\\) has Fourier support in a fixed annulus dilated by \\(N\\), then for every unit ball \\(\\Omega\\) and interval \\(I\\) of length \\(N^{-1}\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\sup_{t\\in I}|S(t)f_N|\\right\\|_{L^{p,\\infty}(\\Omega)}\n\\leq CN^s\\|f_N\\|_2,\n\\]\nwhere, for bounded Fourier support,\n\\[\nS(t)f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-n}\\int\ne^{ix\\cdot\\xi-it|\\xi|^2}\\widehat f(\\xi)\\,d\\xi.\n\\]\nThe constant is independent of the centers of \\(\\Omega,I\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use unitarity and spatial translation to place the time interval at zero and the ball at a fixed center. Set\n\\[\nL=\\sqrt N,\\qquad X=Nx,\\qquad T=N^2t,\\qquad\ng(\\eta)=N^{n/2}\\widehat f_N(N\\eta).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nS(t)f_N(x)=N^{n/2}\\mathcal Eg(Nx,N^2t),\n\\qquad \\|g\\|_2\\asymp\\|f_N\\|_2.\n\\]</p><p>A measurable graph \\(T=T(X)\\) over the dilated spatial ball carries the pushforward of \\(dX\\). Its mass in every spacetime radius-\\(r\\) ball is at most \\(Cr^n\\), regardless of graph regularity. It has bounded native time, unit density \\(\\chi=1\\), and moment \\(D=1\\) by Proposition 8.</p><p>Apply Proposition 13 through Lemma 14.1:\n\\[\n\\mu\\{|\\mathcal Eg|&gt;L^{-e}g_0\\}\n\\leq CL^{2n}g_0^{-p}\\|g\\|_2^p.\n\\]\nReturning to original space multiplies mass by \\(N^{-n}=L^{-2n}\\), and\n\\[\nN^{n/2}L^{-e}=N^{(n-e)/2}=N^s.\n\\]\nFor a finite time list choose the first maximizer, giving a measurable graph. Exhaust a countable dense list and use bounded-frequency continuity. Bounded \\(N\\) follows directly from the Fourier \\(L^1\\) bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Compact layers for bounded-frequency data", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(g\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^n)\\) have support in a fixed bounded frequency box, and \\(L\\geq1\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(g\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^n)\\) have support in a fixed bounded frequency box, and \\(L\\geq1\\). On \\(|T|\\leq CL^2\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal Eg(X,T)=\\sum_{q\\in\\mathbb Z^n}U_q(X,T),\n\\]\nwhere \\(U_q\\) are compact arrays of scale \\(L\\). For every prescribed finite derivative order \\(J\\) and \\(A&gt;0\\), their profiles are uniformly bounded in \\(C^J\\), their phase-space multiplicities are uniform for each \\(q\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_q\\leq C_{A,J}\\langle q\\rangle^{-2A}\\|g\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nFinite coefficient truncations converge uniformly on the stated time range, and the \\(q\\)-sum also converges uniformly. Homogeneous weak \\(L^p\\) array estimates therefore pass to \\(\\mathcal Eg\\) without an \\(L\\)-power or layer count.</p>", "proof": "<p>Partition frequency into \\(L^{-1}\\)-caps \\(\\chi_\\theta\\), with centers \\(\\omega_\\theta\\). On a fixed larger native box let\n\\[\nG_\\theta(\\zeta)=L^{-n/2}(\\chi_\\theta g)(\\omega_\\theta+\\zeta/L).\n\\]\nFourier-series coefficients satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\theta,m}|c_{\\theta,m}|^2\\leq C\\|g\\|_2^2\n\\]\nby Parseval and cap overlap. Multiply the series by a smooth bump \\(\\psi_\\theta\\) equal to one on the rescaled cap support. The extension terms are\n\\[\nc_{\\theta,m}L^{-n/2}\ne^{i(X\\cdot\\omega_\\theta-T|\\omega_\\theta|^2)}\nH_\\theta\\left(\n\\frac{X-\\gamma Lm-2T\\omega_\\theta}{L},\\frac T{L^2}\\right),\n\\]\nwhere \\(H_\\theta\\) is the extension of the fixed smooth bump and has arbitrary spatial decay with fixed derivatives for bounded native time. No derivative of \\(g\\) is taken.</p><p>Take a compact spatial partition \\(\\sum_q\\rho(u-q)=1\\) and a time cutoff equal to one on the needed range. In layer \\(q\\), shift every intercept by \\(Lq\\); the profile is\n\\[\n\\rho(u)\\chi(\\tau)H_\\theta(u+q,\\tau).\n\\]\nIts \\(C^J\\) norm is at most \\(C_{A,J}\\langle q\\rangle^{-A}\\). Extract this factor into coefficients. The entire intercept lattice is translated uniformly, preserving multiplicity.</p><p>At a point, only boundedly many intercepts per cap contribute. Cauchy bounds a coefficient tail by a constant times\n\\[\n\\langle q\\rangle^{-A}\n\\left(\\sum_{\\mathrm{tail}}|c_{\\theta,m}|^2\\right)^{1/2},\n\\]\nuniformly. For \\(A&gt;n\\), the layer sum is uniform. Independently, frequency-series partial sums converge in \\(L^2\\) on bounded support, hence their extensions converge uniformly by Cauchy–Schwarz; the limits coincide.</p><p>Choose \\(b_q&gt;0\\), \\(\\sum_qb_q=1\\), \\(b_q\\asymp\\langle q\\rangle^{-n-1}\\). Then\n\\[\n|\\sum_qU_q|&gt;\\lambda\\ \\Longrightarrow\\\n|U_q|&gt;b_q\\lambda\\text{ for some }q.\n\\]\nThe weak-estimate cost is \\(\\sum_qb_q^{-p}\\mathcal E_q^{p/2}\\), bounded for sufficiently large fixed \\(A\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Dual square sum", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix measurable \\( t:\\Omega\\to[0,1),\\qquad w:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C,\\quad |w|\\leq1, \\) where \\(\\Omega\\) is a unit ball.", "statement": "<p>Fix measurable\n\\[\nt:\\Omega\\to[0,1),\\qquad w:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C,\\quad |w|\\leq1,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\Omega\\) is a unit ball. Let \\(\\psi\\) be a smooth compactly supported annular cutoff. For \\(N=2^j\\), \\(j\\geq1\\), and half-open dyadic \\(I\\subset[0,1)\\), set\n\\[\nG_N(I)(\\xi)=\\psi(\\xi/N)\n\\int_{\\{x\\in\\Omega:t(x)\\in I\\}}\ne^{ix\\cdot\\xi-it(x)|\\xi|^2}w(x)\\,dx,\n\\]\n\\[\nd_j(I)=N^{-2s}\\|G_N(I)\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\geq1}d_j([0,1))\\leq C.\n\\]\nThe same graph \\(t\\) and phase \\(w\\) are used at every frequency.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\nm_I=|\\{x\\in\\Omega:t(x)\\in I\\}|\n\\]\nand \\(\\mathcal D_a\\) be absolute-depth-\\(a\\) dyadic intervals. The squared-norm kernel phase, after \\(\\xi=N\\eta\\), is\n\\[\nN(x-y)\\cdot\\eta-N^2(t(x)-t(y))|\\eta|^2.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently separated intervals in \\(\\mathcal D_j\\), the time term dominates its gradient. Integration by parts gives an arbitrarily negative \\(N\\)-power, integrated once over the entire far-pair set, without a pair-count factor. Neighbor multiplicity is bounded, so\n\\[\nd_j([0,1))\\leq C\\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal D_j}d_j(I)+C2^{-2j}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(J\\subset I\\in\\mathcal D_j\\) have relative depth \\(k\\leq j\\), and \\(r=2^{-k}\\). Its time width is \\(r/N\\). Dualizing Proposition 14 and integrating a weak \\(L^p\\) function over mass \\(m_J\\) gives\n\\[\nd_j(J)\\leq Cm_J^{2-2/p}.\n\\]\nA spatially localized estimate is\n\\[\nd_j(J)\\leq Cr^{2s}m_J.\n\\]\nTo prove it, partition into side-\\(r\\) spatial cubes. Outside fixed neighbor offsets, the spatial phase dominates, and\n\\[\n|K_N(x-y,t(x)-t(y))|\n\\leq CN^n(1+N|x-y|)^{-n-2}.\n\\]\nIts spatial integral is bounded. Normalization gives \\(CN^{-2s}m_J\\leq Cr^{2s}m_J\\), since \\(Nr\\geq1\\).</p><p>For neighboring pairs reduce by Cauchy to single-cube energies. Rescale\n\\[\nx=x_Q+rX,\\qquad t=t_J+r^2T,\\qquad\\eta=r\\xi.\n\\]\nFrequency becomes \\(Nr\\), time width \\(1/(Nr)\\), and squared Fourier norm gains \\(r^n\\). If \\(m_{J,Q}\\) is mass in that spatial cube, Proposition 14 bounds normalized energy by\n\\[\nCr^{n+2s}(m_{J,Q}/r^n)^{2-2/p}\n\\leq Cr^{2s}m_{J,Q}.\n\\]\nSum. The endpoint \\(Nr=1\\) is the elementary bounded-frequency case.</p><p>Take the geometric mean:\n\\[\nd_j(J)\\leq C2^{-sk}m_J^\\alpha,\\qquad\n\\alpha=\\frac32-\\frac1p&gt;1.\n\\]</p><p>From each \\(I\\in\\mathcal D_j\\), follow the heavier child for \\(j\\) steps, breaking ties consistently. Write \\(H(I,k)\\) for the heavy descendant after \\(k\\) steps and \\(P(I,k)\\) for the lighter child at step \\(k\\). These lighter intervals and \\(H(I,j)\\) partition \\(I\\), including atoms because intervals are half-open.</p><p>Additivity of \\(G_N\\), triangle inequality, and weighted Cauchy with weights \\(2^{-sk/2}\\) give\n\\[\nd_j(I)\\leq C\\sum_{k=1}^j2^{-sk/2}m_{P(I,k)}^\\alpha\n+C2^{-sj/2}m_I^\\alpha.\n\\]</p><p>For child masses \\(a\\geq b\\geq0\\),\n\\[\n(a+b)^\\alpha-a^\\alpha-b^\\alpha\n\\geq(2^\\alpha-2)b^\\alpha.\n\\]\nFor \\(b&gt;0\\), divide by \\(b^\\alpha\\) and differentiate in \\(a/b\\geq1\\). Telescope these nonnegative deficits over finite dyadic trees, then pass to increasing trees. The sum of lighter-child \\(\\alpha\\)-powers over distinct parents is bounded by a constant.</p><p>For fixed relative \\(k\\), the parents of \\(P(I,k)\\), as \\(j\\geq k\\), \\(I\\in\\mathcal D_j\\), vary, are distinct: their absolute depth is \\(j+k-1\\), and at fixed \\(j\\) their descendants are disjoint. Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\geq k}\\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal D_j}m_{P(I,k)}^\\alpha\\leq C.\n\\]\nAlso \\(\\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal D_j}m_I^\\alpha\\leq m_{[0,1)}^\\alpha\\). Sum first at fixed \\(k\\), then over \\(k\\); \\(2^{-sk/2}\\), terminal terms, and far-pair errors are summable. This is the common-time-tree mechanism of [24, Section 9].</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Local \\(L^1\\) endpoint maximal estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(n\\geq3\\), \\(s=n/(2(n+1))\\), every \\(f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^n)\\) with compactly supported Fourier transform, and every unit ball \\(\\Omega\\) …", "statement": "<p>For \\(n\\geq3\\), \\(s=n/(2(n+1))\\), every \\(f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^n)\\) with compactly supported Fourier transform, and every unit ball \\(\\Omega\\),\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega\\sup_{0\\leq t&lt;1}|S(t)f(x)|\\,dx\n\\leq C\\|f\\|_{H^s(\\mathbb R^n)}.\n\\]\nThe constant is uniform under spatial translation of \\(\\Omega\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take a smooth inhomogeneous Littlewood–Paley decomposition\n\\[\nf=f_{\\mathrm{lo}}+\\sum_{j\\geq1}f_{2^j}\n\\]\nwith bounded overlap. Low frequencies satisfy\n\\[\n\\sup_{x,t}|S(t)f_{\\mathrm{lo}}(x)|\\leq C\\|f_{\\mathrm{lo}}\\|_2.\n\\]</p><p>For one measurable graph \\(t(x)\\) and phase \\(w(x)\\), choose annular cutoffs equal to one on the corresponding frequency supports. Frequency Cauchy–Schwarz and Proposition 15 give\n\\[\n\\left|\\int_\\Omega w(x)\\sum_jS(t(x))f_{2^j}(x)\\,dx\\right|\n\\leq\nC\\left(\\sum_j2^{2js}\\|f_{2^j}\\|_2^2\\right)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\sum_j2^{-2js}\\|G_{2^j}([0,1))\\|_2^2\\right)^{1/2}\n\\leq C\\|f\\|_{H^s}.\n\\]\nThus the summation uses the Sobolev square norm, not a logarithmic or \\(\\ell^1\\) frequency norm.</p><p>For a finite time list choose the first maximizer of the full field and its conjugate phase, zero on zeros. They are measurable and common to every frequency. The pairing becomes the integral of the finite maximum. Increase lists to a countable dense subset of \\([0,1)\\) containing zero. Bounded-frequency continuity and monotone convergence prove the assertion. Kernel estimates use only spatial differences, so constants are translation-uniform.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Gaussian approximation with uniform local integrals", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(M\\geq0\\) be measurable and \\( A=\\sup_z\\int_{B(z,1)}M(x)\\,dx<\\infty. \\) For \\( K_a(y)=(4\\pi a)^{-n/2}e^{-|y|^2/(4a)},\\qquad a>0, \\) the convolution \\(K_a*M\\) is finite at every point.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(M\\geq0\\) be measurable and\n\\[\nA=\\sup_z\\int_{B(z,1)}M(x)\\,dx&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\nK_a(y)=(4\\pi a)^{-n/2}e^{-|y|^2/(4a)},\\qquad a&gt;0,\n\\]\nthe convolution \\(K_a*M\\) is finite at every point. At every Lebesgue point \\(x\\) of \\(M\\),\n\\[\nK_a*M(x)\\longrightarrow M(x)\\qquad(a\\downarrow0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(\\rho&gt;0\\), cover each annulus\n\\[\n2^\\ell\\rho\\leq|y|&lt;2^{\\ell+1}\\rho\n\\]\nby \\(C(1+2^\\ell\\rho)^n\\) unit balls. Uniformly in the center,\n\\[\n\\int_{|y|\\geq\\rho}K_a(y)M(x-y)\\,dy\n\\leq\nCAa^{-n/2}\\sum_{\\ell\\geq0}\n(1+2^\\ell\\rho)^n e^{-4^\\ell\\rho^2/(4a)}.\n\\]\nThe series is finite for every \\(a&gt;0\\), and for sufficiently small \\(a\\) is at most\n\\[\nC_\\rho Aa^{-n/2}e^{-\\rho^2/(8a)}.\n\\]\nLocal integrability handles the bounded ball, proving finiteness.</p><p>At a Lebesgue point,\n\\[\nH_x(r)=\\int_{|y|&lt;r}|M(x-y)-M(x)|\\,dy=o(r^n).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\rho\\) so \\(H_x(r)\\leq\\varepsilon r^n\\) for \\(r\\leq\\rho\\). Radial integration by parts gives\n\\[\n\\int_{|y|&lt;\\rho}K_a(y)|M(x-y)-M(x)|\\,dy\n\\leq\n\\varepsilon\\left(K_a(\\rho)\\rho^n+\n\\int_0^\\rho(-K_a'(r))r^n\\,dr\\right)\n\\leq C\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nThe complementary integral tends to zero by the annular estimate and the Gaussian tail of the constant \\(M(x)\\). Let \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 19, "cite"], [1, 22, "cite"], [2, 20, "cite"], [3, 6, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [3, 10, "cite"], [3, 11, "cite"], [3, 14, "cite"], [3, 18, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 6, "cite"], [4, 9, "cite"], [4, 10, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 9, "cite"], [6, 10, "cite"], [6, 22, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [7, 14, "cite"], [7, 16, "cite"], [7, 18, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [8, 19, "cite"], [9, 11, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [9, 14, "cite"], [9, 16, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [11, 55, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [14, 16, "cite"], [14, 18, "cite"], [15, 11, "cite"], [15, 20, "cite"], [16, 11, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [16, 20, "cite"], [16, 23, "cite"], [17, 11, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [17, 20, "cite"], [17, 21, "cite"], [18, 11, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [18, 21, "cite"], [19, 11, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [19, 22, "cite"], [20, 11, "cite"], [20, 23, "cite"], [21, 11, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 11, "cite"], [24, 26, "cite"], [24, 29, "cite"], [24, 34, "cite"], [24, 47, "cite"], [24, 56, "cite"], [25, 24, "cite"], [25, 44, "cite"], [26, 47, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [27, 44, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [29, 34, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [30, 44, "cite"], [30, 48, "cite"], [31, 29, "cite"], [31, 44, "cite"], [32, 46, "cite"], [33, 46, "cite"], [34, 32, "cite"], [34, 38, "cite"], [34, 43, "cite"], [35, 32, "cite"], [36, 32, "cite"], [36, 40, "cite"], [36, 42, "cite"], [37, 32, "cite"], [37, 38, "cite"], [38, 32, "cite"], [38, 40, "cite"], [38, 42, "cite"], [39, 32, "cite"], [39, 40, "cite"], [40, 32, "cite"], [40, 41, "cite"], [40, 42, "cite"], [41, 32, "cite"], [41, 42, "cite"], [42, 32, "cite"], [42, 43, "cite"], [43, 32, "cite"], [44, 47, "cite"], [44, 54, "cite"], [45, 44, "cite"], [45, 46, "cite"], [46, 44, "cite"], [47, 56, "cite"], [48, 47, "cite"], [48, 49, "cite"], [49, 47, "cite"], [49, 53, "cite"], [50, 47, "cite"], [50, 51, "cite"], [50, 53, "cite"], [51, 47, "cite"], [51, 52, "cite"], [51, 53, "cite"], [52, 47, "cite"], [52, 53, "cite"], [53, 47, "cite"], [54, 47, "cite"], [54, 55, "cite"], [55, 47, "cite"], [56, 58, "cite"], [57, 56, "cite"], [58, 59, "cite"], [59, 0, "cite"], [60, 0, "cite"]]});
